window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__ = window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__ || {};
window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__["vol4/08_perception"] = {"key": "vol4/08_perception", "sections": [{"id": "sec-1", "level": 2, "title": "Purpose"}, {"id": "sec-2", "level": 2, "title": "Choosing Evidence for a Spatial Decision"}, {"id": "sec-3", "level": 3, "title": "Rolling shutter distortion and motion compensation"}, {"id": "sec-4", "level": 3, "title": "Choosing metric depth for the aisle"}, {"id": "sec-5", "level": 3, "title": "Event cameras and asynchronous contrast"}, {"id": "sec-6", "level": 3, "title": "LiDAR and radar ranging"}, {"id": "sec-7", "level": 3, "title": "Tactile contact sensing"}, {"id": "sec-8", "level": 2, "title": "Dating and Placing the Measurement"}, {"id": "sec-9", "level": 3, "title": "Calibrating the coordinate chain"}, {"id": "sec-10", "level": 2, "title": "Bounding Observation Age"}, {"id": "sec-11", "level": 3, "title": "The latency waterfall"}, {"id": "sec-12", "level": 3, "title": "Dating the machine's LiDAR"}, {"id": "sec-13", "level": 3, "title": "Observation age and feedback stability"}, {"id": "sec-14", "level": 3, "title": "MIPI CSI-2 DMA ingress and zero-copy buffers"}, {"id": "sec-15", "level": 3, "title": "Memory bus contention and observation age"}, {"id": "sec-16", "level": 3, "title": "Clearance insets and transform chain error"}, {"id": "sec-17", "level": 3, "title": "Learned depth as a measurement"}, {"id": "sec-18", "level": 2, "title": "Perception Failure Modes and Sensor Degradation"}], "html": "<h1 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h1\">Sensor Perception</h1>\n<h2 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h2\">Purpose</h2>\n<p><em>Why can an accurate detection still be too late to guide a robot?</em></p>\n<p>A camera can recognize an obstacle correctly and still report where it stood before the robot moved. Exposure, readout, transport, and inference all take time, the machine keeps moving through every millisecond of it, and a shifted mount or a misread clock then places the obstacle somewhere it never stood. None of these errors lowers a detection score. The worst of them, a frame stamped with the wrong time, narrows the estimate's uncertainty around the wrong position, so the machine grows more confident as it grows more wrong. A machine that treats a recognized object as a current position spends its stopping clearance on errors nobody measured. Perception serves a moving machine only when each observation states when it was captured, where it was measured from, and how far it can be trusted, because at the causal boundary a stale or misplaced observation becomes motion that cannot be taken back, and the permission path can refuse only evidence whose age and limits it can check.</p>\n<p>{{&lt; pagebreak &gt;}}</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Learning Objectives</div>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Select sensing modalities by the physical quantity, coverage, and failure limits required for a spatial decision</li>\n<li>Diagnose exposure, rolling-shutter, and calibration errors before measurements enter a geometric estimate</li>\n<li>Calculate capture-to-dispatch age and account for continued aging through downstream consumption without double charging delay</li>\n<li>Evaluate whether stopping margins fit the available clearance once the clearance inset and clock bound rest on measured error, not reported covariance</li>\n<li>Distinguish qualified metric evidence from learned depth estimates and semantic confidence</li>\n<li>Diagnose silent sensor failures, including a misdated frame, that pass transport checks while misrepresenting the scene</li>\n<li>Design an observation contract whose timing, calibration, error qualification, and health checks support downstream use</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"sec-2\" class=\"cs-h2\">Choosing Evidence for a Spatial Decision</h2>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ OPENING SCENE: OBSERVATION AGE AT THE RACK END\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: The opening paragraph of @sec-perception-photons-to-claims;\n# │          also the temporal-batching paragraph and the clock-sync pitfall.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Show how far the warehouse mobile manipulator travels while its\n# │          navigation camera&#x27;s observation of a person ages.\n# │ Show:    t_age 51.6 ms; 77.4 mm of travel at the 1.5 m/s drive limit.\n# │ How:     Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraAge: the site&#x27;s stage allowances\n# │          summed by ObservationAgeModel and priced at the drive limit.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt_latency, fmt_length, fmt_velocity, check\n\nRM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass RackEndOpeningAge:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Travel during the navigation camera&#x27;s observation age at the drive limit.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    scenario = Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraAge  # category: derived (site stage allowances)\n    v = scenario.speed  # category: illustrative (drive limit)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    t_age = scenario.result.age\n    dx = scenario.result.age_travel.to(millimeter)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(t_age.to(millisecond).magnitude - 51.6) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected t_age: {t_age}&quot;)\n    check(abs(v.to(meter / second).magnitude - 1.5) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Unexpected drive limit: {v}&quot;)\n    check(abs(dx.magnitude - 77.4) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected travel during t_age: {dx}&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    t_age_str = fmt_latency(t_age.to(millisecond), unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    v_str = fmt_velocity(v.to(meter / second), unit=meter / second, precision=1)\n    dx_str = fmt_length(dx, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n]```\n\nA vision model on the warehouse mobile manipulator can detect a worker stepping past a rack upright with near-perfect benchmark accuracy and still hand the trajectory planner an obsolete position. The world keeps moving while the navigation camera integrates charge, serializes pixel rows across the bus, and runs inference through the vision pipeline. On this machine, `[RackEndOpeningAge.t_age_str]` elapses between the midpoint of optical exposure and the dispatch of the processed observation (derived in the latency waterfall of @sec-perception-latency-waterfall). Driving at its `[RackEndOpeningAge.v_str]` drive limit, the base covers `[RackEndOpeningAge.dx_str]` over that single interval. Benchmark accuracy reveals nothing about whether an observation is still fresh enough to support safe clearance. On a moving machine, every observation carries an expiration date.\n\nEvery motion a physical AI system executes rests on a claim about the world derived from earlier sensor readings. Perception converts raw energy incident on a detector (photons on a CMOS sensor array, laser pulses reflected to an avalanche photodiode, or elastomer deformation at a fingertip) into spatial claims: the volume a coworker occupies, the metric range to a rack upright, or the incipient slip of a grasped object. Machine perception has historically been scored by static recognition accuracy on fixed benchmarks, which rewards a correct label regardless of when the exposure occurred or how the camera was moving. For an embodied machine that abstraction collapses. A spatial claim can support motion only if it states three properties: the instant its energy was captured, the coordinate frame in which it was measured, and a bound on its spatial uncertainty. Set against the machine&#x27;s current velocity, that qualified uncertainty must fit inside the clearance that remains.\n\nThe evaluation record that closed Part II (@sec-evaluation-logs) hands the runtime a list of obligations rather than a verdict of trust. It limits the learned proposer to its target operational design domain (ODD) and leaves the rest of the machine to supply the runtime monitors that hold it there (@sec-evaluation-limits). Two of those monitors, the input-support monitor and the latency and freshness monitor, can act only on observations that carry their capture time, frame, and uncertainty, so their first obligation falls on perception. Perception delivers this runtime evidence as dated spatial claims across the proposal boundary. It executes within the non-deterministic Brain above that boundary and holds no actuator authority. The deterministic permission path below the boundary evaluates each proposed action against the capture timestamps, coordinate frames, and error qualifications of the perceptual claims behind it. Part III&#x27;s governing law, proposal is not permission (principle \\ref{pri-vol4-proposal-permission}), applies to perception in a specific way. A confident detection does not license motion. It is evidence behind a proposal, and the permission path can refuse that proposal only when the evidence carries an age, a frame, and an error bound it can check.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The proposal boundary and permission path are established in @sec-boundary-machine-model.\n:::\n\nChecking a claim against physical margin means following the evidence from capture to qualification. Each sensor type must first match the physical quantity an action requires. Calibration and detector physics then ground the measurement in space and time, the capture-to-dispatch age must be budgeted, and the spatial error, including the silent corruption no driver reports, must be bounded before any downstream check permits motion. The chapter closes by packaging those properties into an observation contract, so that a consumer can check a measurement&#x27;s age, frame, and error before reading a single payload byte. The contract is the first record of the runtime strand, which meets the limit record and the evaluation record at the permission decision. Perception ends once it yields a qualified measurement. Fusing such measurements across time into persistent spatial state is the work of state estimation and spatial memory in @sec-memory.\n\nWhether an architecture employs an end-to-end vision-language-action (VLA) policy [@brohan2023rt2; @kim2024openvla], an end-to-end visuomotor model without a language backbone [@zhao2023learning], or a modular estimator, it must reconcile the physical scene captured at a past instant with the kinematic state of the robot at execution. Architectural choices alter processing latency and representation formats, but no network topology can bypass photon integration, sensor readout, or bus transport delay. Explicit timestamps and spatial transforms keep this relationship inspectable; a latent representation holds it only implicitly, so checking it requires external instrumentation. Neither approach converts ambiguous sensor signals into ground truth. A specular reflection on a polished warehouse floor remains an optical hazard regardless of model capacity.\n\nChoosing sensors that resist those ambiguities starts with the physical task, because navigating past a blind rack corner requires establishing a bounded occupied volume in the robot&#x27;s base coordinate frame before available stopping distance runs out. RGB appearance helps classify an object as a person, but monocular bearing alone cannot supply metric range. The navigation camera therefore requires corroborated depth measurements before its detections can justify motion clearance, and on the running machine the spinning LiDAR in the sensor suite supplies them. At the end-effector, sensing requirements change completely. While external optical cameras guide the initial reaching approach, detecting contact mechanics, shear forces, and incipient slip requires tactile sensing directly at the fingertips. Sensor selection begins by identifying the physical quantity governing each action and then analyzing sensor coverage, update rates, and environmental failure modes.\n\n@Tbl-08-sensor-modalities compares the physical evidence each sensing mechanism provides alongside the primary failure modes and safety checks required before fusing claims across an embodied sensor suite. Adding a secondary sensor mitigates a shared failure mode only when its independent spatial coverage, timing guarantees, extrinsic calibration, and correlated failure risks are established.\n\n| Modality         | Direct physical information                                                          | Main limitation                                                                                         | Permission-relevant check                             |\n|:-----------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:------------------------------------------------------|\n| RGB camera       | Bearing, appearance, and image motion                                                | Monocular depth scale is ambiguous; glare, darkness, and occlusion can hide objects                     | Fresh capture time and validated metric corroboration |\n| Active stereo    | Disparity-derived range with infrared texture projection                             | Sunlight washes out projected pattern; range error grows quadratically with distance ($\\TargetRange^2$) | Range-dependent residual and bias validation          |\n| Structured light | High-density triangulated depth from projected coded patterns                        | Ambient solar infrared flooding; multi-robot pattern crosstalk; short range ($&lt;3\\,\\text{m}$)            | Lighting lux limits and pattern decoding integrity    |\n| Time-of-flight   | Metric depth via phase delay (iToF) or photon arrival timing (dToF)                  | iToF phase wrapping; multipath reflections in corners; flying pixels on moving edges                    | Ambiguity distance and corner multipath flags         |\n| Pulsed LiDAR     | Metric time-of-flight range at sampled laser beam directions                         | Near-field aerosol backscatter in fog/rain; retroreflector blooming; specular puddle dropouts           | Qualified range, return quality, and scan timing      |\n| FMCW radar       | Metric range and instantaneous radial Doppler velocity of targets                    | Multipath ghost returns off ground; lower angular resolution; antenna sidelobe clutter                  | Track association and coverage of crossing targets    |\n| Event camera     | Asynchronous per-pixel log-intensity contrast events ($\\ge \\EventContrastThreshold$) | Unchanging scenes supply little event evidence; noise and flicker can still generate events             | Event-rate health and independent metric depth source |\n| Optical tactile  | Contact micro-depth, surface normals, and shear force vectors                        | Zero sensing before physical contact; strictly localized to gripper elastomeric pad                     | Contact threshold, slip flags, and marker tracking    |\n\n: **Spatial sensor modality comparison**: Sensor mechanisms provide complementary but fallible claims across physical operating regimes. Numeric range, accuracy, refresh, and link budgets require a named device and mode. For example, [Prophesee EVT2](https://docs.prophesee.ai/stable/data/encoding_formats/evt2.html) encodes an event in 32 bits, so an event camera&#x27;s link load scales with its event rate before transport overhead. {#tbl-08-sensor-modalities tbl-colwidths=&quot;[15,28,31,26]&quot;}\n\nOn an operational robot, this division of responsibility is explicit and follows established industrial safety standards. Neither the vision model nor the spinning LiDAR owns the certified safety claim regarding an obstacle at the rack end. ISO 3691-4 mandates that an autonomous industrial truck detect persons in its path at Performance Level d (PL d) [@iso3691part4; @tuv2021iso3691], the second highest of the five reliability grades (a through e) defined in ISO 13849-1 [@iso13849part1]. Mobile bases satisfy this mandate using an industrial safety laser scanner, a dual-channel planar time-of-flight sensor certified as an active opto-electronic protective device under IEC 61496-3 [@iec61496part3; @keyence2026szv]. By design and certification standards, the safety scanner performs no semantic classification; it reports solely whether an opaque object has entered its protective optical field, directly triggering the certified emergency stop circuit.\n\nThe mobile base&#x27;s spinning LiDAR (an Ouster OS1-64) carries no functional safety rating; its datasheet certifies only electrical, laser, and electromagnetic compatibility [@ouster2025os1]. Instead, it provides the dense 3D metric range field that the permission path evaluates against spatial clearance limits. The permission path runs on its own safety microcontroller (MCU), as @sec-nervous placed it, but the clearance evaluation it performs there is an unrated admission check, a software layer above the certified safety scanner. Two components therefore guard the rack end, the permission path&#x27;s unrated clearance check and the certified scanner beneath it. The permission path&#x27;s role is to keep learned proposals smooth and proactive, adjusting trajectories well before the robot approaches the scanner&#x27;s protective boundary and causes an abrupt hardware stop.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The certified drive, scanner, and arm safety functions beneath the permission path, and the one rated watchdog on it, are developed in @sec-enforcement-certified-functions.\n:::\n\nMeanwhile, the navigation camera enriches the learned proposer with semantic context: distinguishing a coworker from a stationary tote and predicting velocity headings. Because the proposer constructs its trajectory predictions relative to the camera frame, that image frame&#x27;s capture timestamp defines the evidence epoch carried by the resulting motion proposal. @Sec-perception-lidar-age details how the high-rate LiDAR stream is synchronized and cross-dated against this camera epoch. When operational conditions shift, other sensors fill the gaps: active illumination resolves textureless surfaces, event cameras preserve high-speed edge transitions across wide dynamic ranges, and tactile arrays capture localized contact dynamics that remote cameras cannot see. Before any of these sensors can anchor downstream motion, however, the system must account for how physical capture and mounting geometry distort the raw signal.\n\n## Capture Physics and Calibration {#sec-perception-transduction-calibration}\n\nWhen photons from a warehouse rack upright strike the navigation camera&#x27;s silicon detector, they do not become an instantaneous feature tensor in host memory. Before a neural backbone or state observer ever reads the frame, the photodiode array must accumulate charge over a finite exposure window, serialize rows off the sensor die, and move bytes across the interconnect, while the robot keeps moving through every millisecond of that process. In CMOS active-pixel sensors, incident photons generate electron-hole pairs through the photoelectric effect according to a wavelength-dependent quantum efficiency $\\PhotodiodeQuantumEfficiency(\\OpticalWavelength)$. Silicon photodiodes accumulate this photocharge across a finite exposure interval $\\ExposureDuration$, collecting total charge:\n$$\\CollectedPhotocharge = \\int_0^{\\ExposureDuration} \\frac{\\ElementaryCharge \\PhotodiodeQuantumEfficiency(\\OpticalWavelength)}{\\PlanckConstant \\OpticalFrequency} \\IncidentOpticalPower(\\TimeInstant)\\,d\\TimeInstant$$\nwhere $\\ElementaryCharge$ is the elementary charge, $\\PlanckConstant$ is Planck&#x27;s constant, $\\OpticalFrequency$ is optical frequency, and $\\IncidentOpticalPower(\\TimeInstant)$ is incident optical power. Exposure integration, readout serialization, bus transport across PCIe or MIPI CSI, memory staging, preprocessing kernels, and neural backbone inference each consume a slice of the end-to-end latency budget.\n\nBecause the photodiode accumulates charge over the finite exposure duration $\\ExposureDuration$, the captured frame integrates scene radiance over time while both the robot and the surrounding environment are in motion. Assuming approximately constant linear velocity $\\Velocity$ and angular velocity $\\AngularVelocity$, with the optical center displaced from the vehicle&#x27;s center of rotation by lever arm $\\SensorLeverArm$, a conservative bound on the physical displacement of the camera center during exposure is:\n$$\\ExposureMotionDisplacement = \\|\\Velocity\\| \\, \\ExposureDuration + \\|\\AngularVelocity \\times \\SensorLeverArm\\| \\, \\ExposureDuration$$\nThis distance measures the physical displacement of the camera center, not the pixel footprint of the blur on the detector plane. Projecting this displacement into image pixels requires scene depth, focal length, viewing direction, and relative attitude changes. Pure rotational motion can smear the image severely even when the camera center remains completely stationary. The resulting **motion blur** acts as a low-pass spatial filter, attenuating image gradients and destroying high-frequency edge information before neural feature extraction or corner detection can run. Downstream estimators can use accurate timestamps and extrinsic calibrations to position the blurred observation at its capture epoch, but no coordinate transformation can synthesize geometric detail lost during optical integration.\n\nThe finite integration window also determines which physical instant the resulting image represents. Under an idealized linear sensor response, the pixel value reflects incoming scene radiance $\\SceneRadiance(\\TimeInstant)$ integrated across the exposure window:\n$$\\IntegratedPixelResponse = \\int_{0}^{\\ExposureDuration} \\SceneRadiance(\\TimeInstant)\\,d\\TimeInstant$$\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ ROLLING-SHUTTER SKEW ON THE NAVIGATION CAMERA</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: The exposure paragraph and the rolling-shutter example of</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          @sec-perception-transduction-calibration.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Size the exposure-midpoint lag, per-row blur, and scanline skew</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the navigation camera records while the base passes a vertical</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          rack upright.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    an 8 ms midpoint lag; 3,040 rows in 16.6 ms (5.46 us per row);</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          24.9 mm of along-track skew and 24 mm of blur at the 1.5 m/s</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          drive limit, about half of the along-travel allowance delta_loc;</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          9.1 mm of lateral skew from an illustrative 0.5 rad/s yaw at the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          1.10 m rack-end distance, about 6 percent of the side clearance.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraRollingShutter and its aisle-speed</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          variant (RollingShutterSkewModel over RM.nav_camera, the site's</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          yaw rate, d_clear, delta_loc, and side clearance); RM.imu sample</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          rate for compensation.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import <em> from mlsysim.core.units import </em> from mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_latency, fmt_length, fmt_velocity, fmt_frequency, fmt_int, fmt_percent, fmt_display_math, check</p>\n<p>RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class RollingShutterSkew: \"\"\"Scanline skew and blur on the running machine's navigation camera at the drive limit.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>camera = RM.nav_camera  # category: datasheet (registry: navigation camera) rows = camera.resolution_height  # category: datasheet t_readout = camera.readout_time.to(millisecond)  # category: datasheet t_exp = camera.exposure_time.to(millisecond)  # category: chosen (configured exposure; low-light worst case, not a sensor rating) v = Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraRollingShutter.speed.to(meter / second)  # category: illustrative (drive limit) omega_yaw = AISLE.yaw_rate_turn  # category: illustrative (yaw rate while turning into the aisle) z_upright = AISLE.d_clear  # category: illustrative (range to the rack-end upright) delta_loc = AISLE.delta_loc  # category: illustrative (along-travel localization allowance) side_clearance = AISLE.side_clearance  # category: illustrative f_imu = RM.imu.sample_rate  # category: datasheet v_aisle = Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraRollingShutterAisleSpeed.speed.to( meter / second )  # category: chosen (aisle speed, below the derived ceiling)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>_rs = Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraRollingShutter.result _rs_aisle = Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraRollingShutterAisleSpeed.result t_exp_half = _rs.midpoint_lag.to(millisecond) t_row = _rs.row_interval.to(microsecond) skew = _rs.skew.to(millimeter)  # along the direction of travel blur = _rs.exposure_travel.to(millimeter) skew_over_loc = _rs.skew_over_allowance lateral = _rs.lateral_skew.to(millimeter) lateral_share = _rs.lateral_share skew_aisle = _rs_aisle.skew.to(millimeter)  # sensitivity: the chosen aisle speed skew_aisle_over_loc = _rs_aisle.skew_over_allowance</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(rows == 3040, f\"Unexpected navigation-camera row count: {rows}\") check(abs(t_row.magnitude - 5.46) &lt; 0.005, f\"Unexpected row interval: {t_row}\") check(abs(skew.magnitude - 24.9) &lt; 0.05, f\"Unexpected rolling-shutter skew: {skew}\") check(abs(blur.magnitude - 24.0) &lt; 0.05, f\"Unexpected per-row blur: {blur}\") check(abs(t_exp_half.magnitude - 8.0) &lt; 0.05, f\"Unexpected exposure-midpoint lag: {t_exp_half}\") check(0.45 &lt; skew_over_loc &lt; 0.55, f\"Prose says the along-track skew is about half of delta_loc: {skew_over_loc}\") check(abs(lateral.magnitude - 9.1) &lt; 0.05, f\"Unexpected lateral skew from yaw: {lateral}\") check(0.055 &lt; lateral_share &lt; 0.065, f\"Prose says the lateral skew is about 6 percent of the side clearance: {lateral_share}\") check(abs(skew_aisle.magnitude - 21.6) &lt; 0.05, f\"Unexpected skew at the aisle speed: {skew_aisle}\") check(0.4 &lt; skew_aisle_over_loc &lt; 0.45, f\"Notebook says the aisle speed still spends over two fifths of delta_loc: {skew_aisle_over_loc}\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>rows_str = fmt_int(rows) last_row_str = fmt_int(rows - 1) t_readout_str = fmt_latency(t_readout, unit=millisecond, precision=1) t_exp_str = fmt_latency(t_exp, unit=millisecond, precision=0) t_exp_half_str = fmt_latency(t_exp_half, unit=millisecond, precision=0) t_row_str = fmt_latency(t_row, unit=microsecond, precision=2) skew_str = fmt_length(skew, unit=millimeter, precision=1) blur_str = fmt_length(blur, unit=millimeter, precision=0) v_str = fmt_velocity(v, unit=meter / second, precision=1) omega_yaw_str = fmt(omega_yaw.to(radian / second).magnitude, precision=1) z_upright_str = fmt_length(z_upright.to(meter), unit=meter, precision=2) delta_loc_str = fmt_length(delta_loc.to(millimeter), unit=millimeter, precision=0) lateral_str = fmt_length(lateral, unit=millimeter, precision=1) lateral_pct_str = fmt_percent(lateral_share, precision=1, style=\"prose\") side_clearance_str = fmt_length(side_clearance.to(meter), unit=meter, precision=2) f_imu_str = fmt_frequency(f_imu, unit=hertz, precision=0, commas=True) v_aisle_str = fmt_velocity(v_aisle, unit=meter / second, precision=1) skew_aisle_str = fmt_length(skew_aisle, unit=millimeter, precision=1) skew_aisle_pct_str = fmt_percent(skew_aisle_over_loc, precision=1, style=\"prose\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">Display arithmetic for nbk-perception-readout-shear (magnitudes, LaTeX units)</h1>\n<p>_tr = fmt(t_readout.magnitude, precision=1) _v = fmt(v.magnitude, precision=1) _z = fmt(z_upright.to(meter).magnitude, precision=2) _w = fmt(omega_yaw.to(radian / second).magnitude, precision=1) _rows = fmt_int(rows, commas=False) t_row_math = fmt_display_math( rf\"t_{{\\text{{row}}}} = \\frac{{t_{{\\text{{readout}}}}}}{{H}} = \\frac{{{_tr}\\ \\text{{ms}}}}{{{_rows}}} = {fmt(t_row.magnitude, precision=2)}\\ \\mu\\text{{s}}\" ) skew_math = fmt_display_math( rf\"\\Delta x = v\\,t_{{\\text{{readout}}}} = {_v}\\ \\text{{m/s}} \\times {<em>tr}\\ \\text{{ms}} = {fmt(skew.magnitude, precision=1)}\\ \\text{{mm}}, \\quad \\frac{{\\Delta x}}{{\\delta</em>{{\\text{{loc}}}}}} = \\frac{{{fmt(skew.magnitude, precision=1)}\\ \\text{{mm}}}}{{{fmt(delta_loc.to(millimeter).magnitude, precision=0)}\\ \\text{{mm}}}} = {fmt(skew_over_loc, precision=2)}\" ) lateral_math = fmt_display_math( rf\"\\Delta y = z\\,\\omega\\,t_{{\\text{{readout}}}} = {_z}\\ \\text{{m}} \\times {_w}\\ \\text{{rad/s}} \\times {_tr}\\ \\text{{ms}} = {fmt(lateral.magnitude, precision=1)}\\ \\text{{mm}}, \\quad \\frac{{{fmt(lateral.magnitude, precision=1)}\\ \\text{{mm}}}}{{{fmt(side_clearance.to(millimeter).magnitude, precision=0)}\\ \\text{{mm}}}} = {fmt(lateral_share, precision=3)}\" ) ]```</p>\n<p>The exposure midpoint is the reference epoch assigned to the integrated image, exactly $\\ExposureDuration/2$ after the shutter opens and $\\ExposureDuration/2$ before it closes. This convention dates the measurement without making its blurred content an instantaneous sample; changes in illumination or motion within the window still require qualification. For the mobile base running at its <code>[RollingShutterSkew.v_str]</code> drive limit with the navigation camera configured to a <code>[RollingShutterSkew.t_exp_str]</code> exposure, the frame represents the scene where the robot was <code>[RollingShutterSkew.t_exp_half_str]</code> earlier, while the robot traverses <code>[RollingShutterSkew.blur_str]</code> of translation during the exposure itself. Exposure time is a configured software parameter rather than an immutable hardware constant. The warehouse mobile manipulator configures this relatively long exposure to operate reliably in dim aisle lighting; higher ambient illumination permits shorter integration times, scaling down both motion blur and midpoint latency proportionally.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h3\">Rolling shutter distortion and motion compensation</h3>\n<p>Assigning a single exposure midpoint to an entire image assumes that every pixel row integrates light over that same window. In a <strong>global shutter</strong> sensor, every photodiode integrates charge during the exact same time window and transfers accumulated electrons to shielded in-pixel storage nodes simultaneously. In contrast, cost and silicon die area constraints often dictate the use of a <strong>rolling shutter</strong> sensor, where rows are reset, exposed, and read out sequentially from top to bottom ($\\SensorRowIndex = 0$ to $\\SensorRowIndex = \\ImageHeight - 1$). Each successive scanline begins its exposure after an incremental row readout period $\\RollingShutterRowReadout$, introducing a progressive time skew across the vertical axis: $$\\RowExposureStartTime(\\SensorRowIndex) = \\FrameExposureStartTime + \\SensorRowIndex \\cdot \\RollingShutterRowReadout$$ where $\\FrameExposureStartTime$ denotes the exposure start time of the initial row ($\\SensorRowIndex = 0$). When the vehicle translates at linear velocity $\\Velocity$ perpendicular to a vertical object, scanline $\\SensorRowIndex$ samples that object at an along-track spatial offset: $$\\RollingShutterDistortion(\\SensorRowIndex) = \\Velocity \\cdot \\SensorRowIndex \\cdot \\RollingShutterRowReadout$$ {#eq-percep-rolling-shutter-skew} @Sec-data-rolling-shutter-skew met this row timing as a skew that corrupts the pairing of a logged frame with an action. On a moving base the same skew becomes the spatial offset of @eq-percep-rolling-shutter-skew, and it is charged against clearance.</p>\n<p>Under angular motion, rolling shutter distortion becomes non-linear. If the camera rotates with angular velocity vector $\\AngularVelocity = [\\XAxisAngularRate, \\YAxisAngularRate, \\YawRate]^\\top$ (for example, rapid yaw or pitch during aggressive vehicle maneuvers), the camera attitude drifts continuously throughout the readout phase. The optical axis points in a slightly different direction for every single scanline. For a row at vertical coordinate $\\SensorRowIndex$, the incremental rotation relative to the start of the frame is approximately $\\RowAttitudeIncrement(\\SensorRowIndex) \\approx \\AngularVelocity \\cdot \\SensorRowIndex \\cdot \\RollingShutterRowReadout$. This line-dependent rotation bends straight structural lines into curves, stretches or compresses vertical scale during pitching motions, and shears vertical surfaces into inclined planes, as photographed on the rotating propeller blades in @fig-rolling-shutter-skew.</p>\n<p>Consider the warehouse mobile manipulator navigating past a vertical rack upright at its <code>[RollingShutterSkew.v_str]</code> drive limit. A vertical post that spans the full frame height does not appear vertical in the raw pixel array, because the sensor reads the bottom scanline ($\\SensorRowIndex =$ <code>[RollingShutterSkew.last_row_str]</code>) an entire readout cycle after the top scanline ($\\SensorRowIndex = 0$). This delay induces two distinct geometric errors that consume separate safety margins: along-track translation shears the upright along the direction of travel, while base yaw during a turn shifts the feature laterally. Notebook \\ref{nbk-perception-readout-shear} quantifies both effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: The base passes a rack upright at its drive limit while turning into the aisle. How far apart does the navigation camera record the upright's top and bottom rows, and what share of the corresponding clearance allowance does each error consume?</p>\n<p><strong>Variables</strong>:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Rows $\\ImageHeight =$ <code>[RollingShutterSkew.rows_str]</code> and readout $\\FrameReadoutTime =$ <code>[RollingShutterSkew.t_readout_str]</code> (Datasheet, full-resolution mode).</li>\n<li>Drive limit $\\BaseTravelSpeed =$ <code>[RollingShutterSkew.v_str]</code> (Illustrative).</li>\n<li>Yaw rate $\\YawRate =$ <code>[RollingShutterSkew.omega_yaw_str]</code> rad/s while turning into the aisle, at the rack-end range $\\TargetRange =$ <code>[RollingShutterSkew.z_upright_str]</code> (Illustrative).</li>\n<li>Localization allowance $\\LocalizationBound =$ <code>[RollingShutterSkew.delta_loc_str]</code>, the along-travel term of the stopping budget (@sec-body-stopping-distance-prebrake), and side clearance <code>[RollingShutterSkew.side_clearance_str]</code>, the gap the base keeps between its flank and the rack faces while it drives the aisle (both Illustrative; @sec-appendix-systems-machine-data-sheet).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>:</p>\n<p><em>Row interval.</em> <code>[RollingShutterSkew.t_row_math]</code></p>\n<p><em>Along-track skew.</em> At the last row, @eq-percep-rolling-shutter-skew gives <code>[RollingShutterSkew.skew_math]</code></p>\n<p><em>Lateral skew.</em> The bearing rotates by $\\YawRate\\,\\FrameReadoutTime$ during readout, which moves the upright sideways at range $\\TargetRange$ by <code>[RollingShutterSkew.lateral_math]</code></p>\n<p><em>Sensitivity.</em> At the aisle's chosen <code>[RollingShutterSkew.v_aisle_str]</code>, the along-track skew is still <code>[RollingShutterSkew.skew_aisle_str]</code>, <code>[RollingShutterSkew.skew_aisle_pct_str]</code> of $\\LocalizationBound$.</p>\n<p><strong>Result</strong>: The bottom rows see the upright from a camera position <code>[RollingShutterSkew.skew_str]</code> farther down the aisle than the top rows did, half of $\\LocalizationBound$. Yaw adds <code>[RollingShutterSkew.lateral_str]</code> sideways, about <code>[RollingShutterSkew.lateral_pct_str]</code> of the side clearance.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: Uncorrected, one readout would spend half of the localization allowance that the stopping budget holds for the whole state estimate, and slowing to aisle speed barely helps. The budget's $\\LocalizationBound$ is therefore valid only if the skew is removed before the estimator sees the frame. The lateral term is small at the rack end, but it grows with range and turn rate, so it belongs in the calibration and timing checks rather than in a budget row.</p>\n<p>The along-track error charges directly against the localization allowance $\\LocalizationBound$ established in @sec-body-stopping-distance-prebrake. Treating the image as an instantaneous rigid perspective projection mistakenly models the bottom of the upright as closer along the corridor than the top, directly consuming the along-travel stopping allowance. Both error components scale linearly with total readout time. A global-shutter sensor, such as the wrist camera on the arm, eliminates scanline skew at the expense of larger pixel pitch and higher silicon cost. Selecting a shutter architecture is therefore a primary systems decision whenever a sensor's measurements inform clearance boundaries. The running machine deliberately carries a rolling-shutter navigation camera on its base, so its perception pipeline must handle this constraint rather than avoid it.</p>\n<p>Resolving this spatial artifact requires <strong>rolling-shutter motion compensation</strong>: unwarping image coordinates geometrically using an interpolated camera pose for every scanline. The onboard IMU supplies high-rate (<code>[RollingShutterSkew.f_imu_str]</code>) angular velocity and specific-force samples rather than direct velocity measurements. A fused state estimator integrates these inertial readings with bias estimates, gravity tracking, and wheel odometry to compute continuous body poses. Transforming these poses through calibrated body-to-camera extrinsics yields an estimated optical pose for each scanline, allowing the perception pipeline to rectify the image before running stereo matching or landmark triangulation. Plotted against row index, the uncompensated skew is a straight line, because each row is read a fixed interval after the one above it (@fig-08-rolling-shutter-rows). The line ends halfway to the <code>[RollingShutterSkew.delta_loc_str]</code> allowance, which is why compensation, not a budget row, must absorb it.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Specific Force</strong>: An accelerometer measures non-gravitational acceleration, $\\LinearAcceleration-\\GravityVector$, so a unit resting on the floor reads one $\\Gravity$ upward and one in free fall reads zero. Dead reckoning must therefore rotate specific force into the navigation frame and restore the gravity vector, and an attitude error $\\AttitudeError$ leaks $\\Gravity\\sin\\AttitudeError$ into horizontal acceleration, which integrates twice into the per-row poses that de-skewing relies on.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ ROLLING-SHUTTER ROW SAMPLING (FIGURE)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @fig-08-rolling-shutter-rows in\n# │          @sec-perception-transduction-calibration.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Make the along-travel skew physical: row index against the camera\n# │          position at which that row was read, with per-row blur and the\n# │          localization allowance it spends.\n# │ Show:    0 -&gt; 24.9 mm over 3,040 rows (16.6 ms); 24 mm blur band; 50 mm\n# │          delta_loc line.\n# │ How:     Reads RollingShutterSkew only; the plotted endpoint is guarded\n# │          against its skew.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom mlsysim import viz\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import check\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import millimeter\n\nRS = RollingShutterSkew\nPETROL, PINE, SOFT = &quot;#007A87&quot;, &quot;#1A4D3E&quot;, &quot;#4A5568&quot;  # measured, structural, neutral (theme-colors.tex)\n\n# ┌── 1. LOAD ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nn_rows = RS.rows\nv_mm_per_ms = RS.v.to(millimeter / millisecond).magnitude\nt_row_ms = RS.t_row.to(millisecond).magnitude\nblur_mm = RS.blur.to(millimeter).magnitude\ndloc_mm = RS.delta_loc.to(millimeter).magnitude\n\n# ┌── 2. EXECUTE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nrows_axis = np.array([0.0, n_rows - 1.0])\nx_cam = v_mm_per_ms * t_row_ms * rows_axis\n\n# ┌── 3. GUARD ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\ncheck(abs(v_mm_per_ms * t_row_ms * n_rows - RS.skew.to(millimeter).magnitude) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;The plotted skew must equal v * t_readout.&quot;)\ncheck(RS.skew.to(millimeter).magnitude &lt; dloc_mm, &quot;The skew must sit inside the drawn allowance.&quot;)\n\n# ┌── 4. PLOT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfig, ax, COLORS, plt = viz.setup_plot(figsize=(6.0, 3.2))\nax.fill_betweenx(rows_axis, x_cam - blur_mm / 2, x_cam + blur_mm / 2, color=PETROL, alpha=0.14, lw=0)\nax.plot(x_cam, rows_axis, color=PETROL, lw=2.0)\nax.axvline(dloc_mm, color=PINE, lw=1.1, ls=(0, (4, 3)))\nax.text(dloc_mm - 1, n_rows * 0.08, rf&quot;$\\delta_{{\\mathrm{{loc}}}}$ = {RS.delta_loc_str}&quot;, ha=&quot;right&quot;, va=&quot;center&quot;, fontsize=9, color=PINE)\nax.annotate(\n    f&quot;skew {RS.skew_str}&quot;,\n    xy=(x_cam[-1], rows_axis[-1]),\n    xytext=(x_cam[-1] + 6, n_rows * 0.72),\n    fontsize=9,\n    color=PETROL,\n    va=&quot;center&quot;,\n    arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=&quot;-&quot;, color=SOFT, lw=0.6),\n)\nax.text(-blur_mm / 2 + 0.5, n_rows * 0.18, f&quot;blur {RS.blur_str}&quot;, ha=&quot;left&quot;, va=&quot;center&quot;, fontsize=9, color=SOFT)\nax.set_xlim(-blur_mm / 2 - 2, dloc_mm + 6)\nax.set_ylim(n_rows * 1.03, -n_rows * 0.03)\nax.set_xlabel(&quot;camera position along travel when the row is read (mm)&quot;, fontsize=9)\nax.set_ylabel(&quot;image row&quot;, fontsize=9)\nax.tick_params(labelsize=9)\ntop = ax.secondary_xaxis(&quot;top&quot;, functions=(lambda x: x / v_mm_per_ms, lambda t: t * v_mm_per_ms))\ntop.set_xlabel(&quot;read time after the top row (ms)&quot;, fontsize=9)\ntop.tick_params(labelsize=9)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n]```\n\n:::\n\n::: {#fig-rolling-shutter-skew fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;H&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Rolling shutter distortion geometry**: A phone camera&#x27;s rolling shutter photographs a turning aircraft propeller. Because scanlines are exposed and read sequentially from top to bottom rather than simultaneously, blades that move appreciably during the frame&#x27;s readout are recorded as curved, disconnected arcs; each row samples the blade at a later instant. On a moving robot the same row-by-row sampling shears straight structures and biases the geometry downstream estimators triangulate. (Credit: [Dick Lyon](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Propellor_with_rolling-shutter_artifact.jpg), Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0.)&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Photograph from an aircraft window of a propeller and engine nacelle in flight, with sky and ground below. The blades appear as bent, curved shapes, some detached from the hub, instead of straight radial blades.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\nGeometric unwarping must precede spatial interpretation, as the detached propeller blades of @fig-rolling-shutter-skew show. Because scanlines are exposed and read in sequence rather than simultaneously, rapid motion shears radial structures across rows. Learned feature backbones can misinterpret row-by-row shear as intrinsic physical geometry, while classical stereo matchers fail when scanline displacement shifts correspondences off the calibrated epipolar line. Scaling up model capacity cannot compensate for uncorrected physical sampling distortion; the network learns biased geometric representations instead.\n\n: **Epipolar Line**: For a calibrated camera pair, a point seen in one image can appear in the other only along one line, the projection of the ray from the first camera&#x27;s center through that point (@sec-perception-error-clearance derives it). Stereo matchers search that line alone, so a rolling-shutter shear that moves a correspondence even one row off it produces a mismatch rather than a slightly worse match. \n\n### Camera calibration\n\nUnwarping a rolling-shutter image still leaves a pixel without a physical direction or position if the camera&#x27;s internal lens parameters or chassis mount have shifted. Under the **pinhole camera model** [@hartley2003multiple], the intrinsic matrix $\\CameraIntrinsicCalibrationMatrix$ maps a point $\\CameraFramePoint = [\\CameraFrameHorizontalCoordinate, \\CameraFrameVerticalCoordinate, \\CameraFrameDepth]^\\top$ in the camera&#x27;s optical frame to the pixel $[\\PixelColumnCoordinate, \\PixelRowCoordinate]^\\top$:\n$$\\HomogeneousPixelCoordinates \\sim \\frac{1}{\\CameraFrameDepth} \\CameraIntrinsicCalibrationMatrix \\CameraFramePoint = \\frac{1}{\\CameraFrameDepth} \\begin{bmatrix} \\HorizontalFocalLength &amp; 0 &amp; \\PrincipalPointColumn \\\\ 0 &amp; \\VerticalFocalLength &amp; \\PrincipalPointRow \\\\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} \\CameraFrameHorizontalCoordinate \\\\ \\CameraFrameVerticalCoordinate \\\\ \\CameraFrameDepth \\end{bmatrix}$$ {#eq-percep-pinhole-projection}\nwhere $\\HorizontalFocalLength, \\VerticalFocalLength$ are focal lengths in pixels and $(\\PrincipalPointColumn, \\PrincipalPointRow)$ is the principal point. Inverting the map recovers only a bearing ray, along which every depth is consistent with the pixel:\n$$\\BearingRay(\\HomogeneousPixelCoordinates) = \\CameraIntrinsicCalibrationMatrix^{-1} \\HomogeneousPixelCoordinates = \\begin{bmatrix} \\frac{\\PixelColumnCoordinate - \\PrincipalPointColumn}{\\HorizontalFocalLength} \\\\ \\frac{\\PixelRowCoordinate - \\PrincipalPointRow}{\\VerticalFocalLength} \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix}$$ {#eq-percep-bearing-ray}\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ CAPTURE-TIMING AND ATTITUDE ERROR SCALES AT THE RACK END</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: The bearing-ray paragraph of</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          @sec-perception-transduction-calibration and the hardware-trigger</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          paragraph of @sec-perception-handoff.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Put machine-scale numbers on timing and attitude errors: travel per</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          millisecond of misdating, lateral error per degree at the rack end,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the travel hidden by an uncorrected exposure-start trigger stamp,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          and the lateral error per millisecond of stamp jitter while turning.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    1.5 mm per ms; 19.2 mm per degree at 1.10 m; 8 ms and 12 mm for</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the trigger correction; 0.5 mrad and 0.55 mm per ms of jitter.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Scenarios.WarehouseRackEndTimingAttitude (one millisecond and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          one degree at the rack end) and the trigger offset of</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraAge.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>import math from mlsysim import <em> from mlsysim.core.units import </em> from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_angle, fmt_latency, fmt_length, fmt_velocity, check</p>\n<p>RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class CaptureTimingScale: \"\"\"Machine-scale travel and lever-arm errors from timing and attitude offsets.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>scenario = Scenarios.WarehouseRackEndTimingAttitude  # category: illustrative (1 ms, 1 degree at the rack end) v = scenario.speed.to(meter / second)  # category: illustrative (drive limit) z = scenario.distance  # category: illustrative (rack-end distance) omega_yaw = scenario.yaw_rate  # category: illustrative (yaw rate)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>_err = scenario.result _age = Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraAge.result dx_per_ms = _err.timing_travel.to(millimeter) dy_per_deg = _err.angle_lateral.to(millimeter) t_trigger_corr = _age.trigger_offset.to(millisecond) dx_trigger = _age.trigger_travel.to(millimeter) dtheta_per_ms = _err.misorientation.to(milliradian) dy_per_ms_jitter = _err.timing_lateral.to(millimeter)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(dx_per_ms.magnitude - 1.5) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Unexpected travel per ms: {dx_per_ms}\") check(abs(dy_per_deg.magnitude - 19.2) &lt; 0.05, f\"Unexpected lateral error per degree: {dy_per_deg}\") check(abs(dx_trigger.magnitude - 12.0) &lt; 1e-6, f\"Unexpected trigger-correction travel: {dx_trigger}\") check(abs(dy_per_ms_jitter.magnitude - 0.55) &lt; 1e-6, f\"Unexpected lateral error per ms of jitter: {dy_per_ms_jitter}\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>v_str = fmt_velocity(v, unit=meter / second, precision=1) z_str = fmt_length(z.to(meter), unit=meter, precision=2) dx_per_ms_str = fmt_length(dx_per_ms, unit=millimeter, precision=1) dy_per_deg_str = fmt_length(dy_per_deg, unit=millimeter, precision=1) t_trigger_corr_str = fmt_latency(t_trigger_corr, unit=millisecond, precision=0) dx_trigger_str = fmt_length(dx_trigger, unit=millimeter, precision=0) dtheta_per_ms_str = fmt_angle(dtheta_per_ms, unit=milliradian, precision=1) dy_per_ms_jitter_str = fmt_length(dy_per_ms_jitter, unit=millimeter, precision=2) ]```</p>\n<p>This bearing ray emanates from the camera's optical center at the precise instant of photon capture ${\\CaptureTimestamp}_0$. If downstream motion planning unprojects $\\BearingRay(\\HomogeneousPixelCoordinates)$ using the robot's <em>current</em> pose instead of its historical capture pose, the current evidence age at that downstream use, $\\CurrentMonotonicTime - {\\CaptureTimestamp}<em>0$, translates the ray origin by $\\ObservationAgeDisplacement = \\int</em>{{\\CaptureTimestamp}_0}^{\\CurrentMonotonicTime} \\Velocity(\\TimeInstant)\\,d\\TimeInstant$. These timing and angular errors scale along entirely different physical axes. At the <code>[CaptureTimingScale.v_str]</code> drive limit, each millisecond of unmodeled latency shifts the ray origin by <code>[CaptureTimingScale.dx_per_ms_str]</code>, making the dispatch-age contribution of <code>[RackEndOpeningAge.t_age_str]</code> displace the origin by <code>[RackEndOpeningAge.dx_str]</code>. Attitude errors, by contrast, scale with range. A single degree of orientation misalignment shifts a target at the <code>[CaptureTimingScale.z_str]</code> rack-end distance by <code>[CaptureTimingScale.dy_per_deg_str]</code> laterally, expanding proportionally with distance. Unchecked, either discrepancy can push a physical rack upright entirely outside the bounding volume evaluated by the permission path. A single monocular camera cannot recover metric range along its bearing rays; scale must be supplied by stereo disparity, an active ranging sensor, or a learned geometric prior, and each introduces uncertainty that @sec-perception-error-clearance audits against the operating clearance budget. Optical lens distortion is rectified inside the hardware image signal processor (ISP) stage detailed in @tbl-08-latency-budget. @Sec-appendix-systems-vision develops the full projection equations, radial distortion models, and Zhang's planar calibration method [@zhang2000flexible].</p>\n<p>Spatial extrinsics and temporal synchronization between the camera and the IMU must be calibrated jointly [@furgale2013unified]. An extrinsic orientation error $\\ExtrinsicMisalignmentAngle$ misplaces an observed feature at depth $\\TargetRange$ by approximately $\\TargetRange\\,\\ExtrinsicMisalignmentAngle$, while spatial translation between the camera center and the IMU introduces the centripetal and tangential lever-arm accelerations given in @sec-appendix-control-spatial. Similarly, an uncalibrated temporal offset $\\CameraImuTimeOffset$ between image exposure and inertial sampling causes an effective orientation error of $\\YawRate\\,\\CameraImuTimeOffset$ while turning at angular velocity $\\YawRate$. This timing skew displaces the reconstructed feature by approximately $\\TargetRange\\,\\YawRate\\,\\CameraImuTimeOffset$, growing with both vehicle yaw rate and target distance. Hardware-latching exposure triggers and IMU clock signals to a shared real-time counter bounds this synchronization offset to sub-microsecond regimes, leaving small residual latencies for online filtering. Because mechanical shocks and thermal expansion degrade calibration over time, every observation packet must record its calibration identifier alongside its empirical operating envelope.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h3\">Choosing metric depth for the aisle</h3>\n<p>Even when intrinsic and extrinsic calibration are exact, a single monocular bearing ray leaves range undetermined, so robotic systems often pair passive cameras with active optical depth cameras to recover scale. In a warehouse aisle, however, ambient lighting, surface material properties, and multi-robot optical interference dictate which depth-sensing principle survives in practice. <strong>Structured light</strong> projects a known pseudo-random or coded infrared pattern onto the scene and triangulates surface deformation using an offset receiver camera. Although this active pattern injects distinct visual texture onto featureless cardboard cartons, direct sunlight from skylights or open loading docks easily washes out the projected infrared pattern, and overlapping projectors from adjacent mobile bases create optical interference that corrupts feature correspondence. Engineering qualification must therefore evaluate the operating environment under peak ambient lux and multi-robot contention rather than treating structured light as an unconditional depth source.</p>\n<p>Rather than triangulating a spatial pattern, <strong>indirect time-of-flight (iToF)</strong> calculates distance by measuring the phase shift of continuous-wave amplitude-modulated infrared light. Phase differences yield dense range maps with compact hardware. Modulation periodicity, however, creates phase wrapping and distance ambiguity intervals. Multipath reflections in metallic warehouse corners and industrial shelving also send diffuse photons along several bounces, which bias the depth estimate. Motion between sequential phase exposures also introduces edge artifacts in dynamic scenes. <strong>Direct time-of-flight (dToF)</strong> avoids periodic phase wrapping by measuring the arrival times of discrete photon pulses using single-photon avalanche diodes (SPADs), yet it remains subject to timing jitter in the receiver discriminator, multipath contamination, and optical pile-up from high-reflectance foregrounds.</p>\n<p><strong>Active stereo</strong> combines structured infrared illumination with a calibrated stereo camera pair. Unlike structured light systems that must decode a proprietary pattern, active stereo uses projected speckle purely to synthesize artificial texture on smooth surfaces while computing standard epipolar stereo correspondence. If bright ambient light washes out the infrared pattern, the underlying stereo pipeline continues operating over ambient visible texture; if a smooth surface lacks ambient texture, however, high illumination alone still cannot resolve depth. The range-dependent uncertainty analysis in @sec-perception-error-clearance determines whether active stereo depth uncertainty satisfies the robot's required stopping clearance.</p>\n<p>For the rack end, the running machine carries none of these depth cameras. Each of these active principles has a failure mode the aisle supplies, whether daylight through open dock doors, a neighboring base's projector, or multipath off metal shelving. The machine's metric range comes instead from the OS1-64 LiDAR described later, and the navigation camera contributes bearing and class. Stereo returns in @sec-perception-error-clearance only as an illustrative rig that shows where a camera-only design meets a range wall.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h3\">Event cameras and asynchronous contrast</h3>\n<p>Before turning to that LiDAR range sensor, one other optical transducer addresses the motion blur and dynamic-range limits of conventional CMOS sensors, which integrate photons over a fixed exposure window and force a trade-off between blur on fast targets and photon starvation in dim aisles. <strong>Neuromorphic event cameras</strong> (Dynamic Vision Sensors, or DVS) avoid frame-rate exposure trade-offs by abandoning discrete frames entirely [@lichtsteiner2008128; @gallego2022event]. Each pixel on an event sensor array operates autonomously, containing a logarithmic photodiode, an analog amplifier, and two differential voltage comparators. The pixel continuously monitors incoming scene radiance $\\SceneRadiance(\\PixelColumnCoordinate, \\PixelRowCoordinate, \\TimeInstant)$. Whenever the temporal change in logarithmic intensity exceeds a configurable contrast threshold $\\EventContrastThreshold$, the pixel emits an asynchronous digital event packet $\\EventPacket_k$: $$|\\FiniteChange \\ln \\SceneRadiance(\\PixelColumnCoordinate, \\PixelRowCoordinate, \\TimeInstant)| = |\\ln \\SceneRadiance(\\PixelColumnCoordinate, \\PixelRowCoordinate, \\TimeInstant) - \\ln \\SceneRadiance(\\PixelColumnCoordinate, \\PixelRowCoordinate, \\PixelLastEventTime)| \\ge \\EventContrastThreshold$$ $$\\EventPacket_k = \\langle \\PixelColumnCoordinate_k, \\PixelRowCoordinate_k, \\TimeInstant_k, \\EventPolarity_k \\rangle$$ where $(\\PixelColumnCoordinate_k, \\PixelRowCoordinate_k)$ is the pixel address, $\\TimeInstant_k$ is the event timestamp latched with microsecond resolution ($1\\text{--}10\\,\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$), and $\\EventPolarity_k \\in \\{-1, +1\\}$ is the polarity indicating whether radiance increased or decreased.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Neuromorphic</strong>: Carver Mead coined the term at Caltech for analog circuits that compute the way neural tissue does [@mead1990], and the silicon retina built in his group is the ancestor of the Dynamic Vision Sensor [@lichtsteiner2008128]. Like the retina, each pixel reports change rather than level, so an obstacle that stands still in front of a stationary sensor produces no events and is invisible to it.</p>\n<p>Event sensing preserves the temporal fidelity of high-speed edge transitions without waiting for a complete exposure cycle and serial scanline readout, providing high temporal resolution for rapid maneuvers. The systems trade-off lies in temporal contrast, because static obstacles produce zero events when the camera is stationary and remain undetectable without ego-motion. High-frequency fluorescent lighting flicker and thermal noise also generate spurious event bursts. The bandwidth demand across the physical sensor interface is therefore dynamic and scene-dependent rather than deterministic and fixed.</p>\n<p>Downstream estimation algorithms must convert asynchronous coordinate streams in $(\\PixelColumnCoordinate, \\PixelRowCoordinate, \\TimeInstant)$ space into representations suitable for neural networks or state observers. Accumulating events into fixed time slices allows the use of standard convolutional backbones but reintroduces latency; processing individual event timestamps maintains sub-millisecond temporal responsiveness at the cost of specialized asynchronous graph or spiking architectures. Neither representation provides absolute metric depth without stereo baseline pairing or active sensor fusion. For the rack-end decision, an event camera would add edge timing but no range, and it would lose a worker who stops at the corner, so the running machine does not carry one.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h3\">LiDAR and radar ranging</h3>\n<p>Passive cameras cannot measure metric distance along a single viewing ray, and short-range depth cameras fail when dock sunlight or metal shelving corrupts their patterns. Active ranging sensors, principally Light Detection and Ranging (<strong>LiDAR</strong>) and Radio Detection and Ranging (<strong>radar</strong>), overcome these limits by emitting structured electromagnetic energy and timing its return to measure metric range and radial velocity directly. Pulsed time-of-flight LiDAR calculates radial distance $\\RadialRange$ by measuring the round-trip flight duration $\\PulseRoundTripTime$ of an emitted laser pulse traveling at the speed of light $\\VacuumLightSpeed$: $$\\RadialRange = \\frac{\\VacuumLightSpeed \\cdot \\PulseRoundTripTime}{2}$$ In three-dimensional space, optical scanners steer each pulse along azimuth $\\LidarAzimuthAngle$ and elevation $\\LidarElevationAngle$, casting an emitted ray along unit direction vector $\\LidarBeamDirection(\\LidarAzimuthAngle, \\LidarElevationAngle) = [\\cos\\LidarElevationAngle\\cos\\LidarAzimuthAngle, \\; \\cos\\LidarElevationAngle\\sin\\LidarAzimuthAngle, \\; \\sin\\LidarElevationAngle]^\\top$. When reflected energy trips the receiver threshold after flight time $\\PulseRoundTripTime$, the sensor unprojects the return into a 3D point in the sensor frame $\\LidarReferenceFrame$ via ray casting (@eq-percep-lidar-ray): $$\\LidarFramePoint = \\RadialRange \\cdot \\LidarBeamDirection(\\LidarAzimuthAngle, \\LidarElevationAngle) = \\frac{\\VacuumLightSpeed \\cdot \\PulseRoundTripTime}{2} \\begin{bmatrix} \\cos\\LidarElevationAngle \\cos\\LidarAzimuthAngle \\\\ \\cos\\LidarElevationAngle \\sin\\LidarAzimuthAngle \\\\ \\sin\\LidarElevationAngle \\end{bmatrix}$$ {#eq-percep-lidar-ray}</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ THE MACHINE&#x27;S LIDAR: RANGE ERROR AND SWEEP DISTORTION\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: The LiDAR paragraphs of @sec-perception-transduction-calibration;\n# │          reused by the LiDAR age budget (the state-estimation example of\n# │          @sec-perception-state-estimation re-derives its inputs).\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Give the OS1-64&#x27;s datasheet range precision and bias, and size\n# │          the motion the base makes during one 20 Hz revolution.\n# │ Show:    precision 5-30 mm (1 sigma), accuracy 25 mm; 50 ms sweep; 75 mm\n# │          of travel and 25 mrad (1.4 degrees) of yaw across one sweep.\n# │ How:     Ouster OS1 Rev7 datasheet (firmware 3.1) precision and accuracy\n# │          (registry), specified in the 1024 x 10 Hz mode; sweep motion from\n# │          Scenarios.WarehouseRackEndLidarAge (RM.lidar.scan_rate, the\n# │          drive limit, and the site&#x27;s yaw rate).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_latency, fmt_length, fmt_frequency, fmt_angle, check\n\nRM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\n\nclass LidarSweep:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Range error and one-revolution motion distortion for the machine&#x27;s OS1-64.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    lidar = RM.lidar  # category: datasheet (registry: Ouster OS1-64)\n    f_scan = lidar.scan_rate  # category: datasheet (configured 20 Hz mode)\n    sigma_r_min = lidar.range_precision_min  # category: datasheet (registry: OS1 Rev7 precision min, 1 sigma, beyond 1 m)\n    sigma_r_max = lidar.range_precision_max  # category: datasheet (registry: OS1 Rev7 precision max, 1 sigma, beyond 1 m)\n    bias_r = lidar.range_accuracy  # category: datasheet (registry: OS1 Rev7 accuracy, Lambertian targets)\n    wavelength = lidar.laser_wavelength  # category: datasheet (registry: OS1 Rev7 laser wavelength)\n    v = Scenarios.WarehouseRackEndLidarAge.speed.to(meter / second)  # category: illustrative (drive limit)\n    omega_yaw = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle.yaw_rate_turn  # category: illustrative (yaw rate)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    _sweep = Scenarios.WarehouseRackEndLidarAge.result\n    t_sweep = _sweep.sweep_period.to(millisecond)\n    dx_sweep = _sweep.sweep_travel.to(millimeter)\n    dtheta_sweep = _sweep.sweep_rotation.to(milliradian)\n    dtheta_sweep_deg = dtheta_sweep.to(degree)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(f_scan.to(hertz).magnitude - 20.0) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Unexpected LiDAR scan rate: {f_scan}&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_sweep.magnitude - 50.0) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Unexpected sweep period: {t_sweep}&quot;)\n    check(abs(dx_sweep.magnitude - 75.0) &lt; 1e-6, f&quot;Unexpected travel per sweep: {dx_sweep}&quot;)\n    check(abs(dtheta_sweep.magnitude - 25.0) &lt; 1e-6, f&quot;Unexpected yaw per sweep: {dtheta_sweep}&quot;)\n    check(wavelength.to(nanometer).magnitude &lt; 1000, &quot;The OS1-64 emits in the near infrared that silicon detectors sense.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    wavelength_str = fmt(wavelength.to(nanometer).magnitude, precision=0)\n    f_scan_str = fmt_frequency(f_scan, unit=hertz, precision=0)\n    sigma_r_min_str = fmt_length(sigma_r_min, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    sigma_r_max_str = fmt_length(sigma_r_max, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    bias_r_str = fmt_length(bias_r, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    t_sweep_str = fmt_latency(t_sweep, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    dx_sweep_str = fmt_length(dx_sweep, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    omega_yaw_str = fmt(omega_yaw.to(radian / second).magnitude, precision=1)\n    dtheta_sweep_str = fmt(dtheta_sweep.magnitude, precision=0)\n    dtheta_sweep_deg_str = fmt(dtheta_sweep_deg.magnitude, precision=1)\n]```\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ ACTIVE-SENSOR RESOLUTION SCALES</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: The LiDAR transverse-uncertainty item and the FMCW range-resolution</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          paragraph of @sec-perception-transduction-calibration.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Size the LiDAR spot at range and the FMCW radar range resolution.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    30 m x 2 mrad = 60 mm spot; c / (2 x 4 GHz) = 3.75 cm.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     w_spot = R * div (full-angle divergence); Delta R = c / (2 B).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import <em> from mlsysim.core.units import </em> from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_angle, fmt_length, fmt_frequency, check</p>\n<p>class ActiveSensorScales: \"\"\"LiDAR spot size and FMCW range resolution for generic sensors.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>r_spot = 30.0 <em> meter  # category: illustrative (range for the spot example) div = 2.0 </em> milliradian  # category: illustrative (generic beam divergence) b_chirp = 4.0 <em> gigahertz  # category: illustrative (77-81 GHz automotive sweep) c_light = (1 </em> ureg.speed_of_light).to(meter / second)  # category: constant (speed of light)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>spot = (r_spot <em> div.to(radian).magnitude).to(millimeter) dr_radar = (c_light / (2 </em> b_chirp)).to(centimeter)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(spot.magnitude - 60.0) &lt; 1e-6, f\"Unexpected LiDAR spot: {spot}\") check(abs(dr_radar.magnitude - 3.747) &lt; 0.01, f\"Unexpected radar range resolution: {dr_radar}\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>r_spot_str = fmt_length(r_spot, unit=meter, precision=0) div_str = fmt_angle(div, unit=milliradian, precision=0) spot_str = fmt_length(spot, unit=millimeter, precision=0) b_chirp_str = fmt_frequency(b_chirp, unit=gigahertz, precision=0) dr_radar_str = fmt_length(dr_radar, unit=centimeter, precision=2) ]```</p>\n<p>Spatial uncertainty in this unprojected 3D point decomposes into two distinct physical mechanisms:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Radial uncertainty ($\\RadialRangeStandardDeviation$)</strong>: Governed by receiver discriminator timing jitter $\\ReceiverTimingJitter$ and pulse rise time, $\\RadialRangeStandardDeviation = \\frac{\\VacuumLightSpeed}{2} \\ReceiverTimingJitter$ remains approximately constant across operating distances until return signal attenuation drops photon counts near the detection threshold. The manufacturer datasheet for the robot's OS1-64 specifies a one-standard-deviation precision between <code>[LidarSweep.sigma_r_min_str]</code> and <code>[LidarSweep.sigma_r_max_str]</code> beyond 1 m. It also specifies a systematic accuracy bias of <code>[LidarSweep.bias_r_str]</code> on diffuse targets in the specified 1024×10 Hz mode [@ouster2025os1]. These are datasheet scales, not qualified bounds for the running machine's selected scan mode, surfaces, and motion. The accuracy figure describes the bias of the mean, a calibration offset that temporal averaging cannot remove.</li>\n<li><strong>Transverse footprint</strong>: Governed by optical beam divergence $\\BeamDivergenceAngle$; internal angular encoder quantization contributes a separate bearing error. The laser pulse expands into a narrow cone rather than propagating along an infinitesimal line. Taking $\\BeamDivergenceAngle$ as the full-angle divergence, the transverse spot diameter scales as range times divergence. At $\\RadialRange =$ <code>[ActiveSensorScales.r_spot_str]</code>, the illustrative <code>[ActiveSensorScales.div_str]</code> divergence gives a <code>[ActiveSensorScales.spot_str]</code> spot diameter. This geometric footprint is not a standard deviation of position error. When it strikes a surface boundary, the detector receives mixed returns from foreground and background, blurring geometric edges; the resulting error distribution must be measured.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>How far a LiDAR can hold those radial and angular bounds depends on its optical wavelength and scanning mechanics. Most terrestrial systems operate either at near-infrared wavelengths below $1{,}000\\,\\text{nm}$, where silicon detectors respond, or at an eye-safe $1{,}550\\,\\text{nm}$ (permitting higher pulse energies under IEC 60825-1 Class 1 laser regulations). The machine's OS1-64 is of the first kind; it emits at <code>[LidarSweep.wavelength_str]</code> nm as a Class 1 laser product [@ouster2025os1]. Mechanical spinning LiDAR systems rotate an array of 16 to 128 laser diodes and avalanche photodiode (APD) detectors across an internal spindle at $10\\text{--}20\\,\\text{Hz}$ to sweep a $360^\\circ$ panoramic point cloud.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Laser Class 1</strong>: The class of IEC 60825-1 whose emission is safe under all reasonably foreseeable viewing, including through optical instruments [@iec60825part1]. Below about $1{,}400\\,\\text{nm}$ the eye focuses the beam onto the retina, while at $1{,}550\\,\\text{nm}$ the cornea and lens absorb it first, so the permitted pulse energy is far higher. Those longer wavelengths need indium gallium arsenide detectors rather than silicon, which makes wavelength a range vs. cost choice made under a fixed safety ceiling.</p>\n<p>Much like rolling-shutter cameras expose scanlines in sequence, a spinning mechanical LiDAR does not record an instantaneous 3D snapshot. Completing a full $360^\\circ$ azimuth sweep requires $\\LidarSweepPeriod = 1/\\LidarScanRate$, corresponding to <code>[LidarSweep.t_sweep_str]</code> on the machine's OS1-64 configured at <code>[LidarSweep.f_scan_str]</code>. If the mobile base translates at velocity $\\Velocity$ and rotates at angular velocity $\\AngularVelocity$ while the sensor spins, pulses fired at the start of the sweep ($\\LidarAzimuthAngle = 0^\\circ$) and near completion ($\\LidarAzimuthAngle = 360^\\circ$) originate from physically distinct vehicle poses. At the <code>[RollingShutterSkew.v_str]</code> drive limit and an illustrative <code>[LidarSweep.omega_yaw_str]</code> rad/s turn rate, the robot travels <code>[LidarSweep.dx_sweep_str]</code> and rotates <code>[LidarSweep.dtheta_sweep_str]</code> mrad (<code>[LidarSweep.dtheta_sweep_deg_str]</code>°) during a single scan. Without motion correction, this sampling lag produces an apparent geometric tear where the end of the revolution meets the beginning, while shearing vertical structures across the corridor.</p>\n<p>Restoring rigid geometric fidelity requires <strong>continuous-time LiDAR de-skewing</strong>. The sensor hardware latches each point return $\\CartesianPosition_i$ with an exact firing timestamp $\\TimeInstant_i \\in [\\LidarSweepStartTime, \\LidarSweepStartTime + \\LidarSweepPeriod]$. A rigid pose transformation $\\Pose \\in \\text{SE}(3)$ encapsulates orientation and translation. In the two-subscript convention, the first subscript names the destination frame and the second names the source: <code>T_ab</code> maps coordinates from frame b to frame a, and its inverse maps back. A homogeneous point appends a final coordinate of 1 so that the matrix applies translation as well as rotation. @Sec-appendix-control-spatial develops this representation and composition; @sec-perception-coordinate-chain applies it to the machine's mounting chain. Combining high-frequency (<code>[RollingShutterSkew.f_imu_str]</code>) inertial data with wheel odometry allows the state estimator to compute the continuous trajectory of the vehicle body frame, $\\WorldBodyTransform(\\TimeInstant_i)$. Applying the calibrated mounting extrinsic $\\BodyLidarTransform$ transforms coordinates from the LiDAR frame to the body frame, yielding the world-frame sensor pose $\\WorldLidarTransform(\\TimeInstant_i) = \\WorldBodyTransform(\\TimeInstant_i)\\BodyLidarTransform$. Each individual homogeneous point return is then projected back into the LiDAR's coordinate frame at the frame reference epoch $\\LidarSweepStartTime$: $$\\CartesianPosition_i(\\LidarSweepStartTime) = \\WorldLidarTransform^{-1}(\\LidarSweepStartTime) \\cdot \\WorldLidarTransform(\\TimeInstant_i) \\cdot \\CartesianPosition_i(\\TimeInstant_i)$$ De-skewing is what makes an accumulated LiDAR map usable. The returns registered in @fig-08-lidar-pointcloud, gathered over about 18 s of driving through a city intersection, keep crisp building facades, road striping, and parked vehicles only because each return was placed with the vehicle pose at its own firing epoch $\\TimeInstant_i$. Independently moving obstacles in the environment require separate velocity estimation and clustering. Flash LiDAR architectures avoid sweep distortion altogether by illuminating the entire field of view with a single laser pulse to capture an instantaneous 3D range matrix. Distributing finite optical pulse energy across a wide solid angle, however, sharply limits maximum operational range compared to concentrated, steerable beams.</p>\n<p>Millimeter-wave radar offers an orthogonal active sensing channel, operating in the $76\\text{--}81\\,\\text{GHz}$ band ($\\RadiationWavelength \\approx 3.9\\,\\text{mm}$). Rather than emitting short, high-energy pulses, <strong>Frequency-Modulated Continuous-Wave (FMCW) radar</strong> transmits linear frequency chirps whose instantaneous carrier frequency sweeps across bandwidth $\\text{BW}<em>{\\text{chirp}}$ over chirp duration $\\ChirpDuration$ with slope $\\ChirpSlope = \\text{BW}</em>{\\text{chirp}} / \\ChirpDuration$. When the emitted waveform reflects from an obstacle at range $\\RadialRange$, the echo returns delayed by round-trip flight time $\\EchoRoundTripTime = 2\\RadialRange/\\VacuumLightSpeed$. Mixing the received echo with a replica of the transmitted signal generates an intermediate frequency (IF) beat tone at frequency $\\BeatFrequency$: $$\\BeatFrequency = \\ChirpSlope \\cdot \\EchoRoundTripTime = \\frac{2 \\RadialRange \\, \\text{BW}<em>{\\text{chirp}}}{\\VacuumLightSpeed \\, \\ChirpDuration}$$ Computing a fast Fourier transform across the digitized fast-time samples of each chirp (the <strong>Range-FFT</strong>) extracts metric range $\\RadialRange$ directly, without requiring optical contrast or ambient scene illumination. Physical range resolution depends strictly on total sweep bandwidth: $$\\RadarRangeResolution = \\frac{\\VacuumLightSpeed}{2\\,\\text{BW}</em>{\\text{chirp}}}$$ For a standard <code>[ActiveSensorScales.b_chirp_str]</code> radar sweep ($77\\text{--}81\\,\\text{GHz}$), FMCW radar achieves a fine range resolution of $\\RadarRangeResolution \\approx$ <code>[ActiveSensorScales.dr_radar_str]</code>. The transmitted chirps simultaneously encode radial velocity and bearing. A moving target shifts the phase of successive chirp reflections proportionally to its Doppler velocity; evaluating a second Fourier transform across chirps (the Doppler-FFT) extracts this radial velocity within a single coherent processing interval, avoiding the latency and noise of multi-frame position differencing. Array processing across multiple receive antennas resolves target azimuth and elevation, albeit at substantially coarser angular resolution than optical LiDAR. The running machine does not mount radar, but millimeter-wave sensing is a primary modality on outdoor autonomous platforms. Its millimeter wavelength penetrates the dust, fog, and steam that scatter optical wavelengths (@sec-perception-failure-modes). However, radar returns remain sensitive to specular scattering, surface orientation, and multi-path ghost targets, providing complementary evidence rather than an infallible, all-weather guarantee.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h3\">Tactile contact sensing</h3>\n<p>At the end-effector interface, external cameras and range finders cannot measure contact pressure or verify friction adhesion during physical manipulation; indeed, the gripper structure often occludes the contact patch entirely. An optical tactile sensor addresses this perceptual blind spot by mounting a compliant, internally illuminated elastomer membrane on the fingertip and imaging its deformation with an internal camera. Photometric stereo algorithms translate surface shading into calibrated local contact topography, while tracking arrays of embedded fiducial markers yields direct measurements of tangential shear strain and incipient slip. Converting these raw optical deformations into a calibrated contact wrench requires a validated finite-element or empirical model that accounts for elastomer hysteresis, dynamic loading, and mechanical wear over time.</p>\n<p>: <strong>GelSight</strong>: Adelson's group at MIT turned a reflective elastomer skin and an internal camera into a contact sensor that recovers surface geometry and, from painted markers, shear force [@yuan2017gelsight]. The design makes touch one more image stream, so a tactile frame carries an exposure, a readout, and a calibration of its own and must be dated and qualified like the navigation camera's.</p>\n<p>Tactile sensing thus governs the terminal contact phase of robotic grasping, whereas external cameras and range sensors guide the initial approach trajectory. Across both regimes, downstream estimators cannot treat sensor outputs as timeless, unframed tensors. Every tactile frame and spatial point cloud carries a finite physical age, a mounting transform, and a calibration uncertainty that must be attached to the measurement before any consumer can use it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h2\">Dating and Placing the Measurement</h2>\n<p>A frame from the navigation camera lands in host memory as an array of pixel values. Nothing in that array says when the light was captured, where the camera was mounted, or how far a position read from it can be trusted, yet the planner that checks rack-end clearance needs all three. Every modality has the same gap. A trajectory optimizer cannot plan against unorganized time-of-flight returns, and a force controller cannot track a grid of raw intensities. Downstream estimation, planning, and spatial memory require structured measurements: metric positions, bounding volumes, and semantic classes, with directly measured velocity where the sensing modality supplies it. Translating raw streams into those measurements is the perception pipeline's responsibility. Whatever form its output takes, whether an explicit message packet or an observation token sequence, it must carry three mandatory properties before any consumer can use it: a capture timestamp, a reference frame, and a qualified uncertainty envelope.</p>\n<p>The first mandatory property is the <strong>capture timestamp</strong> ${\\CaptureTimestamp}_0$, which specifies the temporal midpoint of the energy integration window, the effective instant of a camera exposure or a laser firing. Because physical sensors do not take instantaneous snapshots, an optical camera integrates photons across an exposure duration while a mechanical lidar sweeps laser pulses over an azimuth arc across several milliseconds. Downstream state observers and vision-language-action policies require ${\\CaptureTimestamp}_0$ to ground observation tokens into the machine's dynamic equations of motion. By contrast, the arrival stamp $\\ArrivalTimestamp$ marks the moment the operating system kernel completes the direct memory access (DMA) transfer. It is the software arrival timestamp that @sec-data-hw-vs-sw-timestamps showed corrupting logged velocities; at runtime it corrupts the pose an observation is paired with. Half of the exposure duration, sensor readout, physical transport, and host DMA transfer all elapse before that interrupt fires, so the arrival offset $\\ArrivalStampOffset = \\ArrivalTimestamp - {\\CaptureTimestamp}_0$ is never zero. Substituting arrival time for capture time directly corrupts downstream state estimation and action generation.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ ARRIVAL-TIMESTAMP OFFSET ON THE NAVIGATION CAMERA\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: The capture-timestamp paragraph of @sec-perception-handoff.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Size the error an estimator makes when it dates the navigation\n# │          camera&#x27;s frame at DMA completion instead of at the exposure midpoint.\n# │ Show:    26.6 ms from exposure midpoint to DMA completion; 39.9 mm of base\n# │          travel at the 1.5 m/s drive limit.\n# │ How:     t_recv - t0 = t_exp/2 + t_readout + t_transport + t_dma, the\n# │          arrival offset of Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraAge.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt_latency, fmt_length, fmt_velocity, check\n\nRM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass ArrivalStampOffset:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Age hidden by an arrival timestamp on the running machine&#x27;s navigation camera.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    camera = RM.nav_camera  # category: datasheet (registry: navigation camera)\n    t_exp = camera.exposure_time  # category: chosen (configured exposure)\n    t_readout = camera.readout_time  # category: datasheet\n    t_transport = AISLE.t_transport  # category: illustrative\n    t_dma = AISLE.t_dma  # category: illustrative\n    v = Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraAge.speed  # category: illustrative (drive limit)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    tau_recv = Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraAge.result.arrival_offset.to(millisecond)\n    dx_recv = Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraAge.result.arrival_travel.to(millimeter)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(tau_recv.magnitude - 26.6) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected arrival offset: {tau_recv}&quot;)\n    check(abs(dx_recv.magnitude - 39.9) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected arrival-stamp displacement: {dx_recv}&quot;)\n    check(tau_recv &lt; AISLE.perception_age, &quot;Arrival offset must be part of the full observation age&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    tau_recv_str = fmt_latency(tau_recv, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    dx_recv_str = fmt_length(dx_recv, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    v_str = fmt_velocity(v.to(meter / second), unit=meter / second, precision=1)\n]```\n\nAt constant velocity $\\BaseTravelSpeed$, the hidden travel is $\\ArrivalOffsetTravel = \\BaseTravelSpeed\\,\\ArrivalStampOffset$. On the mobile manipulator, the navigation camera&#x27;s frame completes its DMA transfer `[ArrivalStampOffset.tau_recv_str]` after the exposure midpoint. An estimator that timestamps the frame upon arrival dates the measurement late by that full duration. At the base&#x27;s `[ArrivalStampOffset.v_str]` drive limit, this temporal mismatch pairs the visual observation with an estimated pose `[ArrivalStampOffset.dx_recv_str]` ahead of the physical position the camera occupied at capture.\n\nEliminating this arrival offset and bounding the camera-to-inertial offset $\\CameraImuTimeOffset$ of @sec-perception-transduction-calibration require stamping the capture event directly in hardware rather than recording when a software thread first handles the frame buffer. Software timestamps inherit operating system scheduling jitter, driver queue delays, and thread preemption. While the base rotates at `[RollingShutterSkew.omega_yaw_str]` rad/s, each millisecond of scheduling jitter misorients the camera by `[CaptureTimingScale.dtheta_per_ms_str]`, producing a lateral displacement of `[CaptureTimingScale.dy_per_ms_jitter_str]` at rack-end distance. Two hardware architectures avoid dating capture by software-handler scheduling. In the first architecture, a hardware GPIO strobe (such as FSYNC) from the IMU microcontroller simultaneously latches an inertial sample and triggers the camera exposure. Because this trigger edge marks only the start of exposure for the first sensor row rather than the optical midpoint, the driver must add $\\ExposureDuration/2$ to reach the exposure midpoint and $\\SensorRowIndex\\,\\RollingShutterRowReadout$ for the specific image row $\\SensorRowIndex$ under consideration. Omitting this correction dates the navigation camera frame `[CaptureTimingScale.t_trigger_corr_str]` early, translating to `[CaptureTimingScale.dx_trigger_str]` of unmodeled travel at the drive limit. Because automatic exposure control varies $\\ExposureDuration$ dynamically across frames, the driver must also apply the specific exposure time reported in each frame&#x27;s metadata. In the second architecture, used for networked sensors such as the lidar units and GigE Vision cameras, each peripheral timestamps samples against an onboard hardware clock. The Precision Time Protocol (PTP) [@ieee15882019], or its time-sensitive networking profile gPTP [@ieee8021as2020], disciplines these local clocks to a master reference across the Ethernet bus. Each free-running oscillator (the camera trigger counter, the LiDAR&#x27;s internal clock, the application processor, the safety MCU) defines its own **clock domain**. A capture time stamped in one domain cannot be compared with a time read in another until it is converted. The largest error that conversion may carry is the **clock-conversion bound** $\\ClockConversionBound$, which every dated observation must carry alongside its capture time; @sec-perception-qualifying-error details how to measure it.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ EXTRINSIC ANGLE ERRORS AGAINST THE AISLE'S CLEARANCES</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: The reference-frame paragraph of @sec-perception-handoff, the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          transform-chain paragraph of @sec-perception-error-clearance, and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the calibration takeaway.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Tie two lever-arm errors to the stopping budget's terms: an</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          uncalibrated 1 degree mount error at 15 m against the base's side</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          clearance, and a 0.005 rad extrinsic shift at 10 m against delta_loc.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    0.26 m, about 1.7x the 0.15 m side clearance; 50 mm, the whole</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          delta_loc allowance.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Delta_y = d theta: Scenarios.WarehouseLongAisleMountError</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          (against AISLE.side_clearance) and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          Scenarios.WarehouseLidarExtrinsicShift (against AISLE.delta_loc).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>import math from mlsysim import <em> from mlsysim.core.units import </em> from mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_length, fmt_multiple, check</p>\n<p>AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class ExtrinsicErrorBudget: \"\"\"Lever-arm errors from angular mount errors, set against the aisle clearances.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>theta_frame = Scenarios.WarehouseLongAisleMountError.angle_error  # category: illustrative (uncalibrated mount error) d_frame = Scenarios.WarehouseLongAisleMountError.distance  # category: illustrative (detection range) theta_ext = Scenarios.WarehouseLidarExtrinsicShift.angle_error  # category: illustrative (extrinsic shift after shock) z_ext = Scenarios.WarehouseLidarExtrinsicShift.distance  # category: illustrative (target range) side_clearance = AISLE.side_clearance  # category: illustrative delta_loc = AISLE.delta_loc  # category: illustrative</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>dy_frame = Scenarios.WarehouseLongAisleMountError.result.angle_lateral.to(meter) dy_over_side = Scenarios.WarehouseLongAisleMountError.result.angle_lateral_share dx_ext = Scenarios.WarehouseLidarExtrinsicShift.result.angle_lateral.to(millimeter) dx_over_loc = Scenarios.WarehouseLidarExtrinsicShift.result.angle_lateral_share</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(dy_frame.magnitude - 0.2618) &lt; 0.0005, f\"Unexpected 1-degree lever-arm error: {dy_frame}\") check(dy_frame &gt; side_clearance, \"Prose says the 1-degree error exceeds the side clearance\") check(abs(dx_ext.magnitude - 50.0) &lt; 0.05, f\"Unexpected extrinsic-shift error: {dx_ext}\") check(abs(dx_over_loc - 1.0) &lt; 1e-4, \"Prose says the extrinsic shift spends all of delta_loc\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">margin_clearance_slip_ladder.svg fig-alt hand-types 262 mm, 50 mm and 150 mm.</h1>\n<p>check(round(dy_frame.to(millimeter).magnitude) == 262, f\"Slip-ladder mount rung drifted: {dy_frame}\") check( abs(delta_loc.to(millimeter).magnitude - 50.0) &lt; 1e-9 and abs(side_clearance.to(millimeter).magnitude - 150.0) &lt; 1e-9, \"Slip-ladder reference lines drifted\", )</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>theta_frame_str = fmt(theta_frame.to(degree).magnitude, precision=0) d_frame_str = fmt_length(d_frame, unit=meter, precision=0) dy_frame_str = fmt_length(dy_frame, unit=meter, precision=2) dy_over_side_mult_str = fmt_multiple(dy_over_side, precision=1) side_clearance_str = fmt_length(side_clearance.to(meter), unit=meter, precision=2) theta_ext_str = fmt(theta_ext.magnitude, precision=3) z_ext_str = fmt_length(z_ext, unit=meter, precision=0) dx_ext_str = fmt_length(dx_ext, unit=millimeter, precision=0) delta_loc_str = fmt_length(delta_loc.to(millimeter), unit=millimeter, precision=0) ]```</p>\n<p>The second mandatory property is the <strong>reference frame</strong> identifier $\\SensorReferenceFrame$. Every spatial measurement is natively resolved in the local coordinate system of the physical transducer. A camera measures bearing angles relative to its optical center, whereas a radar measures range and Doppler velocity relative to its antenna array. Downstream trajectory planners and occupancy maps, by contrast, operate in a chassis body frame $\\BodyReferenceFrame$ or an inertial navigation frame $\\WorldReferenceFrame$. Transforming an observation from $\\SensorReferenceFrame$ to $\\BodyReferenceFrame$ requires applying a rigid-body spatial transformation consisting of a rotation matrix and a translation vector that define the sensor mounting extrinsics, the calibrated location and orientation at which the sensor is bolted to the machine. Coordinate frames nest hierarchically from the physical operating environment down to the transducer: $\\EarthReferenceFrame \\to \\MapReferenceFrame \\to \\OdometryReferenceFrame \\to \\BodyReferenceFrame \\to \\SensorReferenceFrame$, where every link in this tree introduces its own transform and uncertainty (@fig-08-coordinate-frame-tree).</p>\n<p>When perception outputs omit the frame identifier or rely on nominal CAD dimensions rather than calibrated physical extrinsics, small angular offsets create large spatial errors that scale with distance. The same uncalibrated yaw error of $\\ExtrinsicMisalignmentAngle =$ <code>[ExtrinsicErrorBudget.theta_frame_str]</code>° that costs <code>[CaptureTimingScale.dy_per_deg_str]</code> at the <code>[CaptureTimingScale.z_str]</code> rack end grows linearly as the planner looks farther down the aisle. For an object at an illustrative range $\\TargetRange =$ <code>[ExtrinsicErrorBudget.d_frame_str]</code> down a long aisle, it induces a transverse position error $\\ExtrinsicLateralError \\approx \\TargetRange \\sin\\ExtrinsicMisalignmentAngle$ of <code>[ExtrinsicErrorBudget.dy_frame_str]</code>. This lateral offset is approximately <code>[ExtrinsicErrorBudget.dy_over_side_mult_str]</code> the <code>[ExtrinsicErrorBudget.side_clearance_str]</code> side clearance that the base maintains from the warehouse racks, causing the planner to either steer into the racking or falsely reject an open aisle as obstructed.</p>\n<p>The third mandatory property is an <strong>uncertainty representation</strong>, such as a measurement covariance $\\MeasurementCovariance$ paired with a validated tail bound or hard error envelope when physical clearance depends on it. Environmental degradations such as low illumination, atmospheric scattering, specular reflections, and out-of-distribution neural network errors distort the residual distribution far beyond Gaussian assumptions. While a covariance matrix characterizes second moments, it does not guarantee a conservative bounding volume for the true physical state. Downstream state estimators and policy heads rely on $\\MeasurementCovariance$ to weight incoming sensory updates against dynamic motion predictions. If perception reports an artificially optimistic covariance, the estimator over-weights spurious measurements and commands aggressive, erratic actuator responses. Robust physical safety decisions must therefore account explicitly for systematic bias, missed detections, and sensor operational limits alongside the reported covariance.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h3\">Calibrating the coordinate chain</h3>\n<p>Even when each sensor frame is named, an observation measured at the wrist camera still cannot guide the arm or base until the runtime chains every joint and mount transform back to the world at the exact capture instant ${\\CaptureTimestamp}_0$. A coordinate frame transform maps a point expressed relative to one origin into the coordinate system of another: its rotational part reorients the coordinate axes, while its translational part accounts for the spatial offset between origins. Homogeneous matrix representations package both operations so that camera-to-wrist, wrist-to-base, and base-to-world transforms can be composed at ${\\CaptureTimestamp}_0$. The notation below writes the destination as a lower index and the source as an upper index, expressing the same mapping direction as the two-subscript convention used for LiDAR de-skewing.</p>\n<p>A robot's spatial coordinate system forms a <strong>directed acyclic graph (DAG)</strong> of transforms $\\TransformGraph = (\\TransformGraphVertexSet, \\TransformGraphEdgeSet)$, where vertices $\\TransformGraphVertexSet = \\{\\TransformGraphFrame_i\\}$ represent physical coordinate frames and directed edges $\\TransformGraphEdgeSet$ represent time-varying rigid-body transforms $\\Pose_{ij}(\\TimeInstant) \\in \\text{SE}(3)$. In practice, systems constrain this DAG to a directed tree where each non-root frame has exactly one parent, ensuring a unique path between any two frames. When unprojecting an optical observation $\\CameraFramePoint({\\CaptureTimestamp}_0)$ into world coordinates, the runtime composes the transforms along that tree: $$\\WorldFramePoint({\\CaptureTimestamp}_0) = \\WorldMapTransform \\cdot \\MapOdometryTransform({\\CaptureTimestamp}_0) \\cdot \\OdometryBaseTransform({\\CaptureTimestamp}_0) \\cdot \\BaseFlangeTransform({\\CaptureTimestamp}_0) \\cdot \\FlangeCameraTransform \\cdot \\CameraFramePoint({\\CaptureTimestamp}_0)$$ The physical mounting location of the sensor on the kinematic chain dictates whether those transforms stay fixed on the body or update with the arm's joints across this DAG. Two canonical mounting topologies govern how the extrinsic calibration is solved.</p>\n<p>In an <strong>eye-in-hand</strong> configuration, the camera or depth sensor is bolted directly to the manipulator's moving tool flange ($\\FlangeReferenceFrame$). Moving any preceding joint alters the sensor's optical center relative to the world, requiring continuous forward kinematics updates. Solving for the rigid extrinsic transform $\\EyeInHandUnknownTransform = \\FlangeCameraTransform \\in \\text{SE}(3)$ requires executing robot motions across $\\CalibrationRotationAxisCount \\ge 2$ non-parallel rotational axes while observing a stationary fiducial target, such as a ChArUco grid. The running machine's wrist camera is mounted this way, which makes this the calibration its grasps depend on. Because the fiducial target stays fixed in the base frame across both poses, the composed base-to-target transform $\\EarlierBaseFlangeTransform \\EyeInHandUnknownTransform \\EarlierCameraTargetTransform = \\LaterBaseFlangeTransform \\EyeInHandUnknownTransform \\LaterCameraTargetTransform$ is identical at poses $\\CalibrationPoseIndex$ and $\\CalibrationPoseIndex+1$, and left-multiplying by $(\\EarlierBaseFlangeTransform)^{-1}$ while right-multiplying by $(\\LaterCameraTargetTransform)^{-1}$ isolates the relative motions on opposite sides of the unknown mount so that the closed kinematic loop establishes the classic <strong>hand-eye matrix equation</strong>: $$\\EyeInHandFlangeMotion \\EyeInHandUnknownTransform = \\EyeInHandUnknownTransform \\EyeInHandCameraMotion$$ where $\\EyeInHandFlangeMotion = (\\EarlierBaseFlangeTransform)^{-1} \\LaterBaseFlangeTransform \\in \\text{SE}(3)$ is the relative flange displacement reported by the forward kinematics chain, and $\\EyeInHandCameraMotion = \\EarlierCameraTargetTransform (\\LaterCameraTargetTransform)^{-1} \\in \\text{SE}(3)$ is the relative camera displacement extracted from optical target unprojection.</p>\n<p>: <strong>ChArUco Board</strong>: A chessboard whose white squares carry uniquely coded ArUco markers. The chessboard corners give subpixel accuracy and the codes identify which corners are in view, so calibration works from partial views of the board, the usual case for a camera on a moving wrist.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Hand-Eye Calibration</strong>: The problem was posed as $\\Glyph{\\mathbf{AX}}=\\Glyph{\\mathbf{XB}}$ independently by @shiu1989calibration and @tsai1989new, for cameras bolted to robot wrists. The camera's pose relative to the flange is never measured directly, only inferred from paired motions, so any error in $\\EyeInHandUnknownTransform$ enters every grasp the arm plans from that camera, and poorly spread calibration motions leave it weakly determined.</p>\n<p>In an <strong>eye-to-hand</strong> calibration rig, the camera overlooks the workspace from a body-mounted mast or a stationary overhead beam. When calibrating against an overhead camera, the mobile base must remain parked so that the camera-to-base transform remains constant ($\\EyeToHandUnknownTransform = \\BaseCameraTransform \\in \\text{SE}(3)$), while the target is affixed to the robot's moving end-effector ($\\EyeToHandTargetTransform = \\FlangeTargetTransform \\in \\text{SE}(3)$). At each joint configuration, let $\\EyeToHandFlangePose = (\\BaseFlangeTransform)^{-1}$ map base coordinates into the flange frame and $\\EyeToHandCameraPose = (\\CameraTargetTransform)^{-1}$ map camera coordinates into the target frame. These transforms are absolute poses for this rig rather than the relative motions of the eye-in-hand formulation, and both paths map camera coordinates into the flange frame: $$\\EyeToHandFlangePose \\EyeToHandUnknownTransform = \\EyeToHandTargetTransform \\EyeToHandCameraPose$$ If the mobile base subsequently moves relative to the overhead camera, the runtime must update the camera-to-base transform accordingly. For a mast-mounted camera that rides with the chassis, $\\BaseCameraTransform$ remains fixed on the body while $\\OdometryBaseTransform({\\CaptureTimestamp}_0)$ carries the base motion into the world frame.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ THERMAL EXPANSION OF AN ALUMINUM LINK\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: The calibration-drift paragraph of @sec-perception-coordinate-chain.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Size the length change of a warmed aluminum link.\n# │ Show:    23e-6/K x 1 m x 10 K = 0.23 mm.\n# │ How:     dL = alpha * L * dT: Scenarios.AluminumLinkWarming (aluminum&#x27;s\n# │          coefficient from physics.constants).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_int, fmt_length, check\n\nclass LinkThermalExpansion:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Length change of a uniformly warmed aluminum link.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    alpha = Scenarios.AluminumLinkWarming.coefficient  # category: constant (aluminum, CRC Handbook)\n    l_link = Scenarios.AluminumLinkWarming.length  # category: illustrative (link length)\n    d_temp = Scenarios.AluminumLinkWarming.temperature_rise  # category: illustrative (uniform warming)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    dl = Scenarios.AluminumLinkWarming.result.expansion.to(millimeter)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(dl.magnitude - 0.23) &lt; 0.005, f&quot;Unexpected expansion: {dl}&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    alpha_str = fmt_int(round(alpha.to(1 / kelvin).magnitude * 1e6))  # 23.1e-6/K shown as the prose&#x27;s approximate 23\n    l_link_str = fmt_length(l_link, unit=meter, precision=0)\n    d_temp_str = fmt(d_temp.to(kelvin).magnitude, precision=0)\n    dl_str = fmt_length(dl, unit=millimeter, precision=2)\n]```\n\nTreating the nominal rigid transform chain from CAD as an exact physical calibration ignores load and temperature effects. Physical manipulators deflect under payload mass, accumulate lost motion and torsional wind-up in the joints (strain wave gears have near-zero backlash, but their flexsplines still twist under load), and expand with temperature (structural aluminum expands at $\\ThermalExpansionCoefficient \\approx$ `[LinkThermalExpansion.alpha_str]`$\\times 10^{-6}/\\text{K}$). For an illustrative `[LinkThermalExpansion.l_link_str]` aluminum link that warms uniformly by `[LinkThermalExpansion.d_temp_str]`$^\\circ\\text{C}$, the corresponding expansion is `[LinkThermalExpansion.dl_str]`. A motor temperature rise alone does not establish that link-temperature change, as localized thermal gradients across structural members and encoder mountings require direct measurement before updating calibration parameters.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ JOINT-TO-CAMERA TIMESTAMP SKEW AT THE WRIST</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: The joint-kinematics timing paragraph of</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          @sec-perception-coordinate-chain.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Size the wrist-camera error from a joint/camera timestamp skew and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the largest skew a grasp tolerance allows.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    10 mm + 3 mm = 13 mm for a 10 ms skew; 3.8 ms allowed for 5 mm.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     dp = (v + omega r) dt; dt_max = eps_pos / (v + omega r).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import <em> from mlsysim.core.units import </em> from mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_latency, fmt_length, fmt_velocity, check</p>\n<p>class WristTimestampSkew: \"\"\"Wrist-camera position error from joint/camera timestamp skew.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>v_wrist = 1.0 <em> meter / second  # category: illustrative (wrist linear speed) omega_wrist = 2.0 </em> radian / second  # category: illustrative (wrist angular speed) r_cam = 0.15 <em> meter  # category: illustrative (camera lever arm) dt_skew = 10.0 </em> millisecond  # category: illustrative (unmodeled skew) eps_pos = 5.0 * millimeter  # category: illustrative (grasp position tolerance)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>v_eff = (v_wrist + omega_wrist.to(1 / second).magnitude <em> r_cam / second).to(meter / second) dp_lin = (v_wrist </em> dt_skew).to(millimeter) dp_rot = ((v_eff - v_wrist) * dt_skew).to(millimeter) dp = (dp_lin + dp_rot).to(millimeter) dt_max = (eps_pos / v_eff).to(millisecond)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(dp.magnitude - 13.0) &lt; 1e-6, f\"Unexpected skew error: {dp}\") check(abs(dt_max.magnitude - 3.85) &lt; 0.01, f\"Unexpected skew bound: {dt_max}\") check(dt_max &gt; 1.0 * millisecond, \"Prose says millisecond-level alignment suffices\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>v_wrist_str = fmt_velocity(v_wrist, unit=meter / second, precision=0) omega_wrist_str = fmt(omega_wrist.to(radian / second).magnitude, precision=0) r_cam_str = fmt_length(r_cam, unit=meter, precision=2) dt_skew_str = fmt_latency(dt_skew, unit=millisecond, precision=0) dp_lin_str = fmt_length(dp_lin, unit=millimeter, precision=0) dp_rot_str = fmt_length(dp_rot, unit=millimeter, precision=0) dp_str = fmt_length(dp, unit=millimeter, precision=0) eps_pos_str = fmt_length(eps_pos, unit=millimeter, precision=0) dt_max_str = fmt_latency(dt_max, unit=millisecond, precision=1) ]```</p>\n<p>Fusing joint kinematics with optical frames also requires temporal alignment across the transform DAG. Any uncalibrated timestamp skew $\\InterSensorClockSkew = \\CameraSampleTimestamp - \\JointSampleTimestamp$ between the motor communication bus (such as EtherCAT or CANopen) and the camera exposure window evaluates the forward kinematics chain at stale joint angles $\\GeneralizedCoordinates(\\JointSampleTimestamp) \\neq \\GeneralizedCoordinates(\\CameraSampleTimestamp)$, inducing spatial tracking error: $$\\WristSkewDisplacement = \\WristLinearVelocity \\, \\InterSensorClockSkew + (\\WristAngularVelocity \\times \\SensorLeverArm) \\, \\InterSensorClockSkew$$ At an illustrative wrist linear velocity $\\BodySpeed =$ <code>[WristTimestampSkew.v_wrist_str]</code> and angular velocity $\\AngularSpeed =$ <code>[WristTimestampSkew.omega_wrist_str]</code> rad/s with a camera lever arm $\\SensorLeverArmLength =$ <code>[WristTimestampSkew.r_cam_str]</code>, an unmodeled <code>[WristTimestampSkew.dt_skew_str]</code> skew displaces the observation by <code>[WristTimestampSkew.dp_lin_str]</code> plus <code>[WristTimestampSkew.dp_rot_str]</code>, or <code>[WristTimestampSkew.dp_str]</code>. This error causes the arm to miss a grasp whose position tolerance is <code>[WristTimestampSkew.eps_pos_str]</code>. Holding that displacement within the tolerance gives the timing requirement $\\InterSensorClockSkew \\le \\PositionalTaskTolerance/(\\BodySpeed + \\AngularSpeed \\SensorLeverArmLength)$, which evaluates to <code>[WristTimestampSkew.dt_max_str]</code>. Millisecond-level alignment therefore suffices, whereas microsecond synchronization is unnecessary. Systems typically meet this requirement by timestamping both streams against a single shared clock (a fieldbus distributed clock or a PTP master) and interpolating the high-rate joint stream at the camera's exposure midpoint.</p>\n<p>Once those joint and sensor transforms are synchronized in time, the <a href=\"https://reps.openrobotics.org/rep-0105/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">REP 105 frame convention</a> names these frames and separates a locally continuous <code>odom</code> pose from a globally corrected <code>map</code> pose. On the mobile manipulator, $\\BodyReferenceFrame$ is the base's <code>base_link</code>, tracked through the odometric frame <code>odom</code>, and the arm's serial chain maps its mounting flange <code>tool0</code> through the joint angles back to <code>base_link</code>. @Tbl-08-rep105-tree lists the frame roles and the behavioral rules each consumer follows. A local controller consumes a continuous estimate and rejects unexpected transform jumps, while runtime checks verify transform timestamps and estimator uncertainties before execution.</p>\n<p>: <strong>ROS Enhancement Proposal (REP)</strong>: The Robot Operating System records its community conventions as numbered REPs, a process copied from Python's enhancement proposals. REP 105 fixes the meaning of <code>map</code>, <code>odom</code>, and <code>base_link</code> and the continuity each must keep [@meeussen2010rep105], so a frame name written into a record denotes the same physical frame for every consumer that reads it.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Frame</th><th>Parent in this example</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Consumer rule</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>earth</code></td><td>root</td><td>Optional geodetic datum</td><td>Verify datum before global routing</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>map</code></td><td><code>earth</code> if present</td><td>Global localization; corrections may jump</td><td>Keep relocalization jumps out of local feedback</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>odom</code></td><td><code>map</code></td><td>Locally continuous pose estimate; may drift</td><td>Track motion locally; monitor uncertainty and discontinuities</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>base_link</code></td><td><code>odom</code></td><td>Body-fixed origin</td><td>Body pose still inherits odometry uncertainty</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>sensor_link</code></td><td><code>base_link</code></td><td>Calibrated sensor mounting frame</td><td>Apply time-valid extrinsics and their uncertainty</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>REP 105 frame roles for a mobile robot</strong>: A compact example of global, local, body, and sensor frames. REP 105 requires <code>odom</code>-relative pose to remain continuous but does not prescribe $\\Glyph{C}^1$ smoothness, a covariance growth rate, or fixed accuracy; these require estimator-specific evidence. {#tbl-08-rep105-tree tbl-colwidths=\"[14,20,32,34]\"}</p>\n<p>Splitting consumers across the tree keeps a relocalization jump out of the actuators (@fig-08-coordinate-frame-tree). Global planners absorb the discontinuous $\\MapPoseCovariance$ corrections at relocalization, while high-rate local tracking loops consume only the continuous <code>odom</code> $\\to$ <code>base_link</code> branch, so a global correction never reaches the actuators as an abrupt tracking step. Mounting uncertainty still compounds with dynamic pose uncertainty across every intermediate link. Under a first-order propagation model with independent error sources: $$\\WorldPointCovariance = \\MapCovarianceJacobian\\MapPoseCovariance\\MapCovarianceJacobian^\\top + \\OdometryCovarianceJacobian\\OdometryPoseCovariance\\OdometryCovarianceJacobian^\\top + \\ExtrinsicCovarianceJacobian\\ExtrinsicCalibrationCovariance\\ExtrinsicCovarianceJacobian^\\top$$ where the Jacobians $\\FunctionJacobian$ project each frame's spatial covariance into world coordinates. When frame errors correlate, the propagation must include their cross-covariance terms. A downstream consumer requires the frame identifier, capture epoch, and compounded uncertainty envelope, rather than a transform selected merely by its latest publication timestamp.</p>\n<p>↪ How these sensor-to-body transforms go stale as the joints and base move is developed in @sec-memory-reference-frames.</p>\n<p>Pass a perception claim with its capture time and clock domain, coordinate frame, and qualified uncertainty. Refuse a claim when any of those cannot be checked for the proposed motion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h2\">Bounding Observation Age</h2>\n<p>Even when a rack-end observation is dated at capture and placed in a calibrated coordinate frame, the scene it describes belongs to the past. Delivering that measurement across an embodied runtime adds acquisition, bus transport, queueing, and neural inference delay while the robot continues to move. Any delivery deadline for perception must therefore track the full physical interval from energy integration at the detector through dispatch, bounding the observation's age before downstream actuators act.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Observation age</strong></em>} ($\\DispatchObservationAge$), also called spatial information age, is the physical time elapsed between the exposure midpoint at the transducer and the moment perception dispatches the resulting observation. It is the capture-to-dispatch part of the measurement age that @sec-body-measurement-freshness counts from transduction. The observation keeps aging after dispatch, and the age a consumer computes on its own clock when it admits or reuses the observation is its <strong>current evidence age</strong>, which equals $\\DispatchObservationAge$ plus the time elapsed since dispatch.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: Because the robot continues moving while the compute pipeline runs, observation age converts directly into physical displacement (@eq-percep-spatial-displacement) and expands the stopping budget through the pre-brake delay $\\PrebrakeDelay$. On the warehouse mobile manipulator, the navigation-camera observation is already <code>[RackEndOpeningAge.t_age_str]</code> old at dispatch, which is <code>[RackEndOpeningAge.dx_str]</code> of blind travel at the <code>[RackEndOpeningAge.v_str]</code> drive limit before any downstream consumer has inspected the frame.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Conventional benchmark latency measures only the software execution time of a forward pass. In contrast, observation age spans the entire physical journey from photon arrival to dispatch: the exposure midpoint, sensor rolling readout, serialized bus transport, direct memory access (DMA) transfers, driver queueing, image signal processor (ISP) conversions, model inference, and inter-process dispatch. Scheduling delay after dispatch belongs to the current evidence age, not to $\\DispatchObservationAge$.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: Systems engineers frequently confuse a frame's arrival timestamp in userspace memory ($\\ArrivalTimestamp$) with its actual physical observation time (${\\CaptureTimestamp}_0$). On the navigation camera, the arrival timestamp overlooks <code>[ArrivalStampOffset.tau_recv_str]</code> of front-end exposure, readout, and transport. At the maximum drive limit, this unmeasured interval conceals <code>[ArrivalStampOffset.dx_recv_str]</code> of physical travel, saddling downstream controllers with an unmodeled phase lag.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>When evaluated along the machine's trajectory, observation age integrates directly into spatial displacement (@eq-percep-spatial-displacement): $$\\ObservationAgeDisplacement = \\int_{{\\CaptureTimestamp}_0}^{{\\CaptureTimestamp}<em>0+\\DispatchObservationAge} \\Velocity(\\TimeInstant)\\,d\\TimeInstant$$ {#eq-percep-spatial-displacement} Travel is one of three error sources that perception must bound. Adding this motion-induced displacement to first-order stereo triangulation noise and small-angle extrinsic error gives a single error scale for the pipeline (@eq-percep-unified-uncertainty-envelope): $$\\PerceptionErrorScale = \\underbrace{\\int</em>{{\\CaptureTimestamp}_0}^{{\\CaptureTimestamp}<em>0+\\DispatchObservationAge} \\|\\Velocity(\\TimeInstant)\\|\\,d\\TimeInstant}</em>{\\text{path-length travel}} + \\underbrace{\\frac{\\TargetRange^2}{\\FocalLength \\StereoBaseline}\\DisparityStandardDeviation}<em>{\\text{stereo depth standard deviation}} + \\underbrace{\\TargetRange |\\ExtrinsicMisalignmentAngle|}</em>{\\text{small-angle mounting error scale}}$$ {#eq-percep-unified-uncertainty-envelope} The first term integrates instantaneous speed to compute path length, providing an upper bound on net translation during the perception delay that the navigation camera's latency waterfall sizes. The second term characterizes depth uncertainty for a calibrated stereo camera with baseline $\\StereoBaseline$, focal length $\\FocalLength$, operating range $\\TargetRange$, and disparity standard deviation $\\DisparityStandardDeviation$. @Sec-perception-error-clearance derives and evaluates this term on an illustrative rig as a standard-deviation scale rather than an absolute bound. The third term models the lever-arm displacement produced by a small extrinsic angular misalignment $\\ExtrinsicMisalignmentAngle$ over target distance $\\TargetRange$, the error @sec-perception-handoff already sized at the rack end. The sum is therefore an order-of-magnitude scale, not a safety envelope. It adds magnitudes that point in different directions and leaves out calibration bias and the heavy tail of real residuals, which is why the clearance a consumer holds rests instead on a measured residual quantile (@eq-percep-clearance-inset).</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h3\">The latency waterfall</h3>\n<p>While the navigation camera integrates photons, clocks out pixel rows, transfers buffers across the bus, and runs neural inference kernels, the mobile base keeps moving at velocity $\\SpeedAtDetection$, turning every millisecond of pipeline delay into blind travel: $$\\AgeBlindTravel = \\SpeedAtDetection \\cdot \\DispatchObservationAge$$ On the navigation camera, the observation age $\\DispatchObservationAge$ accumulates stage by stage from the exposure midpoint to the moment perception publishes its processed measurement. When hardware stages execute sequentially, the total age expands as a <strong>latency waterfall</strong>; if adjacent stages overlap in hardware or stream through pipelined ring buffers, their combined duration replaces the naive sum of individual stage times: $$\\DispatchObservationAge = \\frac{\\ExposureDuration}{2} + \\FrameReadoutTime + \\SensorLinkTransportTime + \\DmaQueueTime + \\IspProcessingTime + \\VisionBackboneTime + \\PerceptionDispatchTime$$ {#eq-percep-latency-waterfall}</p>\n<p>A complete stopping margin must also count the downstream response: expiry of the chunk lease sized in @sec-nervous-lease-sizing, the control tick, fieldbus transmission, and brake onset. Summing these terms yields the partial pre-brake delay $\\PrebrakeDelay = \\DispatchObservationAge + \\ChunkLease + \\PermissionLoopPeriod + \\CommandTransportTime + \\ActuatorResponseTime$, which governs the corresponding subtotal of blind travel: $$\\BlindTravelDistance = \\SpeedAtDetection \\cdot \\PrebrakeDelay$$ At constant operating velocity, every millisecond of unmodeled latency drains physical clearance linearly. Because the permission path runs concurrently on its own MCU, the safety calculation must reflect that dedicated architecture rather than assuming that every stalled neural forward pass postpones emergency braking. Notebook \\ref{nbk-perception-sensor-transduction-latency-spatial-information-age} steps through each hardware and software stage of this waterfall for the warehouse mobile manipulator, converting elapsed time into traveled distance.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ NAVIGATION-CAMERA INFORMATION AGE AND STOPPING-BUDGET ROW 5\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: \\ref{nbk-perception-sensor-transduction-latency-spatial-information-age},\n# │          @tbl-08-latency-budget, and the stopping-budget paragraph of\n# │          @sec-perception-latency-waterfall.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Sum the warehouse mobile manipulator&#x27;s navigation-camera latency\n# │          waterfall into t_age and add its term to the stopping budget.\n# │ Show:    Stage increments and cumulative ages; t_age 51.6 ms; tau_delay\n# │          133.6 ms; row 5 v*t_age 77.4 mm at 1.5 m/s; running total\n# │          835.5 mm -&gt; 912.9 mm; 187.1 mm of the 1.10 m clear distance left.\n# │ How:     Stage ages from Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraAge; the total before\n# │          row 5 is Scenarios.WarehouseChunkLease&#x27;s running total (braking,\n# │          delta_loc, delta_margin, onset, lease path) and the total after it\n# │          is Scenarios.WarehouseBaseStopFullDelay. The C2 suffix and\n# │          tracking inset join later rows, not this one.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import (\n    fmt_latency,\n    fmt_length,\n    fmt_velocity,\n    fmt_time,\n    fmt_display_math,\n    check,\n)\n\nRM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass SensorInformationAge:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Navigation-camera latency waterfall, t_age, and the observation-age row of the stopping budget.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    camera = RM.nav_camera  # category: datasheet (registry: Sony IMX477 navigation camera)\n    t_exp = camera.exposure_time.to(millisecond)  # category: chosen (configured exposure)\n    t_readout = camera.readout_time.to(millisecond)  # category: datasheet\n    t_transport = AISLE.t_transport.to(millisecond)  # category: illustrative\n    t_dma = AISLE.t_dma.to(millisecond)  # category: illustrative\n    t_isp = AISLE.t_isp.to(millisecond)  # category: illustrative\n    t_backbone = AISLE.t_backbone.to(millisecond)  # category: illustrative\n    t_ipc = AISLE.t_ipc.to(millisecond)  # category: illustrative\n    t_lease = AISLE.t_lease.to(millisecond)  # category: chosen\n    t_tick = AISLE.t_tick.to(millisecond)  # category: chosen\n    t_bus = AISLE.t_bus.to(millisecond)  # category: illustrative\n    t_act = AISLE.t_brake_onset.to(millisecond)  # category: illustrative\n    v = RM.base.max_velocity.to(meter / second)  # category: illustrative (drive limit)\n    a_brake = AISLE.a_brake  # category: illustrative\n    delta_loc = AISLE.delta_loc  # category: illustrative\n    delta_margin = AISLE.delta_margin  # category: chosen\n    d_clear = AISLE.d_clear  # category: illustrative\n    f_frame = camera.frame_rate  # category: datasheet\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    _age = Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraAge.result\n    _stop = Scenarios.WarehouseBaseStopFullDelay.result\n    t_exp_half = _age.trigger_offset.to(millisecond)\n    t_frame = _age.frame_period.to(millisecond)\n    age_after_exposure, age_after_readout, age_after_transport, age_after_dma, age_after_isp, age_after_backbone, tau_age = (\n        st[2] for st in _age.stages\n    )\n\n    tau_delay = AISLE.tau_delay.to(millisecond)\n    dx = _age.age_travel.to(millimeter)\n    row_age = dx\n    total_before = Scenarios.WarehouseChunkLease.result.running_total.to(millimeter)\n    total_after = _stop.required_distance.to(millimeter)\n    remaining = _stop.spare.to(millimeter)\n    dx_frame = _age.frame_travel.to(millimeter)  # checkpoint: one dropped frame of extra age\n    frame_share = (dx_frame / remaining).to(dimensionless).magnitude\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(tau_age.magnitude - AISLE.perception_age.to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Waterfall disagrees with AISLE.perception_age: {tau_age}&quot;)\n    check(abs(tau_age.magnitude - 51.6) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected t_age: {tau_age}&quot;)\n    check(abs(tau_delay.magnitude - AISLE.tau_delay.to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Delay sum disagrees with AISLE.tau_delay: {tau_delay}&quot;)\n    check(abs(tau_delay.magnitude - 133.6) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected tau_delay: {tau_delay}&quot;)\n    check(abs(v.magnitude - 1.5) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Unexpected drive limit: {v}&quot;)\n    check(abs(row_age.magnitude - 77.4) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected observation-age row: {row_age}&quot;)\n    check(abs(total_before.magnitude - 835.5) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected running total before row 5: {total_before}&quot;)\n    check(abs(total_after.magnitude - 912.9) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected running total after row 5: {total_after}&quot;)\n    check(abs((total_after - total_before - row_age).magnitude) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;Row 5 does not close the running total&quot;)\n    check(Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraAge.speed == v and Scenarios.WarehouseBaseStopFullDelay.speed == v, &quot;Every term is priced at the drive limit&quot;)\n    check(abs(remaining.magnitude - 187.1) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected remaining clear distance: {remaining}&quot;)\n    check(abs(dx_frame.magnitude - 25.0) &lt; 0.05 and 0.13 &lt; frame_share &lt; 0.14, f&quot;Checkpoint answer drifted: {dx_frame}, {frame_share}&quot;)\n    check(\n        t_backbone == max(t_exp_half, t_readout, t_transport, t_dma, t_isp, t_backbone, t_ipc),\n        &quot;Notebook says the backbone is the largest single stage&quot;,\n    )\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    t_exp_str = fmt_latency(t_exp, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_exp_half_str = fmt_latency(t_exp_half, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_readout_str = fmt_latency(t_readout, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    t_transport_str = fmt_latency(t_transport, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    t_dma_str = fmt_latency(t_dma, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    t_isp_str = fmt_latency(t_isp, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    t_backbone_str = fmt_latency(t_backbone, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_ipc_str = fmt_latency(t_ipc, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    tau_age_str = fmt_latency(tau_age, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    tau_age_s_str = fmt_time(tau_age.to(second), &#x27;second&#x27;, precision=4)\n    age_after_exposure_str = fmt_latency(age_after_exposure, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    age_after_readout_str = fmt_latency(age_after_readout, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    age_after_transport_str = fmt_latency(age_after_transport, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    age_after_dma_str = fmt_latency(age_after_dma, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    age_after_isp_str = fmt_latency(age_after_isp, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    age_after_backbone_str = fmt_latency(age_after_backbone, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n\n    t_lease_str = fmt_latency(t_lease, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_tick_str = fmt_latency(t_tick, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_bus_str = fmt_latency(t_bus, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_act_str = fmt_latency(t_act, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    tau_delay_str = fmt_latency(tau_delay, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n\n    v_str = fmt_velocity(v, unit=meter / second, precision=1)\n    dx_m_str = fmt_length(dx.to(meter), unit=meter, precision=4)\n    dx_mm_str = fmt_length(dx, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    row_age_str = fmt_length(row_age, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    total_before_str = fmt_length(total_before, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    total_after_str = fmt_length(total_after, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    remaining_str = fmt_length(remaining, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    d_clear_str = fmt_length(d_clear.to(meter), unit=meter, precision=2)\n    t_frame_str = fmt_latency(t_frame, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n\n    tau_age_display_math = fmt_display_math(\n        rf&quot;t_{{\\text{{age}}}} = {t_exp_half_str} + {t_readout_str} + {t_transport_str} + {t_dma_str} + {t_isp_str} + {t_backbone_str} + {t_ipc_str} = {tau_age_str}&quot;\n    )\n    dx_display_math = fmt_display_math(rf&quot;\\Delta x = ({v_str}) \\times ({tau_age_s_str}) = {dx_m_str} = {dx_mm_str}&quot;)\n    t_rest_str = fmt_latency((t_lease + t_tick + t_bus + t_act).to(millisecond), unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    tau_delay_display_math = fmt_display_math(\n        rf&quot;\\tau_{{\\text{{delay}}}} = \\underbrace{{t_{{\\text{{age}}}}}}_{{{tau_age_str}}} + \\underbrace{{T_{{\\text{{lease}}}} + T_{{\\text{{tick}}}} + T_{{\\text{{bus}}}} + T_{{\\text{{act}}}}}}_{{{t_rest_str}}} = {tau_delay_str}&quot;\n    )\n    d_stop_display_math = fmt_display_math(\n        rf&quot;d_{{\\text{{stop}}}} = \\underbrace{{d_{{\\text{{base}}}} + d_{{\\text{{lease}}}}}}_{{{total_before_str}}} + \\underbrace{{v \\cdot t_{{\\text{{age}}}}}}_{{{row_age_str}}} = {total_after_str}&quot;\n    )\n]```\n\n::: {#nbk-perception-sensor-transduction-latency-spatial-information-age .callout-notebook title=&quot;Travel before the frame is read&quot;}\n\n**Problem**: The mobile manipulator drives toward a rack end at its drive limit when a person steps out. How old is the navigation camera&#x27;s observation of that person when perception dispatches it, and how far has the base traveled by then?\n\n**Variables**:\n\n* Exposure $\\ExposureDuration =$ `[SensorInformationAge.t_exp_str]` (Chosen low-light setting), so the midpoint lag is $\\ExposureDuration/2 =$ `[SensorInformationAge.t_exp_half_str]`; rolling readout $\\FrameReadoutTime =$ `[SensorInformationAge.t_readout_str]` (Datasheet).\n* Residual MIPI CSI-2 transport $\\SensorLinkTransportTime =$ `[SensorInformationAge.t_transport_str]` (Illustrative). The [Sony IMX477 flyer](https://www.sony-semicon.com/files/62/pdf/p-13_IMX477-AACK_Flyer.pdf) specifies a four-lane option up to $2.1\\text{ Gbps/lane}$; the budget assigns only the transport left after streaming readout, not frame size divided by peak lane rate.\n* DMA and driver queue $\\DmaQueueTime =$ `[SensorInformationAge.t_dma_str]`, ISP debayering and rectification $\\IspProcessingTime =$ `[SensorInformationAge.t_isp_str]`, INT8 vision backbone $\\VisionBackboneTime =$ `[SensorInformationAge.t_backbone_str]`, and covariance packaging with zero-copy dispatch $\\PerceptionDispatchTime =$ `[SensorInformationAge.t_ipc_str]` (all Illustrative).\n* Drive limit $\\SpeedAtDetection =$ `[SensorInformationAge.v_str]` (Illustrative).\n\n**Math**:\n\n*Age at dispatch.* The serial stages of @eq-percep-latency-waterfall sum to\n`[SensorInformationAge.tau_age_display_math]`\n\n*Travel.* Holding speed through the age window:\n`[SensorInformationAge.dx_display_math]`\n\n*Sensitivity.* Each further millisecond in any stage adds `[CaptureTimingScale.dx_per_ms_str]` of travel at the drive limit.\n\n**Result**: The observation is `[SensorInformationAge.tau_age_str]` old at dispatch, and the base has covered `[SensorInformationAge.dx_mm_str]`. The backbone is the largest single stage, but exposure and readout alone contribute `[SensorInformationAge.age_after_readout_str]` before any computation starts.\n\n**Systems insight**: The base covers this distance before the perception record exists. Timestamped pose propagation can place the old observation correctly in the current frame, but no later stage returns the distance already traveled, so $\\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\DispatchObservationAge$ enters the stopping budget as a term of its own.\n\n:::\n\nThe seven stages of @tbl-08-latency-budget sum to the observation age at dispatch, $\\DispatchObservationAge =$ `[SensorInformationAge.tau_age_str]`. Each stage value is a budgeted allocation, the most that stage may take under load, rather than a typical measurement. The sum is therefore the age the rest of the machine plans against, and the last column of the table names the measurement that must confirm it. The stopping budget then adds the active lease and response path of @sec-nervous-lease-sizing:\n`[SensorInformationAge.tau_delay_display_math]`\nThis is the pre-brake delay that @sec-boundary-kinematic-roofline and the event timing table of @sec-nervous carried with $\\DispatchObservationAge$ entered as a given; its first term is now derived rather than assumed.\n\nThis subtotal is a **partial delay budget** rather than an end-to-end capture-to-response bound. In the timing breakdown of @tbl-04-event-timing, perception governs the first interval from capture to dispatch, while actuator mechanics govern the third interval of physical braking. The observation continues to age across the intermediate second interval while the proposer completes inference, transmits the candidate trajectory, and awaits admission from the permission path. Let $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ denote this dispatch-to-admission interval, encompassing model planning and inter-process communication overheads. Because an active lease takes effect only upon candidate admission, the total delay under a stalled-proposer fault equals the displayed subtotal plus $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$. The transport-budget calculation in @sec-placement-dispatch-admission allocates a budget for $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ that awaits empirical validation on hardware. That allocation includes a full inference after dispatch, and part of that inference overlaps the vision backbone already counted in $\\DispatchObservationAge$. Adding the two therefore counts the overlap twice and overstates the delay, which errs on the safe side; timestamping both intervals on the loaded machine removes the overlap.\n\nLaid on one clock, the partial budget shows where the unbounded interval sits (@fig-08-capture-to-brake-strip). Everything before dispatch at `[SensorInformationAge.tau_age_str]` belongs to perception, while everything after it belongs to the response path that reaches brake onset at `[SensorInformationAge.tau_delay_str]`. The dispatch-to-admission interval $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ enters at the dispatch line, so whatever value it takes on the loaded machine shifts every later event to the right.\n\n::: {#fig-08-capture-to-brake-strip fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Capture-to-brake timing strip**: Time runs from the exposure midpoint of the frame that first shows the hazard. The camera and host lanes carry the navigation camera&#x27;s serial stages, with link, DMA, and ISP drawn as one segment, to dispatch at `[SensorInformationAge.tau_age_str]`; the response lane adds the lease, tick, bus, and actuator onset of a stalled proposer to reach `[SensorInformationAge.tau_delay_str]`. The row beneath reads the same axis as base travel at the `[SensorInformationAge.v_str]` drive limit. The dashed line at dispatch marks where the dispatch-to-admission interval $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ enters; this chapter does not bound it, so every later event shifts right by its measured value.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Horizontal timing strip with three lanes labeled camera, host, and response over a time axis in milliseconds. The camera lane shows a solid exposure bar centered on the capture line and a readout bar after it. The host lane shows a combined link, DMA, and ISP bar, a longer backbone bar, and a thin IPC bar ending at a dispatch line. A dashed line at dispatch is labeled as the point where unbounded dispatch-to-admission time enters. The response lane starts at dispatch with a long lease bar, a thin tick and bus bar, and an onset bar ending at a brake-onset line. A bracket over the strip spans capture to brake onset. A travel row beneath marks millimeters of base travel and the travel before and after dispatch.&quot;}\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ CAPTURE-TO-BRAKE TIMING STRIP (FIGURE)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @fig-08-capture-to-brake-strip in @sec-perception-latency-waterfall.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Put the navigation camera's dispatch age and the stalled-proposer</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          response path on one clock, with the travel each consumes.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    exposure and readout; link, DMA, ISP, backbone, IPC to t_age; the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          point where the unbounded Delta_da enters; lease, tick, bus, and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          onset to tau_delay; travel at the drive limit.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     viz.perception_age_strip (the shared timing-strip family) reads</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          every duration from AISLE; the guards tie its drawn events to</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          SensorInformationAge.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import viz from mlsysim.fmt import check import matplotlib.pyplot as plt from mlsysim.core.units import millimeter</p>\n<p>SIA = SensorInformationAge</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>v_strip = SIA.v  # category: illustrative (drive limit)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>strip_08 = viz.perception_age_strip(AISLE, v_strip) t_dispatch_drawn = next(e.t for e in strip_08.events if e.label.startswith(\"dispatch\")) t_onset_drawn = next(e.t for e in strip_08.events if e.label == \"brake onset\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(t_dispatch_drawn - SIA.tau_age.to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, \"The strip's dispatch must equal t_age.\") check(abs(t_onset_drawn - SIA.tau_delay.to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, \"The strip's brake onset must equal tau_delay.\") check( abs(strip_08.speed_mm_per_unit * t_dispatch_drawn - SIA.row_age.to(millimeter).magnitude) &lt; 1e-6, \"The travel to dispatch must equal the observation-age row.\", )</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. PLOT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>for _s in strip_08.spans:  # at print width the 20 ms onset box takes its label on two lines if _s.label.startswith(\"onset \"): _s.label = _s.label.replace(\"onset \", \"onset\\n\") strip_08.figsize = (6.4, 5.4)  # print scale: the strip's default canvas is 10 in wide fig, ax, axd = viz.plot_timing_strip(strip_08) plt.show() ]```</p>\n<p>Because the permission path validates geometric safety without reprocessing raw images, it cannot retroactively compress this perception pipeline delay; the age reaches it strictly through the proposal's stamped evidence epoch. At the <code>[SensorInformationAge.v_str]</code> drive limit, the observation-age term $\\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\DispatchObservationAge$ contributes <code>[SensorInformationAge.row_age_str]</code> of blind displacement. That travel is the fifth row of the stopping budget. The first three rows are the Body's terms from @eq-body-stopping-distance, braking distance $\\SpeedAtDetection^2/2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$, the overhead $\\LocalizationBound + \\ProtectiveClearanceMargin$, and brake-onset travel $\\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\ActuatorResponseTime$, written together below as $\\BaseStoppingDistance$. The fourth row is the lease-path travel $\\LeasePathTravel = \\SpeedAtDetection\\,(\\ChunkLease + \\PermissionLoopPeriod + \\CommandTransportTime)$ of @sec-nervous-lease-sizing. Adding the fifth row gives <code>[SensorInformationAge.d_stop_display_math]</code> This additional term raises the partial stopping distance from <code>[SensorInformationAge.total_before_str]</code> to <code>[SensorInformationAge.total_after_str]</code>. Although <code>[SensorInformationAge.remaining_str]</code> remains within the nominal <code>[SensorInformationAge.d_clear_str]</code> rack-end clearance, that remainder cannot be claimed as safety buffer. It must still absorb the travel during the dispatch-to-admission interval $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$, the extra distance of a jerk-limited rather than instantaneous brake application (@sec-planning-base-stopping-budget), and the tracking inset of @sec-enforcement-stopping-envelopes. This analysis sizes the perception subsystem's contribution to the stopping budget and clarifies the empirical measurements required before an integrated motion claim can be certified; the final column of @tbl-08-latency-budget names that measurement stage by stage.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Increment</th><th>Serial age after stage</th><th>What to verify</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Exposure midpoint</td><td><code>[SensorInformationAge.t_exp_half_str]</code></td><td><code>[SensorInformationAge.age_after_exposure_str]</code></td><td>Exposure and row timestamp convention</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rolling readout</td><td><code>[SensorInformationAge.t_readout_str]</code></td><td><code>[SensorInformationAge.age_after_readout_str]</code></td><td>Selected row and overlap with streaming</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Residual transport</td><td><code>[SensorInformationAge.t_transport_str]</code></td><td><code>[SensorInformationAge.age_after_transport_str]</code></td><td>Selected sensor mode and host receiver</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DMA and queue</td><td><code>[SensorInformationAge.t_dma_str]</code></td><td><code>[SensorInformationAge.age_after_dma_str]</code></td><td>Arbitration and queue tail</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ISP</td><td><code>[SensorInformationAge.t_isp_str]</code></td><td><code>[SensorInformationAge.age_after_isp_str]</code></td><td>Mode and competing jobs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vision backbone</td><td><code>[SensorInformationAge.t_backbone_str]</code></td><td><code>[SensorInformationAge.age_after_backbone_str]</code></td><td>Workload, clock, thermal state</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dispatch</td><td><code>[SensorInformationAge.t_ipc_str]</code></td><td><strong><code>[SensorInformationAge.tau_age_str]</code></strong></td><td>Capture-to-dispatch age and queue tail</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Serial observation-age budget</strong>: The navigation camera's budgeted stage increments, assumed nonoverlapping, sum to its observation age at dispatch. A real pipeline can overlap readout, transfer, and processing, so capture-to-dispatch age requires an end-to-end measurement including queueing tails. Later consumers must add their elapsed post-dispatch interval. {#tbl-08-latency-budget tbl-colwidths=\"[24,18,20,38]\"}</p>\n<p>Observation age is paid in travel before any consumer can react, and no later stage returns it.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Observation age</strong>: Can you explain how the interval from the exposure midpoint to dispatch converts into travel that erodes the stopping clearance before a policy can react?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Exposure midpoint vs. arrival timestamp</strong>: Can you explain why dating a frame at DMA completion rather than at its exposure midpoint pairs the observation with the wrong pose?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Pose propagation from capture time</strong>: Can you explain how propagating the machine's pose forward from an observation's capture time places an old observation correctly in the current frame, and why that propagation cannot return the distance already traveled?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Observation age in the stopping budget</strong>: Can you explain why the observation-age term $\\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\DispatchObservationAge$ adds to the stopping distance, and why the permission path, which never reads the image, cannot shorten it?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Dropped-frame estimate</strong>: If a dropped frame leaves a proposal resting on the previous frame, one frame period (<code>[SensorInformationAge.t_frame_str]</code>) older, how much travel does that add at the <code>[SensorInformationAge.v_str]</code> drive limit, and what share of the <code>[SensorInformationAge.remaining_str]</code> remainder does it consume?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h3\">Dating the machine's LiDAR</h3>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ LIDAR AGE AT THE RACK END: STREAMED SECTOR VS FULL SWEEP\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @sec-perception-lidar-age and @tbl-08-lidar-age.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Compare two acquisition/processing allocations with the camera\n# │          allocation; actual evidence ages require one consumer clock.\n# │ Show:    the aisle ahead subtends 61.2 degrees, swept in 8.5 ms; age before\n# │          processing 19.5 ms streamed vs 61.0 ms full sweep (with 10 ms data\n# │          latency and a 1 ms PTP bound); 32.1 ms left for de-skew and\n# │          occupancy extraction within the camera&#x27;s 51.6 ms allocation;\n# │          scan/frame phase, reuse, and downstream waiting remain separate.\n# │ How:     Scenarios.WarehouseRackEndLidarAge (LidarSweepAgeModel over\n# │          RM.lidar.scan_rate, AISLE.aisle_width and AISLE.d_clear, the Ouster\n# │          OS1 Rev7 data latency (&lt; 10 ms) and PTP accuracy (&lt; 1 ms), and\n# │          AISLE.perception_age).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nimport math\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_latency, fmt_length, check\n\nRM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass LidarAge:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Age of the OS1-64&#x27;s rack-end sector under two consumption policies.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    t_sweep = (1 / RM.lidar.scan_rate).to(millisecond)  # category: datasheet (20 Hz mode)\n    aisle_width = AISLE.aisle_width  # category: illustrative\n    d_clear = AISLE.d_clear  # category: illustrative\n    t_data = RM.lidar.nominal_latency.to(millisecond)  # category: datasheet (registry: OS1 Rev7 data latency upper bound)\n    eps_ptp = RM.lidar.time_sync_accuracy.to(millisecond)  # category: datasheet (registry: OS1 Rev7 PTP accuracy upper bound)\n    t_camera = AISLE.perception_age.to(millisecond)  # category: derived\n    v = RM.base.max_velocity.to(meter / second)  # category: illustrative (drive limit)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    _lidar = Scenarios.WarehouseRackEndLidarAge.result\n    sector_deg = _lidar.sector_angle.to(degree).magnitude\n    t_sector = _lidar.sector_time.to(millisecond)\n    age_stream = _lidar.age_streamed.to(millisecond)\n    age_full = _lidar.age_full.to(millisecond)\n    dx_stream = _lidar.travel_streamed.to(millimeter)\n    dx_full = _lidar.travel_full.to(millimeter)\n    slack_stream = _lidar.slack_streamed.to(millisecond)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(sector_deg - 61.2) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected forward sector: {sector_deg}&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_sector.magnitude - 8.5) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected sector time: {t_sector}&quot;)\n    check(abs(age_stream.magnitude - 19.5) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected streamed age: {age_stream}&quot;)\n    check(abs(age_full.magnitude - 61.0) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Unexpected full-sweep age: {age_full}&quot;)\n    check(age_full &gt; t_camera, &quot;The full-sweep preprocessing allocation exceeds the camera allocation&quot;)\n    check(abs(slack_stream.magnitude - 32.1) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected streamed slack: {slack_stream}&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    t_sweep_str = fmt_latency(t_sweep, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    aisle_width_str = fmt_length(aisle_width.to(meter), unit=meter, precision=2)\n    d_clear_str = fmt_length(d_clear.to(meter), unit=meter, precision=2)\n    sector_deg_str = fmt(sector_deg, precision=1)\n    t_sector_str = fmt_latency(t_sector, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    t_data_str = fmt_latency(t_data, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    eps_ptp_str = fmt_latency(eps_ptp, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    age_stream_str = fmt_latency(age_stream, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    age_full_str = fmt_latency(age_full, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    dx_stream_str = fmt_length(dx_stream, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    dx_full_str = fmt_length(dx_full, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    t_camera_str = fmt_latency(t_camera, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    slack_stream_str = fmt_latency(slack_stream, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n]```\n\nWhereas that latency waterfall prices the navigation camera stream consumed by the proposer, the permission path&#x27;s clearance check inspects range returns from the mobile base&#x27;s Ouster OS1-64 LiDAR, whose timing structure differs from that of the optical camera. A rolling-shutter camera exposes and reads out a discrete frame grid. In contrast, a spinning mechanical LiDAR continuously fires laser diodes and timestamps each azimuth column at the firing instant, disciplined by the IEEE 1588 Precision Time Protocol (PTP). Because every point carries an individual capture epoch, the scan lacks a singular frame time. The age of the resulting rack-end spatial estimate is therefore governed by how long the software pipeline buffers returns before releasing the angular sector facing the hazard.\n\nAt the configured scan rate, one mechanical revolution requires `[LidarAge.t_sweep_str]`. At a clear distance of `[LidarAge.d_clear_str]`, the `[LidarAge.aisle_width_str]` aisle subtends a forward azimuth sector of `[LidarAge.sector_deg_str]`°, which the sensor sweeps in `[LidarAge.t_sector_str]`. @Tbl-08-lidar-age compares two ingestion architectures for handling this point stream: streaming sector packets to consumers immediately upon arrival vs. buffering the entire sweep before dispatching a complete 360° point cloud.\n\n| Stage                                    |              Stream the forward sector |              Wait for the full sweep | Source                             |\n|:-----------------------------------------|---------------------------------------:|-------------------------------------:|:-----------------------------------|\n| Acquisition of the oldest relevant point |       `[LidarAge.t_sector_str]` |      `[LidarAge.t_sweep_str]` | Scan rate and aisle geometry       |\n| Sensor data latency, upper bound         |         `[LidarAge.t_data_str]` |       `[LidarAge.t_data_str]` | Datasheet                          |\n| Clock-conversion bound (PTP)             |        `[LidarAge.eps_ptp_str]` |      `[LidarAge.eps_ptp_str]` | Datasheet; measure on the machine  |\n| Upper age before processing              | **`[LidarAge.age_stream_str]`** | **`[LidarAge.age_full_str]`** | Computed                           |\n| Travel at the drive limit                |      `[LidarAge.dx_stream_str]` |      `[LidarAge.dx_full_str]` | Computed                           |\n| De-skew and occupancy extraction         |                   Needs measured value |                 Needs measured value | Host measurement under camera load |\n\n: **LiDAR age at the rack end**: The OS1-64&#x27;s upper age before host processing under two consumption policies, at the configured scan rate. Data latency and clock accuracy are the datasheet&#x27;s upper bounds; processing time must be measured on the machine under the synchronized camera and inference load. {#tbl-08-lidar-age tbl-colwidths=&quot;[30,22,22,26]&quot;}\n\nWaiting for a full revolution consumes `[LidarAge.age_full_str]` before host computation begins, exceeding the camera&#x27;s `[LidarAge.t_camera_str]` acquisition/processing allocation. In contrast, streaming the forward azimuth sector immediately leaves `[LidarAge.slack_stream_str]` of processing headroom within that allocation for motion de-skew and occupancy extraction. This arithmetic compares pipeline allocations; different scan and frame phases, buffered reuse, and downstream waiting still determine which selected observation is older at consumption. The real-time latency budget therefore dictates the architecture. The robot streams and processes sector packets incrementally, propagating individual point timestamps rather than a single aggregated sweep time.\n\nTwo strands of evidence therefore meet at the rack end, and the oldest required capture sets the shared age charge. The proposal is anchored to the camera&#x27;s frame epoch, while the admission check rests on LiDAR range. The permission path must date both on its own clock at the same admission or tick, including bounded clock uncertainty. Streaming fits the illustrated camera-sized acquisition/processing allocation only when de-skew, occupancy extraction, and any phase, reuse, or waiting allowance required by the selected consumption policy fit the `[LidarAge.slack_stream_str]` headroom. Sharing the camera&#x27;s row-five charge further requires that the actual LiDAR capture be no older than the camera capture supporting the proposal at that common check. Otherwise the budget must charge the older required evidence, with each elapsed interval counted once. The dispatch-to-admission interval remains a separate downstream charge. Neither strand includes the internal latency of the certified safety scanner, because its worst-case response time is a factory-certified parameter accounted for separately in the scanner&#x27;s own timing budget described in @sec-enforcement-composite-budget.\n\nPer-column timestamps also enable continuous motion de-skew. Over a single LiDAR revolution at full speed, the mobile base translates `[LidarSweep.dx_sweep_str]`, and the state estimator eliminates this distortion by interpolating robot odometry at each return&#x27;s precise firing timestamp (@sec-perception-transduction-calibration). While the optical camera avoids rotational sweeping delays, its serial latency budget has no spare stage to absorb arbitrary queueing, because row five charges exactly the budgeted age. In particular, collecting consecutive frames into multi-frame batches introduces substantial latency penalties before host memory contention is even considered.\n\n### Temporal batching\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ TEMPORAL BATCHING ON THE NAVIGATION CAMERA</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: The temporal-batching paragraph of @sec-perception-age-budget.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Show what a four-frame batch collected across capture times adds</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          to the navigation camera's observation age.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    16.7 ms frame period at 60 Hz; waits of 50.0, 33.3, and 16.7 ms;</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          age 51.6 ms -&gt; 101.6 ms, roughly double.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraAge (four-frame batch): the oldest of</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          n frames waits (n - 1) frame periods of RM.nav_camera.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import <em> from mlsysim.core.units import </em> from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_latency, fmt_frequency, check</p>\n<p>RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class TemporalBatching: \"\"\"Age added by batching successive navigation-camera frames.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>f_frame = RM.nav_camera.frame_rate  # category: datasheet t_age = AISLE.perception_age.to(millisecond)  # category: derived n_batch = Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraAge.batch_frames  # category: illustrative (batch size)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>_age = Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraAge.result t_period = _age.frame_period.to(millisecond) wait_1, wait_2, wait_3 = (w.to(millisecond) for w in _age.batch_waits) t_age_batched = _age.batched_age.to(millisecond) ratio = _age.batched_age_ratio</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(f_frame.to(hertz).magnitude - 60.0) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Unexpected frame rate: {f_frame}\") check(abs(wait_1.magnitude - 50.0) &lt; 0.05, f\"Unexpected oldest-frame wait: {wait_1}\") check(abs(t_age_batched.magnitude - 101.6) &lt; 0.05, f\"Unexpected batched age: {t_age_batched}\") check(1.8 &lt; ratio &lt; 2.2, f\"Prose says batching roughly doubles the age: {ratio}\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>f_frame_str = fmt_frequency(f_frame, unit=hertz, precision=0) t_period_str = fmt_latency(t_period, unit=millisecond, precision=1) wait_1_str = fmt_latency(wait_1, unit=millisecond, precision=0) wait_2_str = fmt_latency(wait_2, unit=millisecond, precision=1) wait_3_str = fmt_latency(wait_3, unit=millisecond, precision=1) t_age_batched_str = fmt_latency(t_age_batched, unit=millisecond, precision=1) ]```</p>\n<p>At the navigation camera's <code>[TemporalBatching.f_frame_str]</code> sampling rate ($\\CameraFramePeriod \\approx$ <code>[TemporalBatching.t_period_str]</code>), forming a batch of four consecutive frames to amortize kernel launch overhead or raise systolic-array utilization requires holding the first frame for <code>[TemporalBatching.wait_1_str]</code>, the second for <code>[TemporalBatching.wait_2_str]</code>, and the third for <code>[TemporalBatching.wait_3_str]</code>, while only the fourth enters immediately. Accumulating frames across successive capture times ages the earliest samples: appending that <code>[TemporalBatching.wait_1_str]</code> queueing delay to the baseline <code>[RackEndOpeningAge.t_age_str]</code> observation age roughly doubles total age to <code>[TemporalBatching.t_age_batched_str]</code>. Because row five of the stopping budget charges only the single-frame age, the observation contract at the end of this chapter sets its dispatch threshold at that age plus a sub-millisecond clock bound (@sec-perception-observation-contract). The batched frame is therefore refused before candidate admission begins. When multi-camera systems synchronize exposure pulses in hardware, grouping frames from distinct sensors across spatial dimensions can improve throughput safely; however, temporal batching across successive timestamps directly erodes closed-loop timeliness.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h3\">Observation age and feedback stability</h3>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ PHASE MARGIN SPENT BY OBSERVATION AGE\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: The phase-margin paragraph of @sec-perception-age-budget\n# │          and its margin figure.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Turn a nominal phase margin at crossover into the delay ceiling\n# │          a pure age may spend, and show how much of it a 60 ms age uses.\n# │ Show:    45 deg at 10 rad/s -&gt; 78.5 ms ceiling; 60 ms uses 76.4 percent.\n# │ How:     A pure delay tau removes phase omega_c * tau, so the ceiling is\n# │          PM_nom / omega_c (@sec-appendix-control-zoh):\n# │          Scenarios.VisualServoDelayPhaseMargin.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nimport math\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_latency, fmt_percent, check\n\nclass PhaseMarginAge:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Delay ceiling set by a nominal phase margin at crossover.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    scenario = Scenarios.VisualServoDelayPhaseMargin  # category: illustrative (visual-servo loop)\n    omega_c = scenario.crossover.to(radian / second).magnitude  # category: illustrative (gain crossover, rad/s)\n    pm_nom_deg = scenario.phase_margin.to(degree).magnitude  # category: illustrative (nominal phase margin, degrees)\n    t_used = scenario.delay  # category: illustrative (exposure, transport, and inference)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    pm_nom_rad = scenario.result.phase_margin.to(radian).magnitude\n    f_c = scenario.result.crossover_frequency.to(hertz).magnitude\n    tau_max = scenario.result.delay_ceiling.to(millisecond)\n    frac_used = scenario.result.share_used\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(tau_max.magnitude - 78.54) &lt; 0.01, f&quot;Unexpected delay ceiling: {tau_max}&quot;)\n    check(0.76 &lt; frac_used &lt; 0.77, f&quot;Unexpected share of margin used: {frac_used}&quot;)\n    check(t_used &lt; tau_max, &quot;The example age must sit below the ceiling&quot;)\n    # margin_phase_margin_erosion_knee.svg fig-alt hand-types PM 45 degrees, 60 ms and 78.5 ms.\n    check(pm_nom_deg == 45.0 and abs(t_used.to(millisecond).magnitude - 60.0) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Phase-margin margin labels drifted&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    omega_c_str = fmt(omega_c, precision=0)\n    f_c_str = fmt(f_c, precision=1)\n    pm_nom_deg_str = fmt(pm_nom_deg, precision=0)\n    pm_nom_rad_str = fmt(pm_nom_rad, precision=3)\n    tau_max_str = fmt_latency(tau_max, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    t_used_str = fmt_latency(t_used, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    frac_used_pct_str = fmt_percent(frac_used, precision=1, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n]```\n\nBeyond draining physical stopping distance, observation age also spends the phase margin of any feedback loop closed through perception, the extra lag that loop can absorb before its corrections reinforce the motion they were meant to damp. @Sec-nervous-time-constants-phase-lag establishes that a pure delay removes phase in proportion to frequency without changing loop gain. A loop with nominal phase margin $\\text{PM}_{\\text{nom}}$ at gain crossover frequency $\\CrossoverFrequency$ therefore has no margin left once $\\DispatchObservationAge \\ge \\text{PM}_{\\text{nom}}/\\CrossoverFrequency$. Perception draws on that same account. A visual-servoing tracking loop crosses over at `[PhaseMarginAge.omega_c_str]` rad/s (approximately `[PhaseMarginAge.f_c_str]` Hz) with a nominal phase margin of `[PhaseMarginAge.pm_nom_deg_str]`$^\\circ$ (`[PhaseMarginAge.pm_nom_rad_str]` rad). With no other delay charged, its nominal zero-margin ceiling for this perception contribution is $\\MaximumObservationAge = \\text{PM}_{\\text{nom}}/\\CrossoverFrequency =$ `[PhaseMarginAge.tau_max_str]`. If sensor exposure, transport, and neural inference consume `[PhaseMarginAge.t_used_str]`, the perception pipeline spends `[PhaseMarginAge.frac_used_pct_str]` of the loop&#x27;s stability allowance before motor commands are even computed. Whether the integrated loop remains adequately damped requires qualification of its complete dynamics and delays.\n\n\n\nThe perception contribution to both checks spends the same elapsed duration in different currencies. Phase lag erodes closed-loop stability, while displacement consumes forward clearance ($\\AgeBlindTravel = \\SpeedAtDetection \\cdot \\DispatchObservationAge$), so each consequence is checked on its own terms and the latency is never counted twice in the stopping budget. Rigorous capture timestamps, qualified reference frames, and explicit error bounds make these physical consequences observable throughout the software stack. Even when zero-copy DMA architectures eliminate intermediate buffer copies during host ingestion, memory controller arbitration and bus contention continue to introduce subtle waiting times.\n\n## Ingestion Overhead and Memory Bus Contention {#sec-perception-cost-ingestion}\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ NAIVE MULTI-CAMERA INGESTION COPY TRAFFIC</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: The naive-baseline paragraph of @sec-perception-cost-ingestion.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Size the copy traffic when four 1080p60 RGB streams are copied</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          three times on their way to the accelerator.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    4 x 1920 x 1080 x 3 B x 60 Hz = 1.5 GB/s per copy; 4.5 GB/s total.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     rate = cameras x pixels x bytes/pixel x frame rate x copies.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import <em> from mlsysim.core.units import </em> from mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_bandwidth, fmt_frequency, check</p>\n<p>class NaiveIngestionCopies: \"\"\"Copy traffic of a naive user-space multi-camera ingestion path.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>n_cam = 4  # category: illustrative (camera count) px = 1920 <em> 1080  # category: definitional (1080p frame) bytes_px = 3  # category: illustrative (24-bit RGB after conversion) f_cam = 60.0 </em> hertz  # category: illustrative (frame rate) n_copies = 3  # category: illustrative (driver ring, user space, device memory)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>per_copy = (n_cam <em> px </em> bytes_px <em> byte </em> f_cam).to(gigabyte / second) total = (per_copy * n_copies).to(gigabyte / second)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(per_copy.magnitude - 1.493) &lt; 0.01, f\"Unexpected per-copy rate: {per_copy}\") check(total &gt; 4.0 * gigabyte / second, f\"Unexpected total copy rate: {total}\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>f_cam_str = fmt_frequency(f_cam, unit=hertz, precision=0) per_copy_str = fmt_bandwidth(per_copy, unit=gigabyte / second, precision=1) total_str = fmt_bandwidth(total, unit=gigabyte / second, precision=1) ]```</p>\n<p>Understanding why zero-copy DMA matters starts with a common architectural pitfall in early physical AI prototypes, which connect multiple high-resolution cameras through standard USB or Ethernet ports and ingest frames using naive user-space libraries such as OpenCV's <code>VideoCapture</code> or default ROS image transport nodes. In this unoptimized baseline, every incoming frame is copied from kernel USB request blocks into a driver ring buffer, copied a second time into user-space process memory, and copied a third time across the PCIe bus into GPU memory. For four 1080p cameras streaming 24-bit color at <code>[NaiveIngestionCopies.f_cam_str]</code>, each copy stage moves <code>[NaiveIngestionCopies.per_copy_str]</code>, meaning the pipeline churns through about <code>[NaiveIngestionCopies.total_str]</code> of continuous memory copies. This overhead generates substantial memory traffic and CPU copying work, though the exact utilization depends on host core capabilities, memory architecture, and pixel format. That data rate alone does not establish CPU saturation or accelerator starvation, but it wastes memory bus bandwidth and cache capacity that concurrent real-time inference urgently requires.</p>\n<p>Real-time physical systems avoid this collapse by treating pixel ingestion as a zero-copy DMA hardware pipeline. Raw pixel streams arriving over dedicated camera buses bypass CPU copying entirely, writing frames directly into shared physical DRAM before any software thread touches the data. On a shared application processor, however, these camera writes still contend for memory service before inference can read them, and unmanaged bursts can delay host reads or overrun a receiver FIFO and drop a frame.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h3\">MIPI CSI-2 DMA ingress and zero-copy buffers</h3>\n<p>Once analog-to-digital converters on the sensor die digitize the accumulated pixel charges, the frame must cross a physical link into host memory without stalling the CPU on byte-by-byte copies or dropping scanlines when a receiver buffer fills. Camera streams enter the application processor over the MIPI CSI-2 bus. Across that link, dedicated receiver logic and scatter-gather DMA engines move serialized pixel packets from the physical lanes into kernel ring buffers in four stages.</p>\n<p>: <strong>MIPI CSI-2 (Mobile Industry Processor Interface, Camera Serial Interface 2)</strong>: The MIPI Alliance's point-to-point camera link streams serialized pixels from the sensor straight to an application processor's receiver and DMA controller, over differential D-PHY lanes or three-wire C-PHY trios, with no network stack between them. That directness is why the camera's transport term in @tbl-08-latency-budget is set by sensor mode and lane rate rather than by protocol processing.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>D-PHY / C-PHY physical layer</strong>: Physical lanes serialize pixel packets at the selected sensor and receiver's supported rate; packet headers carry error checks.</li>\n<li><strong>System-on-chip (SoC) CSI-2 host controller</strong>: Deserializes byte packets, strips headers, and validates CRC integrity.</li>\n<li><strong>Scatter-gather DMA controller</strong>: A dedicated DMA engine transfers pixel byte words directly into DRAM over the internal system interconnect (for example, an AXI crossbar).</li>\n<li><strong>Kernel DMA ring buffers</strong>: A Linux <code>v4l2</code> driver can map capture buffers into user space.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Once DMA write completion occurs, the kernel capture driver (V4L2, the Linux video-capture interface) issues an interrupt. To eliminate user-space copies, modern embedded Linux systems export DMA buffers directly through the Linux <code>dma_buf</code> subsystem. Compared with a staged three-copy variant (Kernel DMA buffer $\\to$ User-space buffer $\\to$ Pinned host memory $\\to$ GPU VRAM), <code>dma_buf</code> allows the hardware Image Signal Processor (ISP), CPU, and GPU to import and share identical physical DRAM pages through system IOMMU page tables. A zero-copy buffer handoff eliminates bulk CPU copying, but DMA arbitration, synchronization, queueing, and consumer scheduling still contribute to ingestion latency. Format conversion, layout changes, or device alignment constraints can still require a targeted transform, but the bulk pixel payload never traverses the CPU cache hierarchy. On a platform without hardware I/O cache coherency, the buffer handoff must explicitly synchronize ownership for the intended consumer. CPU access requires the platform's DMA-to-CPU cache maintenance primitives; accelerator access requires corresponding device mapping, completion ordering, and device-side cache invalidation. Flushing or invalidating the CPU cache alone does not guarantee that an independent neural accelerator will read current pixel values. These synchronization and cache maintenance operations belong directly in the measured ingestion budget.</p>\n<p>: <strong>IOMMU (Input-Output Memory Management Unit)</strong>: The device-side counterpart of the CPU's MMU. It translates the addresses a DMA engine issues through page tables the kernel controls, so the ISP, CPU, and GPU can share one set of physical pages without copying while each device stays confined to its own mappings. A translation miss on that path is one more source of ingestion delay that the measured budget must include.</p>\n<p>The copy path in @fig-08-shared-capture-buffer uses pinned host staging rather than the USB driver ring in the earlier baseline. The shared path separates physical storage from device mappings and marks the release that permits the capture engine to reuse that storage.</p>\n<p>A frame identifier therefore cannot establish that all consumers read the same physical pages. A conversion preserves the observation's identity while changing its representation, and the capture driver must track the lifetime of the source pages independently of the converted output.</p>\n<p>Interface selection balances cable reach and mechanical routing flexibility against transport, queueing, and copying overhead. MIPI CSI-2 supports direct sensor ingress to the SoC. Serializer/deserializer links extend that path across the machine over multi-meter automotive wiring. USB introduces a host protocol stack and driver buffering whose timing penalty must be measured empirically. The stage breakdown in @tbl-08-latency-budget locates those costs for the selected MIPI path. Any alternative interface requires its own measured stage budget, because an interface specification alone does not determine end-to-end latency. Sensor operating modes, inference workloads, and host memory contention must all mirror the intended deployment conditions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-15\" class=\"cs-h3\">Memory bus contention and observation age</h3>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ HOST CONTENTION THE RENEWAL-JITTER ALLOWANCE TOLERATES\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: The memory-bus contention paragraph of @sec-perception-cost-ingestion.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Size how much application-processor contention the chunk stream\n# │          can absorb before a healthy proposer&#x27;s renewals leave the lease\n# │          rule&#x27;s jitter allowance, and when a late renewal lapses the lease.\n# │ Show:    50 ms chunk period; 5 ms renewal-jitter allowance; a 12.5 percent\n# │          ratio to 40 ms inference (conditional one-for-one admission delay);\n# │          the lease lapses at 10 ms late.\n# │ How:     Chunk period from RM.chunk_rate; inference from\n# │          RM.chunk_inference_latency; lease from AISLE.t_lease; the 5 ms\n# │          allowance and the lapse point are Scenarios.WarehouseChunkLease&#x27;s\n# │          (the lease rule of @sec-nervous-lease-sizing).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt_latency, fmt_percent, check\n\nRM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass HostContentionAllowance:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Application-processor contention absorbed by the lease&#x27;s renewal-jitter allowance.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    chunk_rate = RM.chunk_rate  # category: chosen\n    t_infer = RM.chunk_inference_latency.to(millisecond)  # category: illustrative (P99 incl. vision)\n    t_lease = AISLE.t_lease.to(millisecond)  # category: chosen\n    t_jitter = (\n        Scenarios.WarehouseChunkLease.renewal_jitter\n    )  # category: chosen (renewal-jitter allowance of the lease rule, @sec-nervous-lease-sizing)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    t_period = (1 / chunk_rate).to(millisecond)\n    t_lapse = Scenarios.WarehouseChunkLease.result.renewal_lateness_limit.to(millisecond)\n    stretch = (t_jitter / t_infer).to(dimensionless).magnitude\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(t_period.magnitude - 50.0) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Unexpected chunk period: {t_period}&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_jitter.to(millisecond).magnitude - 5.0) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Unexpected renewal-jitter allowance: {t_jitter}&quot;)\n    check(t_period + t_jitter &lt; t_lease, &quot;Lease rule: chunk period plus jitter must be shorter than the lease&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_lapse.magnitude - 10.0) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Unexpected lapse point: {t_lapse}&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_lease.magnitude - 60.0) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Margin figure draws a 60 ms lease: {t_lease}&quot;)\n    check(t_infer &lt; t_period, &quot;Chunk inference must fit inside one chunk period&quot;)\n    check(abs(stretch - 0.125) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Unexpected inference stretch: {stretch}&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    t_period_str = fmt_latency(t_period, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_jitter_str = fmt_latency(t_jitter.to(millisecond), unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_infer_str = fmt_latency(t_infer, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_lapse_str = fmt_latency(t_lapse, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    stretch_pct_str = fmt_percent(stretch, precision=1, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n]```\n\nEven when camera frames sit comfortably within peak DRAM bandwidth when averaged over a one-second window, synchronized camera DMA bursts can still stall neural accelerator reads for milliseconds and inflate the tail of observation age. The raw bandwidth requirements of the mobile manipulator&#x27;s navigation and wrist cameras are sized in @sec-data-telemetry-ingestion-storage. When the perception stack reads those frames, transforms intermediate buffers, and the accelerator fetches model weights and writes activations, the memory controller carries several times that ingress rate, yet average utilization hides the microbursts that stretch individual read transactions. On edge robotics SoCs such as the NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin, the CPU cluster, deep learning accelerator (DLA), GPU, and CSI-2 DMA engines share a single physical DRAM bus in a Unified Memory Architecture (UMA). Synchronized cameras concentrate their DMA writes into sharp microbursts, saturating memory channels and triggering widespread DRAM bank conflicts. When multiple cameras dump uncached raster lines across the system crossbar simultaneously, they repeatedly open and close DRAM page buffers across active banks. If the neural accelerator attempts to stream transformer model weights and intermediate activation tensors during this exact window, memory controller arbitration stalls accelerator read requests behind high-priority sensor DMA streams. These memory read stalls introduce pipeline bubbles into hardware tensor cores, stretching neural inference latency and inflating its tail ($\\text{p99}$ jitter). The magnitude of this latency stretch depends on the platform, its interconnect arbitration policy, and the concurrent camera load. Rather than assuming nominal inference latency, engineers must measure tail stretch directly on physical hardware under the synchronized camera workload; the coupled safety lease constrains the loaded end-to-end renewal interval.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ Contention for the shared memory system, and how it would stall the permission tick on a shared-die placement, are developed in @sec-placement-resource-contention.\n:::\n\nOn the mobile manipulator, this burst lands only on the application processor. Because the permission path executes on its own microcontroller and reads its state from isolated SRAM, a memory burst cannot stretch its safety tick; rejecting a shared-die placement removed that hazard. Contention on the application processor reaches the permission path only through two observable timing channels: the age of the frame used to construct an action proposal, and the lateness of the renewal message sent by the chunk policy. These two paths have fundamentally different timing allowances. A frame that waits behind camera and accelerator traffic reaches dispatch older than the budgeted stages. The observation contract of @sec-perception-observation-contract sets the dispatch threshold at exactly the budgeted age, because a frame any older would spend travel that row five does not charge. The permission path therefore refuses every proposal built on that delayed frame.\n\nA chunk policy whose inference stalls behind that same memory traffic renews the execution lease late. Under the coupled budget rule of @sec-nervous-coupled-budget, the lease reserves `[HostContentionAllowance.t_jitter_str]` of renewal jitter above the `[HostContentionAllowance.t_period_str]` chunk period. The ratio to the chunk policy&#x27;s `[HostContentionAllowance.t_infer_str]` inference is `[HostContentionAllowance.stretch_pct_str]`, a conditional sensitivity when incremental inference delay reaches admission one-for-one. The actual allowance constrains inter-admission jitter, so the inference stretch it tolerates depends on the renewal schedule and the scheduling, transmission, validation, and mailbox delays sharing that allowance. Qualification must measure the loaded end-to-end inter-admission interval. A renewal that arrives `[HostContentionAllowance.t_lapse_str]` late causes the lease to lapse, triggering a deterministic stop. When physical isolation, frame-age validation, and feasible fallback maneuvers remain intact, excess contention compromises availability rather than safety, because the permission path refuses any action delayed beyond the qualified budget. That guarantee holds only so long as those architectural premises and uncorrupted local sensor evidence remain intact.\n\n\n\nThe duration of a bus-saturating burst also threatens the camera interface itself. Ingest engines rely on direct memory access ring buffers with double or triple buffering to decouple hardware arrival interrupts from consumer reading threads. If host memory bus contention delays DMA completion beyond what the CSI-2 receiver hardware FIFO can buffer, the receiver suffers an overrun and silently drops the frame. A dropped frame immediately doubles the effective observation interval between samples. Downstream state estimators must treat the missing update as an age or sequence fault rather than assuming that fresh scene geometry will arrive on schedule.\n\nThe underlying microarchitectural mechanisms governing frame delivery (DMA rings, IOMMU mappings, and memory controller arbitration) are treated systematically by @hennessy2019computer and @bryant2015computer. For physical AI systems, the main design metric is capture-to-consumer age and its tail distribution under the intended camera workload. Delivering an on-time frame establishes only when the physical scene was sampled, however. Determining where objects reside within that frame, and bounding the uncertainty of that spatial estimate, introduces geometric and calibration errors that consume the remainder of the safety clearance envelope.\n\n## Spatial Error and Clearance Bounds {#sec-perception-error-clearance}\n\nIf perception places a warehouse rack upright just a few centimeters farther from the base&#x27;s trajectory than it actually stands, the motion planner consumes those missing centimeters directly from its `[RollingShutterSkew.side_clearance_str]` side clearance, although no stage of the pipeline decided to spend them. Once downstream control logic uses an estimated coordinate to authorize motion, every spatial error becomes a physical displacement drawn from the clearance that protects the robot from collision. Transforming a raw sensor observation into a trustworthy clearance claim requires **metric grounding**, which maps detector measurements into Euclidean space. This grounding relies on unprojecting two-dimensional sensor measurements into three dimensions, an inverse problem that assumes calibrated optics, rigid structural mountings, and predictable surface properties.\n\nClassical projective geometry and triangulation [@hartley2003multiple; @szeliski2022computer] govern how measurement noise converts into three-dimensional spatial displacement. In two-view streaming perception, two calibrated cameras with optical centers $\\CameraOpticalCenter_1, \\CameraOpticalCenter_2$ observe a physical scene point $\\CartesianPosition \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$. The baseline vector $\\RigidTransformTranslation \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ connecting $\\CameraOpticalCenter_1$ and $\\CameraOpticalCenter_2$, together with the target point $\\CartesianPosition$, defines the **epipolar plane**. The intersection of this baseline with each image plane defines the two **epipoles** $\\ImageEpipole_1, \\ImageEpipole_2$.\n\nFor corresponding image coordinates $\\HomogeneousPixelCoordinates_1, \\HomogeneousPixelCoordinates_2$ expressed in homogeneous pixel coordinates, the rigid spatial transformation $(\\DirectionalCosineMatrix, \\RigidTransformTranslation) \\in \\text{SE}(3)$ relating the camera frames establishes the fundamental **epipolar constraint**:\n$$\\HomogeneousPixelCoordinates_2^\\top \\FundamentalMatrix \\, \\HomogeneousPixelCoordinates_1 = 0$$\nwhere $\\FundamentalMatrix = \\CameraIntrinsicInverseTranspose_2 \\EssentialMatrix \\, \\CameraIntrinsicCalibrationMatrix_1^{-1} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3\\times3}$ is the **Fundamental matrix**, and $\\EssentialMatrix = [\\RigidTransformTranslation]_\\times \\DirectionalCosineMatrix \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3\\times3}$ is the **Essential matrix** (with $[\\RigidTransformTranslation]_\\times$ denoting the skew-symmetric matrix of baseline translation). Geometrically, because the two unprojected bearing rays $\\CameraIntrinsicCalibrationMatrix_1^{-1}\\HomogeneousPixelCoordinates_1$ and $\\CameraIntrinsicCalibrationMatrix_2^{-1}\\HomogeneousPixelCoordinates_2$ meet at the same physical point, both rays and the baseline vector $\\RigidTransformTranslation$ lie in the epipolar plane, so expressing the first ray in the second camera&#x27;s frame via $\\DirectionalCosineMatrix$ and taking its cross product with $\\RigidTransformTranslation$ (written as multiplication by $[\\RigidTransformTranslation]_\\times$) produces the plane normal that must be orthogonal to the second ray. The vector $\\EpipolarLine_2 = \\FundamentalMatrix \\HomogeneousPixelCoordinates_1$ defines the **epipolar line** in the second image; any valid physical correspondence $\\HomogeneousPixelCoordinates_2$ must lie strictly along $\\EpipolarLine_2$.\n\nIn a calibrated **rectified stereo rig**, both images are digitally warped onto a shared virtual image plane parallel to the baseline ($\\DirectionalCosineMatrix = \\IdentityMatrix$, $\\RigidTransformTranslation = [\\StereoBaseline, 0, 0]^\\top$). Rectification forces epipolar lines to be exactly parallel and collinear with horizontal pixel scanlines ($\\PixelRowCoordinate_1 = \\PixelRowCoordinate_2$). This geometric transformation collapses an expensive two-dimensional correspondence search, the kind dense optical-flow networks perform, into a one-dimensional search along the same horizontal scanline. Neural stereo networks such as RAFT-Stereo evaluate this correspondence as a 1D correlation cost volume [@lipson2021raftstereo]. Disparity is then the horizontal coordinate difference $\\StereoDisparity = \\PixelColumnCoordinate_1 - \\PixelColumnCoordinate_2$, recovering metric depth through $\\CameraFrameDepth = \\frac{\\FocalLength \\StereoBaseline}{\\StereoDisparity}$.\n\nWhen mechanical vibration, chassis twist, or thermal expansion induces uncalibrated relative pitch or roll between cameras, or when rolling-shutter readout shears scanlines during sharp platform turns, the true physical correspondence shifts vertically off the horizontal epipolar line ($\\FiniteChange \\PixelRowCoordinate \\neq 0$). The 1D correlation cost volume fails or latches onto spurious texture matches across adjacent rows, injecting severe triangulation outliers into the reconstruction. Differentiating the unprojection $\\CameraFrameDepth = \\frac{\\FocalLength \\StereoBaseline}{\\StereoDisparity}$ with respect to disparity yields a negative signed derivative whose magnitude defines the small-error relation (@eq-percep-stereo-uncertainty):\n$$|\\SmallPerturbation \\CameraFrameDepth| \\approx \\frac{\\CameraFrameDepth^2}{\\FocalLength \\StereoBaseline} |\\SmallPerturbation \\StereoDisparity|$$ {#eq-percep-stereo-uncertainty}\nwhere $\\SmallPerturbation \\StereoDisparity$ represents disparity error. If $\\SmallPerturbation \\StereoDisparity$ denotes a disparity standard deviation $\\DisparityStandardDeviation$, first-order error propagation gives $\\DepthStandardDeviation \\approx \\frac{\\CameraFrameDepth^2}{\\FocalLength \\StereoBaseline}\\DisparityStandardDeviation$. Doubling range quadruples this modeled standard deviation at fixed focal length $\\FocalLength$, baseline $\\StereoBaseline$, and disparity precision $\\DisparityStandardDeviation$. For distant landmarks, an estimator instead parameterizes depth as inverse depth $\\InverseDepth = 1/\\CameraFrameDepth = \\StereoDisparity / (\\FocalLength \\StereoBaseline)$. Differentiating inverse depth with respect to disparity yields:\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\InverseDepth}{\\partial \\StereoDisparity} = \\frac{1}{\\FocalLength \\StereoBaseline} = \\text{constant}$$\nBecause this derivative is independent of distance, measurement errors in disparity map linearly into inverse depth ($\\SmallPerturbation \\InverseDepth = \\frac{1}{\\FocalLength \\StereoBaseline} \\SmallPerturbation \\StereoDisparity$). This linear mapping provides **inverse-depth metric grounding**, preserving numerical conditioning for distant landmarks when the disparity-error model is appropriate. Inverse depth does not eliminate mismatch outliers or guarantee estimator stability. In contrast, a pulsed time-of-flight sensor estimates range directly from transit time, $\\RadialRange = \\frac{\\VacuumLightSpeed \\PulseRoundTripTime}{2}$; its range error depends on its timing and detection electronics, remaining approximately flat across its rated operating region.\n\n: **Inverse-Depth Parametrization**: @civera2008inverse introduced it for monocular SLAM, where a newly seen landmark may lie arbitrarily far away. Storing $\\InverseDepth = 1/\\CameraFrameDepth$ lets a point at infinity enter the estimate as $\\InverseDepth = 0$ with finite variance, where a depth coordinate would need an infinite one, so near and distant landmarks share one well-conditioned representation. The full parameterization is given in @sec-appendix-ml-spatial.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ STEREO DEPTH-ERROR SCALE AND BASELINE THERMAL BIAS</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: The stereo worked example of @sec-perception-error-clearance and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the thermal-expansion footnote of @sec-perception-observation-contract.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Show how the one-sigma stereo depth scale grows with squared range,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          why adding it to a clearance kept along the viewing ray does not</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          give an inset, and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          what an unmodeled baseline change does to depth at long range.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    sigma_z 12.5 mm at 2 m and 312.5 mm at 10 m; a 150 mm ray clearance plus</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          one sigma 162.5 mm and 462.5 mm; 10 mm depth bias at 20 m.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     sigma_z = z^2 sigma_d / (f b); |dz| = z |db| / b:</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          Scenarios.WarehouseStereoRigDepthError (StereoDepthErrorModel).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>import math from mlsysim import <em> from mlsysim.core.units import </em> from mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_length, fmt_display_math, check</p>\n<p>AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class StereoDepthError: \"\"\"One-sigma stereo depth scale on an illustrative rig, and baseline thermal bias.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>rig = Scenarios.WarehouseStereoRigDepthError  # category: illustrative (stereo rig) b = rig.baseline  # category: illustrative (stereo baseline) f_px = rig.focal_length_px  # category: illustrative (focal length, pixels) sigma_d_px = rig.disparity_sigma_px  # category: illustrative (disparity standard deviation, pixels) z_near = rig.near_range  # category: illustrative z_far = rig.far_range  # category: illustrative db = rig.baseline_change  # category: illustrative (unmodeled baseline change) z_thermal = rig.bias_range  # category: illustrative c_ray = rig.ray_clearance  # category: illustrative (clearance kept along the viewing ray) z_rack = AISLE.d_clear  # category: illustrative (rack-end clear distance) delta_loc = AISLE.delta_loc  # category: illustrative (localization allowance) side_clearance = AISLE.side_clearance  # category: illustrative</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>_st = rig.result sigma_z_near = _st.sigma_near.to(millimeter) sigma_z_far = _st.sigma_far.to(millimeter) naive_near = _st.inset_near.to(millimeter) naive_far = _st.inset_far.to(millimeter) dz_thermal = _st.baseline_bias.to(millimeter) sigma_z_rack = _st.sigma_target.to(millimeter) z_star_loc = _st.range_at_loc_allowance.to(meter)  # range where sigma_z = delta_loc z_star_side = _st.range_at_side_clearance.to(meter)  # range where sigma_z = side clearance z_star_side_half_b = _st.range_at_side_clearance_half_baseline.to(meter)  # checkpoint: halved baseline</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(sigma_z_near.magnitude - 12.5) &lt; 1e-6, f\"Unexpected near sigma_z: {sigma_z_near}\") check(abs(sigma_z_far.magnitude - 312.5) &lt; 1e-6, f\"Unexpected far sigma_z: {sigma_z_far}\") check(abs(sigma_z_far / sigma_z_near - 25.0) &lt; 1e-9, \"Depth scale must grow with squared range\") check(abs(dz_thermal.magnitude - 10.0) &lt; 1e-6, f\"Unexpected thermal depth bias: {dz_thermal}\") check(abs(sigma_z_rack.magnitude - 3.78) &lt; 0.01, f\"Unexpected sigma_z at the rack end: {sigma_z_rack}\") check(abs(z_star_loc.magnitude - 4.0) &lt; 1e-6, f\"Unexpected delta_loc crossover range: {z_star_loc}\") check(abs(z_star_side.magnitude - 6.93) &lt; 0.005, f\"Unexpected side-clearance crossover range: {z_star_side}\") check(z_rack &lt; z_star_loc &lt; z_star_side &lt; z_far, \"Notebook says the rack end sits well inside both crossovers\") check(abs(z_star_side_half_b.magnitude - 4.90) &lt; 0.005, f\"Checkpoint answer drifted: {z_star_side_half_b}\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>b_str = fmt_length(b, unit=meter, precision=2) f_px_str = fmt(f_px, precision=0) sigma_d_px_str = fmt(sigma_d_px, precision=1) z_near_str = fmt_length(z_near, unit=meter, precision=0) z_far_str = fmt_length(z_far, unit=meter, precision=0) sigma_z_near_str = fmt_length(sigma_z_near, unit=millimeter, precision=1) sigma_z_far_str = fmt_length(sigma_z_far, unit=millimeter, precision=1) c_ray_str = fmt_length(c_ray, unit=millimeter, precision=0) naive_near_str = fmt_length(naive_near, unit=millimeter, precision=1) naive_far_str = fmt_length(naive_far, unit=millimeter, precision=1) db_str = fmt_length(db, unit=millimeter, precision=1) z_thermal_str = fmt_length(z_thermal, unit=meter, precision=0) dz_thermal_str = fmt_length(dz_thermal, unit=millimeter, precision=0) z_rack_str = fmt_length(z_rack.to(meter), unit=meter, precision=2) delta_loc_str = fmt_length(delta_loc.to(millimeter), unit=millimeter, precision=0) side_clearance_str = fmt_length(side_clearance.to(meter), unit=meter, precision=2) sigma_z_rack_str = fmt_length(sigma_z_rack, unit=millimeter, precision=1) z_star_loc_str = fmt_length(z_star_loc, unit=meter, precision=0) z_star_side_str = fmt_length(z_star_side, unit=meter, precision=1)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h1\">Display arithmetic for nbk-perception-stereo-range-wall (magnitudes, LaTeX units)</h1>\n<p>_fb = fmt(f_px <em> b.to(meter).magnitude, precision=0)  # f b in pixel-meters _sd = fmt(sigma_d_px, precision=1) _zn = fmt(z_near.to(meter).magnitude, precision=0) _zf = fmt(z_far.to(meter).magnitude, precision=0) _ratio = fmt((sigma_z_far / sigma_z_near).to(dimensionless).magnitude, precision=0) _loc = fmt(delta_loc.to(meter).magnitude, precision=2) _side = fmt(side_clearance.to(meter).magnitude, precision=2) sigma_rack_math = fmt_display_math( rf\"\\sigma_z = \\frac{{z^2 \\sigma_d}}{{f b}} = \\frac{{({fmt(z_rack.to(meter).magnitude, precision=2)}\\ \\text{{m}})^2 \\times {_sd}\\ \\text{{px}}}}{{{f_px_str}\\ \\text{{px}} \\times {fmt(b.to(meter).magnitude, precision=2)}\\ \\text{{m}}}} = {fmt(sigma_z_rack.magnitude, precision=1)}\\ \\text{{mm}}, \\quad \\frac{{\\sigma_z({_zf}\\ \\text{{m}})}}{{\\sigma_z({_zn}\\ \\text{{m}})}} = \\frac{{{fmt(sigma_z_far.magnitude, precision=1)}}}{{{fmt(sigma_z_near.magnitude, precision=1)}}} = {_ratio}\" ) crossover_math = fmt_display_math( rf\"z^{{</em>}} = \\sqrt{{\\frac{{C\\,f b}}{{\\sigma_d}}}}: \\quad \\sqrt{{\\frac{{{_loc}\\ \\text{{m}} \\times {_fb}\\ \\text{{px}}\\cdot\\text{{m}}}}{{{_sd}\\ \\text{{px}}}}}} = {fmt(z_star_loc.magnitude, precision=0)}\\ \\text{{m}}, \\quad \\sqrt{{\\frac{{{_side}\\ \\text{{m}} \\times {_fb}\\ \\text{{px}}\\cdot\\text{{m}}}}{{{_sd}\\ \\text{{px}}}}}} = {fmt(z_star_side.magnitude, precision=1)}\\ \\text{{m}}\" ) ]```</p>\n<p>On the running machine, the rack-end range comes from the OS1-64 LiDAR, whose datasheet quotes one-standard-deviation precision of <code>[LidarSweep.sigma_r_min_str]</code> to <code>[LidarSweep.sigma_r_max_str]</code> beyond 1 m in the 1024×10 Hz mode. Applying that scale to the selected scan mode requires bias and residual-tail qualification under the intended surfaces and motion. A design that tried to range with cameras alone would have to triangulate, because the navigation camera by itself recovers only the bearing ray of @eq-percep-bearing-ray. An illustrative stereo pair shows where such a design meets a range wall, by asking how far down the aisle its depth error stays inside the stopping budget's allowances. Notebook \\ref{nbk-perception-stereo-range-wall} evaluates @eq-percep-stereo-uncertainty on that rig, and @fig-08-depth-uncertainty-clearance plots the curve.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: An illustrative stereo pair must place a rack upright for the base's clearance checks. How large is its one-standard-deviation depth scale at the rack end, and at what range does that scale alone consume the localization allowance and then the whole side clearance?</p>\n<p><strong>Variables</strong>:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Baseline $\\StereoBaseline =$ <code>[StereoDepthError.b_str]</code>, focal length $\\FocalLength =$ <code>[StereoDepthError.f_px_str]</code> px, and disparity standard deviation $\\DisparityStandardDeviation =$ <code>[StereoDepthError.sigma_d_px_str]</code> px over the tested range (Illustrative rig; the running machine carries no depth camera).</li>\n<li>Rack-end range $\\CameraFrameDepth =$ <code>[StereoDepthError.z_rack_str]</code> (Illustrative).</li>\n<li>Allowances $\\ClearanceAllowance$: localization $\\LocalizationBound =$ <code>[StereoDepthError.delta_loc_str]</code> and side clearance <code>[StereoDepthError.side_clearance_str]</code> (Illustrative; @sec-appendix-systems-machine-data-sheet).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>:</p>\n<p><em>Depth scale.</em> At the rack end, and between the figure's two quoted ranges, <code>[StereoDepthError.sigma_rack_math]</code></p>\n<p><em>Crossover ranges.</em> Setting $\\DepthStandardDeviation$ equal to an allowance $\\ClearanceAllowance$ and solving for range, <code>[StereoDepthError.crossover_math]</code></p>\n<p><em>Sensitivity.</em> Five times the range costs twenty-five times the depth scale, so the crossovers move only with the square root of $\\FocalLength \\StereoBaseline / \\DisparityStandardDeviation$.</p>\n<p><strong>Result</strong>: At the rack end, one standard deviation is <code>[StereoDepthError.sigma_z_rack_str]</code>. It equals $\\LocalizationBound$ at <code>[StereoDepthError.z_star_loc_str]</code> and the whole side clearance at <code>[StereoDepthError.z_star_side_str]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: Stereo depth error is a range budget. This rig's nominal depth scale fits the rack-end allowance, but supporting that decision still requires the tail, bias, and timing qualification developed below. A claim about an obstacle several meters down the aisle spends the stopping budget's $\\LocalizationBound$ on one standard deviation alone. Beyond a few meters, the clearance claim must therefore come from a range sensor whose error does not grow with $\\CameraFrameDepth^2$, or the speed must fall.</p>\n<p>The values in Notebook \\ref{nbk-perception-stereo-range-wall} are nominal <strong>one-standard-deviation scales</strong>, not clearance insets or certified 99th-percentile bounds. A usable safety inset requires an empirical tail quantile of the <em>full spatial residual</em>, calibrated on the intended sensor across realistic range, surface texture, platform motion, and ambient lighting conditions, with systematic bias and missed detections handled separately. A pervasive trap in visual estimation is assuming that measurement errors follow a nominal Gaussian distribution where a $3\\MeasurementErrorStandardDeviation$ gate bounds 99.7 percent of all errors. In physical vision, disparity estimation relies on matching pixel appearance across views. When scenes contain repetitive textures (such as corrugated warehouse siding), specular reflections from polished concrete floors, or partial occlusions along object silhouettes, correspondence matching produces catastrophic multimodal outliers rather than Gaussian scatter. A fraction of stereo returns are then gross mismatch outliers lying many standard deviations from the true surface. Because that outlier fraction depends on scene texture, lighting, and the matching architecture, it must be measured directly on residuals from operational deployments rather than assumed. Relying on Gaussian $3\\MeasurementErrorStandardDeviation$ bounds without robust loss formulations (such as Huber or Tukey loss) or heavy-tailed empirical quantile validation allows these gross outliers to distort the state estimate, directly compromising physical clearance. When validated range-error bounds exceed stopping margins, the vehicle must reduce speed or refuse the motion proposal entirely. Even when optical depth error is bounded in the camera's own frame, however, the obstacle's position must still be mapped across the robot's physical joints and mounts into the frame of the moving body.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h3\">Clearance insets and transform chain error</h3>\n<p>Before an estimated point can govern motion, it must propagate through kinematic coordinate transformations into the robot base frame and end effector. The mechanics of rigid body transformations and SE(3) kinematic frame composition follow classical treatments by Craig [@craig2005introduction] and @lynch2017modern. Each link in this transform chain contributes scale errors, mounting calibration drift, joint encoder quantization, and mechanical compliance under load. As demonstrated by the reference-frame principles of @sec-perception-handoff, angular misalignment at the sensor mount amplifies linearly with range. If the navigation camera's mount shifts by $\\ExtrinsicMisalignmentAngle =$ <code>[ExtrinsicErrorBudget.theta_ext_str]</code> rad against the chassis, the lateral error $\\SmallPerturbation \\CartesianXCoordinate \\approx \\CameraFrameDepth\\,\\ExtrinsicMisalignmentAngle$ at $\\CameraFrameDepth =$ <code>[ExtrinsicErrorBudget.z_ext_str]</code> reaches <code>[ExtrinsicErrorBudget.dx_ext_str]</code>. The shift is lateral, so on the approach to the rack end it is charged against the side clearance, as the readout notebook charged lateral skew. Its size is the point, because it is as large as the entire <code>[ExtrinsicErrorBudget.delta_loc_str]</code> allowance $\\LocalizationBound$ that the stopping budget holds for localization along the direction of travel.</p>\n<p>The comparison therefore concerns error scale rather than which allowance pays. The localization allowance carries small, validated residuals, such as the sub-millimeter travel during the clock-conversion bound that the observation contract absorbs (@sec-perception-observation-contract). An extrinsic shift that consumes that entire budget represents a mechanical calibration failure; downstream consumers must detect and refuse such observations via calibration validity checks rather than absorb them. When combined with quadratic stereo depth dispersion, the total spatial error vector $\\SpatialErrorVector$ at standoff range is anisotropic, dominated by longitudinal depth along the optical axis and angular lever-arm effects orthogonal to it.</p>\n<p>↪ How the localization allowance enters the base's kinetic barrier certificate is developed in @sec-enforcement-kinetic-barrier-certificates.</p>\n<p>Every physical safety barrier operates by defending a geometric boundary around the machine envelope. For a spatial position error vector $\\SpatialErrorVector$, the triangle inequality gives a conservative relation between nominal and true clearance: $$\\TrueClearance \\ge \\NominalClearance - \\|\\SpatialErrorVector\\|$$ To ensure the true obstacle remains beyond the required safe physical clearance $\\RequiredClearance$ (such as the base's side clearance), the permission path enforces a clearance inset $\\ClearanceInset$ (@eq-percep-clearance-inset): $$\\ClearanceInset = \\RequiredClearance + \\|\\SpatialErrorVector\\|<em>{\\text{p99}}$$ {#eq-percep-clearance-inset} Here $\\|\\SpatialErrorVector\\|</em>{\\text{p99}}$ is an empirically certified 99th-percentile quantile of the full spatial residual norm, incorporating optical noise, kinematic propagation, and uncalibrated mounting bias. The tempting shortcut replaces that quantile with one standard deviation from the stereo notebook. For a required clearance of $\\RequiredClearance =$ <code>[StereoDepthError.c_ray_str]</code>, it would hold an inset of <code>[StereoDepthError.naive_near_str]</code> at <code>[StereoDepthError.z_near_str]</code> or <code>[StereoDepthError.naive_far_str]</code> at <code>[StereoDepthError.z_far_str]</code>. Neither number covers the Gaussian tail beyond one standard deviation or the mismatch outliers described earlier. The measured quantile targets 99 percent coverage under the declared calibration assumptions; it does not provide a hard bound on the remaining residual tail. Closing that residual tail requires a justified hard bound on the sensing error, a feasible stopping trajectory, and a safety filter that responds within the remaining latency margin.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Clearance inset</strong></em> ($\\ClearanceInset$) is the separation a consumer holds between an estimated obstacle position and the machine's envelope so that the true obstacle stays at least the required physical clearance away, except with a stated tail probability measured under the conditions of use.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: The inset converts measured spatial error into clearance that the motion gives up (@eq-percep-clearance-inset). Every millimeter of qualified error widens the region the permission path treats as occupied, so the planner must slow or route farther away. On the illustrative stereo rig, one standard deviation of depth error alone equals the base's <code>[StereoDepthError.side_clearance_str]</code> side clearance at <code>[StereoDepthError.z_star_side_str]</code>, so there the inset would double the clearance even if the residual tail were no wider than one standard deviation.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: A covariance describes the second moments of the error an estimator believes it has; the inset rests on residuals measured against ground truth, so it includes the bias and heavy tail that the covariance omits. A 99th-percentile inset is also not a hard bound. It leaves 1 percent of residuals outside, whose containment requires a justified sensing-error bound, a feasible response under that bound, and a timely independent permission path. A stop calculated from the same unsupported observation does not independently cover its residual tail.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that a reported covariance, or a depth network's predicted spread, sets the inset. Neither is measured against ground truth, so neither contains the calibration bias and mismatch outliers that the residual campaign of @sec-perception-qualifying-error captures.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The two halves of the argument sit side by side in @fig-08-depth-uncertainty-clearance. In the upper panel, the stereo depth scale climbs quadratically past the LiDAR's flat datasheet precision for the specified 1024×10 Hz mode. In the lower panel, the inset holds the estimate far enough out that only the measured residual tail, which unmodeled bias can enlarge, falls inside $\\RequiredClearance$.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ DEPTH ERROR SCALE AND CLEARANCE INSET (FIGURE)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @fig-08-depth-uncertainty-clearance in\n# │          @sec-perception-error-clearance.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Contrast a stereo depth scale that grows with squared range with\n# │          OS1-64 datasheet precision at 1024 x 10 Hz, then show what a\n# │          validated quantile inset buys and what it leaves.\n# │ Show:    (a) sigma_z(z) for the illustrative stereo rig with its two quoted\n# │          points, and the OS1-64 precision band; (b) a schematic error\n# │          density along the viewing ray with C_safe, the P99 inset, and the\n# │          residual tail.\n# │ How:     (a) reads StereoDepthError and LidarSweep (no literals); (b) is a\n# │          schematic with symbolic ticks only (no plotted numbers).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom mlsysim import viz\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import check\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import millimeter\n\nSDE, LS = StereoDepthError, LidarSweep\nPETROL, INK, SOFT, PALE = &quot;#007A87&quot;, &quot;#1A202C&quot;, &quot;#4A5568&quot;, &quot;#A0AEC0&quot;\nPINE, CRIMSON = &quot;#1A4D3E&quot;, &quot;#A51C30&quot;  # structural, consequence (theme-colors.tex)\n\n# ┌── 1. LOAD ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nk_mm_per_m2 = (SDE.sigma_d_px / (SDE.f_px * SDE.b)).to(millimeter / meter**2).magnitude  # category: derived\nz_far_m = SDE.z_far.to(meter).magnitude\nz_near_m = SDE.z_near.to(meter).magnitude\nlid_lo = LS.sigma_r_min.to(millimeter).magnitude  # category: datasheet\nlid_hi = LS.sigma_r_max.to(millimeter).magnitude  # category: datasheet\n\n# ┌── 2. EXECUTE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nz = np.linspace(0.0, 1.2 * z_far_m, 200)\nsig = k_mm_per_m2 * z**2\n\n# ┌── 3. GUARD ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\ncheck(abs(k_mm_per_m2 * z_far_m**2 - SDE.sigma_z_far.to(millimeter).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Plotted stereo curve must pass through sigma_z_far.&quot;)\ncheck(abs(k_mm_per_m2 * z_near_m**2 - SDE.sigma_z_near.to(millimeter).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Plotted stereo curve must pass through sigma_z_near.&quot;)\n\n# ┌── 4. PLOT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nviz.set_book_style()\nfig, (ax, bx) = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(6.6, 5.8), gridspec_kw=dict(hspace=0.45))\n\n# (a) depth error scale\nax.axhspan(lid_lo, lid_hi, xmin=1.0 / (1.2 * z_far_m), color=PALE, alpha=0.45, lw=0)\nax.text(\n    1.2 * z_far_m * 0.98,\n    lid_hi + 10,\n    f&quot;OS1 1024×10 Hz\\n$\\\\sigma_R$: {LS.sigma_r_min_str} to {LS.sigma_r_max_str}&quot;,\n    ha=&quot;right&quot;,\n    va=&quot;bottom&quot;,\n    fontsize=9,\n    color=SOFT,\n)\nax.plot(z, sig, color=PETROL, lw=1.8)\nfor zz, s_str in ((z_near_m, SDE.sigma_z_near_str), (z_far_m, SDE.sigma_z_far_str)):\n    ax.plot([zz], [k_mm_per_m2 * zz**2], &quot;o&quot;, color=PETROL, ms=4)\nax.annotate(\n    f&quot;{SDE.sigma_z_far_str} at {SDE.z_far_str}&quot;,\n    xy=(z_far_m, k_mm_per_m2 * z_far_m**2),\n    xytext=(z_far_m * 0.18, k_mm_per_m2 * z_far_m**2 * 1.05),\n    fontsize=9,\n    color=PETROL,\n    va=&quot;center&quot;,\n    arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=&quot;-&quot;, color=PALE, lw=0.6),\n)\nax.annotate(\n    f&quot;{SDE.sigma_z_near_str} at {SDE.z_near_str}&quot;,\n    xy=(z_near_m, k_mm_per_m2 * z_near_m**2),\n    xytext=(z_near_m * 0.55, k_mm_per_m2 * z_far_m**2 * 0.42),\n    fontsize=9,\n    color=PETROL,\n    va=&quot;center&quot;,\n    arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=&quot;-&quot;, color=PALE, lw=0.6),\n)\nax.text(z_far_m * 0.62, k_mm_per_m2 * (z_far_m * 1.1) ** 2, r&quot;stereo $\\sigma_z \\propto Z_c^2$&quot;, fontsize=9, color=PETROL, ha=&quot;right&quot;, va=&quot;center&quot;)\nax.set_xlim(0, 1.2 * z_far_m)\nax.set_ylim(0, k_mm_per_m2 * (1.2 * z_far_m) ** 2 * 1.02)\nax.set_xlabel(&quot;range $Z_c$ (m)&quot;, fontsize=9)\nax.set_ylabel(&quot;one-sigma depth error (mm)&quot;, fontsize=9)\nax.tick_params(labelsize=9)\nax.set_title(&quot;(a)&quot;, fontsize=9, loc=&quot;left&quot;, fontweight=&quot;bold&quot;)\nfor s in (&quot;top&quot;, &quot;right&quot;):\n    ax.spines[s].set_visible(False)\n\n# (b) schematic: error density along the viewing ray, heavy tailed\nx = np.linspace(0, 10, 600)\nc_safe, c_inset = 3.4, 6.0\ndens = (1 + ((x - c_inset) / 0.8) ** 2 / 2.0) ** (-1.5)\nbx.fill_between(x, 0, dens, where=x &gt;= c_safe, color=PETROL, alpha=0.18, lw=0)\nbx.fill_between(x, 0, dens, where=x &lt;= c_safe, color=CRIMSON, alpha=0.35, lw=0)\nbx.plot(x, dens, color=PETROL, lw=1.3)\nbx.axvline(c_safe, color=PINE, lw=1.2)\nbx.axvline(c_inset, color=PINE, lw=1.2, ls=(0, (4, 3)))\nbx.text(c_inset + 0.1, 1.1, &quot;estimate held\\nat the inset&quot;, ha=&quot;left&quot;, va=&quot;bottom&quot;, fontsize=9, color=SOFT)\nbx.annotate(&quot;&quot;, xy=(c_safe, 0.86), xytext=(c_inset, 0.86), arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=&quot;&lt;-&gt;&quot;, color=PINE, lw=0.9))\nbx.text((c_safe + c_inset) / 2, 0.9, &quot;$\\\\|\\\\,\\\\epsilon\\\\,\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{p99}}$&quot;, ha=&quot;center&quot;, va=&quot;bottom&quot;, fontsize=9, color=PINE)\nbx.annotate(&quot;&quot;, xy=(0, 0.62), xytext=(c_safe, 0.62), arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=&quot;&lt;-&gt;&quot;, color=PINE, lw=0.9))\nbx.text(c_safe / 2, 0.66, &quot;$C_{\\\\mathrm{safe}}$&quot;, ha=&quot;center&quot;, va=&quot;bottom&quot;, fontsize=9, color=PINE)\nbx.annotate(\n    &quot;residual tail\\nand bias&quot;,\n    xy=(c_safe - 0.5, 0.05),\n    xytext=(c_safe - 0.4, 0.34),\n    ha=&quot;right&quot;,\n    va=&quot;center&quot;,\n    fontsize=9,\n    color=CRIMSON,\n    arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=&quot;-&quot;, color=CRIMSON, lw=0.6),\n)\nbx.set_xticks([0, c_safe, c_inset])\nbx.set_xticklabels([&quot;body&quot;, &quot;$C_{\\\\mathrm{safe}}$&quot;, &quot;$C_{\\\\mathrm{inset}}$&quot;], fontsize=9)\nbx.set_yticks([])\nbx.set_xlim(0, 10)\nbx.set_ylim(0, 1.3)\nbx.set_xlabel(&quot;distance along the viewing ray&quot;, fontsize=9)\nbx.grid(False)\nbx.set_title(&quot;(b)&quot;, fontsize=9, loc=&quot;left&quot;, fontweight=&quot;bold&quot;)\nfor s in (&quot;top&quot;, &quot;right&quot;, &quot;left&quot;):\n    bx.spines[s].set_visible(False)\nplt.show()\n]```\n\n:::\n\nBecause clearance depends on propagated spatial error, downstream motion planners and safety arbitration layers cannot consume raw coordinate tuples. A covariance matrix $\\BodyFramePointCovariance \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3\\times3}$ alone cannot determine whether a $10\\ \\text{mm}$ or $500\\ \\text{mm}$ clearance inset is justified; establishing that buffer requires an independently validated tail quantile or hard bound. When spatial error is qualified, the permission path can expand the clearance inset, trading speed or workspace for separation. Downstream deterministic safety filters, the per-tick checks that keep the machine inside a region from which it can still stop, hold it outside this expanded boundary $\\ClearanceInset$ and so preserve the clearance buffer against sensing and calibration error. Environmental hazards such as a textureless wall, solar glare, or lost calibration can invalidate that qualification without making a numerical covariance matrix literally infinite. The permission path must then refuse motion proposals that depend on the affected claim and select a feasible fallback.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ Safety filters, and the control barrier functions that formalize them, are developed in @sec-enforcement-safe-sets-conditional-permission.\n:::\n\nOn every frame, the producer builds a spatial claim in a fixed order, where any failed check halts the pipeline. It first converts the capture timestamp to the consumer&#x27;s clock with a bounded synchronization error and computes the observation age $\\DispatchObservationAge$ at dispatch. If age plus clock uncertainty exceeds the dispatch threshold, it marks the observation stale, and proposals that need it are refused. At admission and across subsequent ticks, the permission path computes the current evidence age relative to capture time and compares it with a separate, later runtime staleness threshold (@sec-perception-observation-contract). For a fresh observation, the producer unprojects each pixel measurement into optical coordinates with a sensor covariance. It then carries point and covariance into the body frame by first-order SE(3) error propagation through the calibrated extrinsics at capture. A consumer that predicts forward must attach its motion-model uncertainty without altering the evidence epoch. Validated residuals, including bias, then set the inset radius $\\ClearanceInset$, and when no valid bound remains, the affected motion is refused. What leaves perception is an error-qualified claim $(\\BodyFramePoint, \\BodyFramePointCovariance, \\ClearanceInset, \\DispatchObservationAge)$, never a bare coordinate.\n\n: **Covariance Propagation**: First-order propagation maps measurement covariance to $\\CameraFramePointCovariance = \\UnprojectionJacobian\\MeasurementNoiseCovariance\\UnprojectionJacobian^\\top$ in the optical frame and then to $\\BodyFramePointCovariance = \\CameraExtrinsicRotation\\CameraFramePointCovariance\\CameraExtrinsicRotation^\\top + \\ExtrinsicCalibrationCovariance$ in the body frame, where the extrinsic term is what a mount shift adds. Its largest eigenvalue gives the principal variance scale, which for stereo points lies along the viewing ray; @sec-appendix-systems-vision derives the full propagation.\n\nThat claim describes one measurement at one instant, and perception&#x27;s responsibility ends with it. Keeping the rack-end range current between sweeps, fusing the LiDAR with a second sensor, joining detections into tracks, and applying a late measurement at its capture epoch are estimation over time. @Sec-memory takes them up, beginning with state estimation in @sec-perception-state-estimation, and every one of them consumes the dated, framed, error-qualified claim built here. What perception must still supply is evidence that the claimed error was measured.\n\n### Qualifying the claimed error {#sec-perception-qualifying-error}\n\nPlugging a datasheet precision value or an unmeasured network clock offset into a clearance check leaves systematic mounting bias, heavy-tailed mismatch outliers, and asymmetric path delays uncovered. A physical obstacle can then sit inside the safety boundary while the permission check passes. Two numbers in the claim just built therefore cannot come from a model or a datasheet: the tail quantile that sets the clearance inset, and the clock-conversion bound that dates the claim on the permission path&#x27;s clock. Each is fixed by a measurement campaign, and the observation contract of @sec-perception-observation-contract carries both. Whether a reported covariance matches empirical error is evaluated with consistency statistics that require an estimator&#x27;s prediction; that test is developed alongside the estimator (@sec-memory-estimator-consistency).\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ TAIL-QUANTILE AND CLOCK-DRIFT QUALIFICATION NUMBERS</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-perception-qualifying-error.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Give the conformal rank for a 99th-percentile bound and the drift</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          of a free-running sensor clock between syncs.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    at least 99 residuals for a 1 percent bound; with 1,000, the 991st</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          smallest (10th largest); 10 us of drift.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     split-conformal rank ceil((n + 1)(1 - alpha)),</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          Scenarios.ClearanceInsetConformalRank; Ouster OS1 Rev7 internal</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          clock drift (10 ppm) over a chosen 1 s sync interval,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          Scenarios.WarehouseLidarClockConversion.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          The covariance-consistency bands moved with the estimator to</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          @sec-memory-estimator-consistency.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>import math from mlsysim import <em> from mlsysim.core.units import </em> from mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_int, fmt_latency, fmt_percent, check</p>\n<p>RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator</p>\n<p>class ErrorQualification: \"\"\"Sample sizes for a clearance-inset quantile and the clock-drift bound.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>alpha = Scenarios.ClearanceInsetConformalRank.alpha  # category: chosen (tail mass for the clearance inset) n_resid = Scenarios.ClearanceInsetConformalRank.calibration_count  # category: chosen (calibration residuals) drift_ppm = Scenarios.WarehouseLidarClockConversion.drift_rate.to(ppm).magnitude  # category: datasheet (registry: OS1 Rev7 internal clock drift) t_sync = Scenarios.WarehouseLidarClockConversion.sync_interval  # category: chosen (sync interval)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>_rank = Scenarios.ClearanceInsetConformalRank.result n_min = _rank.min_count k_rank = _rank.rank k_from_top = _rank.rank_from_top drift = Scenarios.WarehouseLidarClockConversion.result.drift_per_interval.to(microsecond)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(n_min == 99, f\"Unexpected minimum calibration size: {n_min}\") check(k_rank == 991 and k_from_top == 10, f\"Unexpected conformal rank: {k_rank}\") check(abs(drift.magnitude - 10.0) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Unexpected clock drift: {drift}\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>alpha_pct_str = fmt_percent(alpha, precision=0, style=\"prose\") n_min_str = fmt_int(n_min) n_resid_str = fmt_int(n_resid, commas=True) k_rank_str = fmt_int(k_rank) k_from_top_str = fmt_int(k_from_top) drift_ppm_str = fmt(drift_ppm, precision=0) t_sync_str = fmt(t_sync.to(second).magnitude, precision=0) drift_str = fmt_latency(drift, unit=microsecond, precision=0) ]```</p>\n<p>The clearance inset of @eq-percep-clearance-inset needs a 99th-percentile bound on the residual norm under intended operational conditions, not a theoretical covariance. The most direct procedure is empirical. Collect $\\CalibrationExampleCount$ residual norms against ground truth, sort them, and select the $\\ConformalQuantileRank$-th smallest with $\\ConformalQuantileRank = \\lceil (\\CalibrationExampleCount+1)(1-\\ConformalMiscoverageRate) \\rceil$. This split-conformal rule guarantees that a future residual falls at or below the bound with probability at least $1 - \\ConformalMiscoverageRate$, provided calibration and deployment residuals are exchangeable [@angelopoulos2023gentle]. It is the inductive conformal construction that @sec-brain-softmax-illusion used to turn a policy's dispersion into a coverage guarantee, applied here to measured residual norms rather than to model scores. For $\\ConformalMiscoverageRate =$ <code>[ErrorQualification.alpha_pct_str]</code>, the rule requires at least <code>[ErrorQualification.n_min_str]</code> residuals, returning the largest observed value when evaluated on that minimum set. With <code>[ErrorQualification.n_resid_str]</code> residuals, it returns the <code>[ErrorQualification.k_rank_str]</code>st smallest value (the <code>[ErrorQualification.k_from_top_str]</code>th largest). This guarantee holds marginally over the calibration distribution. A new floor finish, a lighting change, or a mechanical recalibration breaks exchangeability, requiring new residual campaigns sized by the per-claim sample-size rule of @sec-evaluation-trials-per-claim. Because these residuals are measured directly against ground truth, they capture the sensor's physical bias, which nominal covariance models omit.</p>\n<p>The clock-conversion bound $\\ClockConversionBound$ is likewise measured rather than assumed. The IEEE 1588 Precision Time Protocol (PTP) exchange that @sec-data-hw-vs-sw-timestamps used to pair logged frames with actions estimates a device's clock offset by assuming that both directions of the path take equal time [@ieee15882019]. @Sec-appendix-systems-ptp gives the message exchange and its servo. A path asymmetry $\\PathDelayAsymmetry$ shifts the estimated offset by $\\PathDelayAsymmetry/2$ and leaves no symptom in the exchange. The exchange must therefore be timestamped in hardware at the network interface, where host scheduling cannot add delay to one direction and not the other. Between synchronization exchanges, the local oscillator drifts. The OS1-64's free-running clock is specified at <code>[ErrorQualification.drift_ppm_str]</code> ppm [@ouster2025os1], which over a <code>[ErrorQualification.t_sync_str]</code> s sync interval accumulates <code>[ErrorQualification.drift_str]</code> of worst-case drift. Clock qualification records the measured tail of the servo's reported offset, the path-asymmetry allowance, and the drift between corrections. A sampled tail is statistical evidence, not a maximum-error guarantee. A contract that treats synchronization error as a hard bound also needs a justified limit on unobserved offset excursions and a detector that invalidates the mapping when its assumptions fail. For a hardware-triggered camera, this bound is verified by timing the trigger edge against the IMU latch with a logic analyzer. Until those measurements exist, the clock-conversion bound in the navigation camera's record remains illustrative, and the LiDAR's datasheet guarantees only <code>[LidarAge.eps_ptp_str]</code>. While empirical residual campaigns can qualify a stereo or LiDAR sensor whose physics directly measures depth, modern perception pipelines increasingly pair those metric sensors with neural networks that infer depth from a single image.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h3\">Learned depth as a measurement</h3>\n<p>A monocular depth network can predict plausible-looking meter values that err by meters on an unfamiliar floor texture or a life-sized photograph printed on a flat box, because a single camera physically measures only bearing and guesses scale from learned appearance priors. Learned models add two capabilities to metric perception: a semantic classification of spatial regions, and a depth estimate from a single camera. Lifting labeled detections into a persistent, object-centric scene graph carries state across frames, and @sec-memory-hierarchical-scene-graphs develops the maintenance of that graph. Perception must settle a narrower systems question: whether a learned depth estimate may enter a safety clearance claim at all. The semantic layer identifies candidate objects and contacts; the metric layer must establish their physical geometry. Point clouds supply measured surface samples where pulses reflect, but missing returns never prove free space. Persistent representations that retain geometric evidence cannot alter an observation's capture timestamp or convert a learned model score into an error bound.</p>\n<p>↪ The volumetric maps that retain this geometric evidence across frames are developed in @sec-memory-volumetric-metric-maps.</p>\n<p>Lifting 2D pixel features into 3D metric space requires combining 2D token embeddings with per-pixel depth distributions. In deep monocular depth networks (such as Depth Anything, MiDaS, or metric depth transformers), models predict dense depth maps from monocular visual cues. However, single-view depth estimation is inherently ill-posed due to projective scale ambiguity: an infinite family of physical 3D objects with different scales and distances project to identical pixel arrays. Relative-depth models output scale- or affine-ambiguous predictions, whose conversion to metric depth requires external calibration, represented by $\\MetricDepth = \\DepthCalibrationScale \\cdot \\PredictedRelativeDepth + \\DepthCalibrationShift$ when the model uses that parameterization. Metric-trained heads directly predict depth in meters, but their units do not certify their error. Both model classes require residual validation and can fail silently on unusual geometries, novel textures, or optical illusions (such as a 2D photograph printed on a flat surface).</p>\n<p>Rather than committing to a single depth value per pixel, bird's-eye-view (BEV) architectures such as Lift-Splat-Shoot [@philion2020lift] predict a categorical probability distribution over discrete depth bins along each viewing ray. Each 2D feature token is cast outward along its optical sightline ray and 'splatted' into discrete 3D spatial voxel cells weighted by these predicted depth probabilities, transforming disembodied image embeddings into an explicit 3D volumetric representation. The resulting spatial uncertainty ellipsoid deforms non-linearly under perspective unprojection through the unprojection Jacobian $\\UnprojectionJacobian = \\frac{\\partial \\CameraFramePoint}{\\partial [\\PixelColumnCoordinate, \\PixelRowCoordinate, \\CameraFrameDepth]^\\top}$ (@eq-percep-unproj-covariance): $$\\CameraFramePointCovariance = \\UnprojectionJacobian \\MeasurementNoiseCovariance \\UnprojectionJacobian^\\top$$ {#eq-percep-unproj-covariance} While transverse spatial standard deviation expands linearly with range ($\\TransverseStandardDeviation \\approx \\frac{\\CameraFrameDepth}{\\FocalLength} \\PixelStandardDeviation$), stereo radial depth standard deviation expands quadratically ($\\DepthStandardDeviation \\approx \\frac{\\CameraFrameDepth^2}{\\FocalLength \\StereoBaseline} \\DisparityStandardDeviation$). This perspective unprojection transforms near-field circular uncertainty disks into highly eccentric, needle-shaped ellipsoids oriented along the camera's sightline, with extreme radial dispersion at long ranges that must be bounded before downstream safety filters can certify clearance.</p>\n<p>↪ The lift-splat projection of camera features into a metric bird's-eye-view grid is developed in @sec-appendix-ml-bev-lifting.</p>\n<p>Because the depth-bin distributions produced by learned lifting stages are uncalibrated model scores rather than certified metric error bounds, the clearance inset of @eq-percep-clearance-inset cannot rest on learned depth alone. Downstream safety enforcers require continuous validation against calibrated active ranging sensors or stereo disparity. This is the division of labor on the running machine. A learned head on the navigation camera may classify the worker and even estimate depth, but the rack-end range that enters the clearance check comes from the LiDAR. The split also keeps the check independent of the proposal it judges, because a checker that consumed the camera's own perception would share every blind spot of the proposer it exists to catch.</p>\n<p>↪ The independence a monitor needs from the proposer across sensing, compute, and actuation is developed in @sec-enforcement-where-enforcer-sits.</p>\n<p><strong>Safety is an invariant of metric clearance, not semantic understanding.</strong> Suppose a slow 2 Hz vision-language foundation model classifies a pathway as \"clear aisle\" or dismisses an object as a harmless shadow. If the <code>[LidarSweep.f_scan_str]</code> LiDAR, or the occupancy grid built from it, detects a physical obstacle return within the stopping envelope, the permission path must honor the qualified occupied-region evidence rather than let semantic confidence erase it. The permission path halts or slows the machine regardless of the semantic model's high confidence. Semantic models propose high-level task goals; qualified metric sensing supplies clearance evidence to the permission path. Neither sensor pipeline itself owns actuator authority.</p>\n<p>A spatial claim is spent from the same clearance as its age, and its error must be measured before it is charged.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Quadratic depth error</strong>: Can you explain why stereo depth error grows with the square of range while a pulsed LiDAR's range precision stays roughly flat?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Baseline estimate</strong>: If the illustrative rig's <code>[StereoDepthError.b_str]</code> baseline were halved, at what range would one standard deviation of depth error reach the <code>[StereoDepthError.side_clearance_str]</code> side clearance, compared with <code>[StereoDepthError.z_star_side_str]</code> now?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Covariance vs. clearance inset</strong>: Can you explain why a residual quantile measured against ground truth, not a reported covariance, sets $\\ClearanceInset$, and what that quantile still leaves uncovered?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Learned depth vs. metric evidence</strong>: Can you explain why a depth network's predicted spread cannot enter a clearance claim until its residuals are validated, and why qualified metric evidence overrides a semantic label that calls a return a shadow?</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h2\">Perception Failure Modes and Sensor Degradation</h2>\n<p>Every bound derived so far, whether metric, learned, or temporal, assumes that the sensor behaves as calibrated: propagation bounds the error of a sensor working as modeled, not one that has stopped working that way while still delivering frames at its nominal rate. A navigation camera can fail in two ways that look entirely different to downstream software. If its serializer link loses lock, the driver raises an error and the frame never arrives. If direct sunlight through an open dock door saturates the lens as the vehicle turns into an aisle, the camera delivers a complete frame at its nominal rate, with a shelving rack hidden under a patch of clipped white pixels. Perception failures divide along this boundary into two operational classes. <strong>Detectable faults</strong> occur when the physical sensor or communication transport asserts an explicit hardware or software error condition. Examples include a serializer-deserializer (SerDes) link losing clock synchronization, a cyclic redundancy check (CRC) detecting a corrupted payload, a DMA frame buffer overflowing, or a transport bus timing out. These failures are straightforward to handle. The hardware driver raises a fault flag at the ingestion boundary, downstream consumers drop the corrupted frame, and state estimators expand their uncertainty. Once the age of valid sensor evidence exceeds its configured freshness budget, the permission path rejects motion requests that depend on it and invokes a fallback qualified for the remaining state evidence, actuation authority, and stopping margin. In contrast, <strong>silent failures</strong> occur when the sensor hardware, firmware, and transport bus function without error, delivering a structurally valid tensor whose numerical values misrepresent the physical world.</p>\n<p>Physical degradation of the optical channel is a frequent source of silent failure. When bright sunlight enters the aperture, incident photons overwhelm the full-well capacity of the CMOS photodiodes, clamping pixel values at their maximum quantization limit across large patches of the sensor array. This <strong>dynamic range saturation</strong> destroys local spatial gradients. Downstream feature extractors, optical flow estimators, and stereo disparity algorithms fail because numerical optimization over a uniform flat field yields zero directional gradient, even while the camera driver delivers frames at the full nominal rate. Dimly lit environments trigger the opposite failure mode. When ambient photon flux drops, the discrete, stochastic arrival of photons (<strong>photon shot noise</strong>) and the thermal and electrical fluctuations of the readout circuitry (<strong>sensor read noise</strong>) dominate the signal. As the signal-to-noise ratio collapses, learned neural backbones begin extracting spurious geometric features from random noise patterns rather than physical obstacle boundaries. Contaminants on the protective lens or dome window, such as water droplets, dust, or grease smudges, act as irregular refractive elements and optical low-pass filters. They attenuate contrast, distort spatial geometry, and mask critical obstacles while the transport stack reports flawless operation.</p>\n<p>Timing and transport anomalies can also corrupt an observation silently. If an unshielded physical interface experiences electromagnetic interference, deserializer bit slips (where the hardware drops or duplicates a bit, misaligning the subsequent serial data stream) can alter payload semantics without invalidating standard packet lengths. More dangerous than a dropped packet is an observation stamped with an incorrect capture epoch, because no transport monitor detects the error. If an image sensor captures photons at epoch $\\TimeInstant_0$, but a late interrupt handler or an unsynchronized system clock timestamps the resulting memory buffer at $\\TimeInstant_0 + \\SmallPerturbation \\TimeInstant$, the downstream state estimator integrates spatial features under an incorrect body pose. Consider a machine traveling with instantaneous velocity $\\Velocity$ and linear acceleration $\\LinearAcceleration$ along a given trajectory. When perception suffers an undetected timing desynchronization $\\SmallPerturbation \\TimeInstant$, the state estimator assigns the detected position of an obstacle to the body coordinates at $\\TimeInstant_0 + \\SmallPerturbation \\TimeInstant$ rather than $\\TimeInstant_0$. Over the unmodeled time interval $\\SmallPerturbation \\TimeInstant$, the physical state estimation error $\\FiniteChange \\CartesianPosition$ between the asserted position and the true position is governed by the kinematic drift relation (@eq-percep-kinematic-drift): $$\\FiniteChange \\CartesianPosition = \\Velocity \\cdot \\SmallPerturbation \\TimeInstant + \\frac{1}{2} \\LinearAcceleration \\cdot (\\SmallPerturbation \\TimeInstant)^2$$ {#eq-percep-kinematic-drift}</p>\n<p>The arrival stamp discussed in @sec-perception-handoff represents a silent desynchronization of this exact type. At constant velocity, the acceleration term vanishes, leaving the kinematic offset $\\Velocity\\,\\ArrivalStampOffset$ uncorrected while the driver reports nominal health. The asymmetry between a lost frame and a misdated one needs no estimator mechanics to see. An estimator that accepts a misdated frame as a correctly timed measurement can shrink its reported uncertainty around the wrong position, while a missing update can leave uncertainty to grow under its motion model. In this case, a dropped frame adds no information, so the reported spread grows and the evidence is honestly one frame period (<code>[SensorInformationAge.t_frame_str]</code>) older. A misdated frame is accepted as information, so the spread shrinks around a position offset by $\\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\SmallPerturbation \\TimeInstant$. On the running machine the arrival stamp alone places the obstacle <code>[ArrivalStampOffset.dx_recv_str]</code> from where it stood, and the estimator reports that wrong position with more confidence than before. The motion planner is then sure that it has already cleared an obstacle it is about to strike. @Sec-memory develops the recursive estimator in which this happens, the Kalman filter.</p>\n<p>Defending against silent failures requires dedicated runtime health monitors placed directly at the sensor ingestion boundary. A pixel-intensity histogram monitor inspects raw digitized frames immediately after DMA transfer, flagging overexposure when pixel counts concentrate at maximum bit depth, or flagging lens occlusion when spatial variance collapses below an empirical noise floor. A hardware watchdog timer tracks inter-arrival times across the DMA channel, flagging transport jitter when inter-frame intervals exceed $\\NominalFrameInterval + 3\\TimingJitter$. This empirical diagnostic heuristic does not replace the physically derived freshness deadline. Higher in the ingestion stack, an optical flow plausibility monitor compares calculated pixel displacement vectors against the body's physical velocity envelope, rejecting observations that imply accelerations beyond the chassis's mechanical limits. In multimodal configurations, cross-sensor consistency checks compare high-rate (<code>[RollingShutterSkew.f_imu_str]</code>) inertial measurement unit (IMU) integrations against visual odometry. If visual odometry reports that the robot is stationary while the gravity-compensated IMU integration indicates sustained $2.0\\text{ m/s}^2$ linear acceleration, the monitor flags a cross-modal discrepancy once attitude, sensor bias, latency, and measurement uncertainties are reconciled.</p>\n<p>Because every runtime health monitor observes the physical world through the available transducer signals, no single sensing channel can validate its own semantic interpretations. A camera assembly with pristine optics, optimal exposure, jitter-free transport, and sub-microsecond hardware timestamping can still present an image that causes a deep neural network to hallucinate a navigable path or fail to detect an obstacle. Because deep vision backbones map high-dimensional pixel arrays through non-linear manifolds, subtle visual anomalies (a sharp shadow across an aisle, a life-sized person printed on a cardboard display, or specular glare off shrink-wrapped freight) produce valid pixel statistics, smooth optical flow fields, and misleadingly high prediction confidence. An ingestion monitor confirms that the raw data acquisition pipeline operated within nominal engineering limits, but it cannot confirm that downstream semantic inferences correspond to physical reality. Corroborating optical detections requires independent physical sensing modalities, such as lidar or millimeter-wave radar, whose coverage, beam geometry, and failure dependencies have been rigorously characterized. Even then, a validated permission path guarantees spatial clearance only within explicit operational and timing assumptions; no perception stack can guarantee unrestricted semantic truth. Understanding how the physical environment corrupts each sensing channel explains why single-modality checks fall short.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ FOG CONTRAST RANGE AND WRIST-CAMERA MOTION TRAVEL\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: The atmospheric-scattering and motion-blur items of\n# │          the environmental-degradation subsection.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Range at which dense fog drops contrast to the 0.05 threshold,\n# │          and camera-center travel during one wrist-camera exposure.\n# │ Show:    ln(1/0.05) / 0.05 m^-1 = 60 m; 0.5 m x 3 rad/s x 5 ms = 7.5 mm.\n# │ How:     Koschmieder C = C0 exp(-beta d), Scenarios.DenseFogVisibility;\n# │          dx = r omega t_exp with the registry exposure of the machine&#x27;s\n# │          wrist camera (Sony IMX296).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nimport math\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_length, fmt_latency, check\n\nRM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\n\nclass DegradedOptics:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Fog contrast range and wrist-camera travel within one exposure.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    beta_fog = Scenarios.DenseFogVisibility.extinction  # category: illustrative (dense-fog extinction coefficient)\n    eps_contrast = (\n        Scenarios.DenseFogVisibility.contrast_threshold\n    )  # category: standard (5 percent contrast threshold of meteorological optical range)\n    t_exp = RM.wrist_camera.exposure_time.to(millisecond)  # category: chosen (registry: IMX296 configured exposure)\n    r_arm = 0.5 * meter  # category: illustrative (lever arm from rotation axis to camera)\n    omega = 3.0 * radian / second  # category: illustrative (sweep angular rate)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    d_fog = Scenarios.DenseFogVisibility.result.visibility_range.to(meter)\n    dx_blur = (r_arm * omega.to(1 / second).magnitude / second * t_exp).to(millimeter)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(d_fog.magnitude - 59.9) &lt; 0.1, f&quot;Unexpected fog range: {d_fog}&quot;)\n    check(RM.wrist_camera.shutter_type == &quot;global&quot;, &quot;The wrist camera is the global-shutter IMX296.&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_exp.magnitude - 5.0) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Unexpected wrist exposure: {t_exp}&quot;)\n    check(abs(dx_blur.magnitude - 7.5) &lt; 1e-6, f&quot;Unexpected blur travel: {dx_blur}&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    beta_fog_str = fmt(beta_fog.to(1 / meter).magnitude, precision=2)\n    eps_contrast_str = fmt(eps_contrast, precision=2)\n    d_fog_str = fmt_length(round(d_fog.magnitude) * meter, unit=meter, precision=0)  # 59.9 m shown to the meter\n    t_exp_str = fmt_latency(t_exp, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    r_arm_str = fmt_length(r_arm, unit=meter, precision=1)\n    omega_str = fmt(omega.to(radian / second).magnitude, precision=0)\n    dx_blur_str = fmt_length(dx_blur, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n]```\n\n### Fog, rain, glare, and specular surfaces\n\nIn an indoor warehouse, daylight flooding through an open loading-dock bay door or standing water on polished concrete can saturate cameras and deflect LiDAR beams into empty space, while fog and rain add atmospheric scattering for outdoor platforms that carry the same sensors. Each of these conditions degrades sensory transmission across optical, near-infrared, or radio channels without raising a hardware error flag. Optical cameras undergo severe degradation in the presence of atmospheric scattering and glare. Suspended airborne aerosol particles in fog, mist, and smoke ($1\\text{ to }10\\,\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{m}$ diameter) scatter visible wavelengths ($\\RadiationWavelength \\approx 400\\text{ to }700\\text{ nm}$) under Mie scattering physics. Scene radiance attenuates exponentially across distance according to Koschmieder&#x27;s law:\n$$\\ApparentContrast(\\SightlineRange) = \\ApparentContrast_0 \\, e^{-\\AtmosphericExtinctionCoefficient \\SightlineRange}$$\nwhere $\\ApparentContrast_0$ is intrinsic object contrast, $\\SightlineRange$ is metric range, and $\\AtmosphericExtinctionCoefficient$ is the atmospheric extinction coefficient. In dense fog ($\\AtmosphericExtinctionCoefficient \\approx$ `[DegradedOptics.beta_fog_str]` $\\text{m}^{-1}$, an illustrative value), apparent contrast decays below the `[DegradedOptics.eps_contrast_str]` perceptual detection threshold within `[DegradedOptics.d_fog_str]`. High-frequency edge gradients dissolve into uniform background luminance, preventing convolutional and transformer backbones from forming valid feature embeddings. Airborne rain introduces high-velocity streak noise across frames, while droplets impacting the protective lens act as irregular refractive microlenses. Each droplet deflects incoming rays, creating severe localized geometric distortion, optical blur, and false object silhouettes.\n\nRapid angular motion compounds this degradation through motion blur and modulation transfer function (MTF) collapse. During a yaw rotation or manipulator sweep, a camera center at lever arm $\\SensorLeverArmLength$ travels a tangential distance set by angular rate $\\AngularVelocity$ and exposure duration:\n$$\\FiniteChange \\CameraCenterArcPosition = \\SensorLeverArmLength \\cdot \\|\\AngularVelocity\\| \\cdot \\ExposureDuration$$\nFor the machine&#x27;s global-shutter wrist camera at its configured `[DegradedOptics.t_exp_str]` exposure, swept at an illustrative $\\AngularSpeed =$ `[DegradedOptics.omega_str]` rad/s on an illustrative lever arm $\\SensorLeverArmLength =$ `[DegradedOptics.r_arm_str]`, this travel scale is $\\FiniteChange \\CameraCenterArcPosition \\approx$ `[DegradedOptics.dx_blur_str]`. This metric displacement describes the physical travel of the optical center rather than image-plane blur in pixels. The resulting pixel displacement depends on both scene depth and focal length, while pure camera rotation shifts features across the image plane independently of depth. As motion blur smears sharp transitions across neighboring pixels, spatial gradients attenuate and feature trackers, visual odometry algorithms, and neural backbones lose tracking lock. Systems engineers must evaluate these effects across the full envelope of intended operational velocities and camera exposures.\n\n: **Modulation Transfer Function (MTF)**: The fraction of scene contrast an imaging chain passes at each spatial frequency. A uniform blur of length $\\MotionBlurLength$ in the image multiplies it by a sinc that falls to zero at a period of $\\MotionBlurLength$, so detail finer than the blur is erased rather than dimmed, and no network downstream can restore what the exposure removed. \n\nIntra-frame illumination contrast introduces another challenging failure mode through dynamic range saturation and blooming. Standard auto-exposure algorithms adjust exposure time $\\ExposureDuration$ across consecutive frames to track gradual lighting changes, such as moving from a shaded warehouse aisle into an outdoor yard. However, auto-exposure cannot resolve extreme illumination differences within a single image. When a robot operating in a dim indoor aisle turns toward an open loading dock flooded with daylight, no single exposure setting suffices. An exposure long enough to resolve dark shelving units allows the intense photon flux from the dock to saturate the photodiodes, clipping those image regions to solid white. High dynamic range (HDR) sensors expand operational range by interleaving multiple exposures or employing dual-gain photodiode architectures. Yet staggered multi-exposure HDR introduces timing ambiguities that the observation contract must document. Because each synthesized exposure corresponds to a distinct temporal integration window, the metadata must specify which exposure midpoint dates the frame. When extreme overexposure occurs, excess photo-generated electrons spill over potential barrier implants into neighboring pixels (**charge blooming**), washing out entire sensor columns into saturated values ($255$). Concurrently, internal reflections between lens elements generate veiling glare and lens flare streaks, eliminating the local spatial contrast essential for disparity matching and optical flow.\n\nActive optical ranging encounters parallel limitations under degraded atmospheric conditions. Pulsed time-of-flight LiDAR, at silicon-band near-infrared wavelengths and at $1{,}550\\text{ nm}$ alike, suffers three distinct physical failure mechanisms in industrial and outdoor operation:\n\n1. *Aerosol backscatter clutter*: Emitted laser pulses strike suspended fog droplets and dust particulates, scattering optical power back into the receiver during the initial nanoseconds of time-of-flight. This creates dense near-field point clutter, often appearing as a false obstacle wall within $0.5\\text{ to }3.0\\text{ m}$ of the sensor aperture. Downstream spatial clustering algorithms misinterpret these backscatter returns as imminent collision hazards, triggering spurious emergency stops. Simultaneously, atmospheric extinction depletes forward pulse energy, significantly shortening maximum detection range. The magnitude of this range reduction depends on target reflectivity and the atmospheric extinction coefficient, requiring empirical calibration under the operational conditions for which the sensor is qualified.\n2. *Retroreflector detector blooming and range walk*: High-visibility safety vests, barcode placards, and vehicle license plates incorporate retroreflective prismatic surfaces designed to return incident optical power directly along the emission axis at intensities orders of magnitude higher than diffuse surfaces. This strong return pulse triggers two distinct measurement errors. **Range walk** is a temporal distortion in the receiver&#x27;s avalanche photodiodes (APDs) or single-photon avalanche diodes (SPADs). A simple leading-edge discriminator triggers when an analog pulse crosses a fixed voltage threshold, so a fast-rising, high-amplitude pulse trips the comparator earlier than a weak return and systematically underestimates the distance to the retroreflector. While intensity-based compensation curves can correct this offset under linear amplification, they fail once the analog front-end saturates. Concurrently, **blooming** creates a spatial distortion: optical scattering and electrical crosstalk within the detector array register false returns across adjacent detector channels, surrounding the retroreflector with a spherical halo of phantom points. Sensor manufacturers reflect these physical realities in their specifications; for example, the Ouster OS1 datasheet establishes distinct, wider accuracy tolerances for retroreflective targets compared to diffuse Lambertian surfaces [@ouster2025os1].\n3. *Specular surfaces and non-Lambertian forward bounce*: Triangulation systems, structured-light projectors, and time-of-flight LiDAR all assume that surfaces produce diffuse Lambertian scattering, reflecting incident light uniformly across the hemisphere. Specular surfaces such as polished concrete floors, shrink-wrapped freight, painted metal panels, and architectural glass violate this assumption. Emitted laser beams undergo specular mirror reflection, bouncing away from the surface normal at an angle equal to the incidence angle ($\\ReflectionAngle = \\IncidenceAngle$) and directing optical energy away from the receiver. On wet or polished floors, this specular bounce results in complete signal loss, creating artificial zero-return dropouts (&quot;puddle black holes&quot;). Against glass walls or metal structures, multi-bounce reflections cause emitted pulses to travel indirect paths back to the receiver, generating phantom point clusters that appear meters behind physical walls or deep beneath the concrete floor.\n\nMillimeter-wave radar provides complementary all-weather capability that overcomes many optical failure modes. Operating at $76\\text{ to }81\\text{ GHz}$, frequency-modulated continuous-wave (FMCW) radar uses a carrier wavelength of $\\RadiationWavelength \\approx 3.9\\text{ mm}$, roughly 4,000 times longer than near-infrared laser light and several orders of magnitude larger than fog droplets ($1\\text{ to }10\\,\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{m}$). Scattering by such small airborne particles falls within the Rayleigh scattering regime, where the particle scattering cross-section $\\ScatteringCrossSection$ scales inversely with the fourth power of wavelength:\n$$\\ScatteringCrossSection \\propto \\frac{\\DropletDiameter^6}{\\RadiationWavelength^4}$$\nAlthough this extreme wavelength dependence shields radar from the severe attenuation and backscatter that paralyze optical sensors in fog and dust, radar is not immune to environmental interference. Ground reflections produce multipath ghost detections, standing water films on the radome attenuate radio-frequency signals, and low spatial resolution complicates boundary discrimination. A multimodal perception suite must therefore explicitly qualify radar coverage boundaries when primary optical streams degrade. Even when the physical channel is clear and the sensor hardware operates within specification, however, a neural backbone can still encounter scenes that lie outside its training distribution.\n\n### Out-of-distribution inputs at the sensor boundary\n\nEven when a sensor is calibrated, unoccluded, and operating without transport faults, an unfamiliar scene (such as an overturned forklift, an unusual trailer profile, or a chemical vapor plume) can fall outside the operational distribution on which downstream models were qualified. Uncertainty in these observations stems from two distinct sources. **Aleatoric uncertainty** represents the irreducible physical noise of the transduction process itself, such as photon shot noise, thermal amplifier noise, or aerosol backscatter. The measurement&#x27;s covariance model must quantify this uncertainty. In contrast, **epistemic uncertainty** stems from the model&#x27;s lack of knowledge about scenarios omitted from its training distribution. Upgrading sensor fidelity cannot eliminate epistemic uncertainty. The limits of normalized softmax scores, calibrated uncertainty estimation, and the conversion of calibrated prediction sets into action refusals lie beyond perception&#x27;s scope. Perception carries a narrower engineering responsibility. It must assess at the ingestion boundary whether an incoming observation appears outside the validated input support, and log that assessment directly in the observation contract.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The limits of softmax confidence and the conformal calibration that bounds it are established in @sec-brain-softmax-illusion.\n:::\n\nInput-support monitors evaluate incoming sensor frames, or early feature embeddings from the backbone, against the support of the qualification dataset. Common implementations include computing a free-energy score over unnormalized logits [@liu2020energy], measuring the Mahalanobis distance to class-conditional feature centroids [@lee2018simple], or tracking the reconstruction error of an autoencoder trained strictly on nominal operational data. Each method requires an empirical acceptance threshold calibrated against expected anomalies. An in-bounds score means only that the monitor did not flag the input under its calibrated threshold; it guarantees neither membership in the qualification distribution nor physically accurate semantic inference.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The input-support monitor these checks instantiate is established in @sec-evaluation-limits.\n:::\n\nWhen an input-support monitor detects an anomaly, the perception pipeline sets a `BIT_OOD_DETECTED` flag in the health bitmask that every observation carries, one of the record fields that @sec-perception-observation-contract assembles. The permission path monitors this bitmask to disallow high-speed motions, expand conservative clearance buffers, or trigger a deterministic supervisory fallback. In multimodal systems, the same bitmask arbitrates between sensors. On the running machine, if the navigation camera raises `BIT_OPTICAL_SATURATED` or `BIT_OOD_DETECTED` while the LiDAR&#x27;s returns stay healthy, the pipeline demotes the camera&#x27;s tokens and the rack-end claim rests on LiDAR range alone. This holds only if the LiDAR is independently validated to enforce spatial clearance under those operating conditions. Outdoor platforms apply the same arbitration with radar in the LiDAR&#x27;s place.\n\n::: {#ws-perception-tesla-williston-omission .callout-war-story title=&quot;Silent perception omission (2016)&quot;}\n**Context**: A 2015 Tesla Model S traveling at about $74\\text{ mph}$ with Traffic-Aware Cruise Control and Autosteer active struck a refrigerated semitrailer turning left across its path near Williston, Florida [@ntsb2017tesla].\n\n**Mechanism**: The crossing-path collision lay outside the front-to-rear crash modes for which the 2015 warning and automatic braking systems were designed. NHTSA reported that automatic braking required agreement between camera and radar; unusual vehicle shapes could impede camera classification ([NHTSA ODI PE16-007](https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/inv/2016/INCLA-PE16007-7876.PDF)).\n\n**Impact**: The NTSB found no recorded indication that forward collision warning or automatic emergency braking detected an object, and no pre-impact braking occurred. The vehicle passed beneath the turning semitrailer, shearing off the car&#x27;s roof; the paired investigation photographs in @fig-08-real-ntsb-williston-crash document this physical outcome, highlighting collision scrape damage along the right underside of the trailer alongside the roofless passenger cabin.\n\n**Response**: The NTSB examined operational design domain limits and driver engagement as well as the collision-avoidance systems.\n\n**Systems lesson**: An absent detection is not evidence that a crossing path is clear. A permission design must account for detection coverage and unsupported scenarios; a health flag cannot certify that every relevant object was perceived.\n:::\n\n::: {#fig-08-real-ntsb-williston-crash fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Williston collision damage**: This NTSB presentation slide shows damage on the right side of the semitrailer and the roofless Tesla after the 2016 crash. The crossing-path geometry and absence of pre-impact braking come from the [NTSB accident report](https://www.ntsb.gov/investigations/AccidentReports/Reports/HAR1702.pdf), not from the photographs alone. (Source: NTSB board-meeting presentation for report HAR-17/02; photographs courtesy of Florida Highway Patrol).&quot; fig-alt=&quot;NTSB slide with two photographs: left, a refrigerated semitrailer with damage low on its right side; right, a heavily damaged roofless Tesla. Yellow ovals mark damage areas. The photos do not show the vehicles&#x27; motion or collision angle.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\nAn observation contract can expose only the physical, temporal, and distributional faults that the ingestion layer is designed to measure. Detectable interface errors, transport jitter, and out-of-distribution feature shifts can be encoded into explicit health bitmasks. Silent semantic omissions, such as the missed detection at Williston, evade single-sensor health checks. Preventing catastrophic failures requires an architectural safety design: incorporating independent physical sensing modalities, enforcing conservative spatial permission envelopes, and never mistaking the absence of an error flag for confirmation of physical safety. Omission can also depend on who is in view: a detector qualified mostly on people standing upright can miss a kneeling worker or a child more often, so any detection rate a contract reports for people holds only for the groups it was measured on.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ How a detection gap between groups of people becomes a gap in stopping distance and impact energy, and how large each group&#x27;s sample must be, is developed in @sec-responsibility-perception-disparity.\n:::\n\n## The Observation Contract {#sec-perception-observation-contract}\n\nA downstream tracker, policy backbone, or permission path cannot reconstruct exposure timing, mounting calibration, or byte integrity from a raw tensor alone. The perception subsystem therefore wraps each sensory output in an **observation contract**, the sixth record in the book&#x27;s sixteen-record chain. In closed-loop physical systems, an observation is unusable without an explicit capture epoch, an identified clock domain, a bounded conversion to the permission path&#x27;s clock, and an extrinsic calibration operating within its qualified validity envelope. A safe consumer must reject any incoming record lacking these guarantees before inspecting a single payload byte. The system&#x27;s shared clock domain and $\\SignalHandoff_1$ age budget originate in the nervous-system handoff record of @sec-nervous-handoffs-matrix; perception, in turn, provides the capture-side timestamps and uncertainty bounds that downstream consumers need to verify compliance.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The sixteen-record chain, in which the observation contract is Record 6, is developed in @sec-appendix-systems-record-register.\n:::\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ SENSOR-SIDE AGE AGAINST THE H_1 HANDOFF ALLOTMENT</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: The handoff paragraph of @sec-perception-observation-contract.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Reconcile the handoff matrix's sensor-age allotment (one frame</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          period) with this chapter's exposure-midpoint-plus-readout age.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    16.7 ms frame period; 24.6 ms from the top row's exposure midpoint</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          to the end of readout; 16.3 ms from the middle row's; the top-row</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          convention exceeds the allotment by 7.9 ms.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Row-convention ages of Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraAge (RM.nav_camera</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          frame rate, exposure, and readout). The allotment is taken as one</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          frame period, matching the handoff matrix value.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import <em> from mlsysim.core.units import </em> from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_latency, fmt_frequency, check</p>\n<p>RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator</p>\n<p>class HandoffSensorAge: \"\"\"Sensor-side age of the navigation camera under two row conventions.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>camera = RM.nav_camera  # category: datasheet (registry: navigation camera) t_exp = camera.exposure_time.to(millisecond)  # category: chosen (configured exposure) t_readout = camera.readout_time.to(millisecond)  # category: datasheet f_frame = camera.frame_rate  # category: datasheet</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>_age = Scenarios.WarehouseNavCameraAge.result t_frame = _age.frame_period.to(millisecond) age_top = _age.capture_before_compute.to(millisecond) age_mid = _age.sensor_age_middle_row.to(millisecond) excess_top = _age.top_row_excess.to(millisecond)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(t_frame.magnitude - 16.7) &lt; 0.05, f\"Unexpected frame period: {t_frame}\") check(abs(age_top.magnitude - 24.6) &lt; 0.05, f\"Unexpected top-row sensor age: {age_top}\") check(abs(age_mid.magnitude - 16.3) &lt; 0.05, f\"Unexpected middle-row sensor age: {age_mid}\") check(age_mid &lt; t_frame &lt; age_top, \"Prose says only the middle-row convention fits the allotment\") check(abs(excess_top.magnitude - 7.9) &lt; 0.05, f\"Unexpected excess: {excess_top}\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>t_frame_str = fmt_latency(t_frame, unit=millisecond, precision=1) f_frame_str = fmt_frequency(f_frame, unit=hertz, precision=0) age_top_str = fmt_latency(age_top, unit=millisecond, precision=1) age_mid_str = fmt_latency(age_mid, unit=millisecond, precision=1) excess_top_str = fmt_latency(excess_top, unit=millisecond, precision=1) ]```</p>\n<p>Formally, the contract couples either a raw observation tensor or an extracted geometric bounding envelope with the validation metadata that downstream consumers require (@eq-percep-observation-contract-tuple): $$\\mathcal{O} = \\langle \\CaptureTimestamp, \\text{clock\\_id}, \\ClockConversionBound, \\text{frame\\<em>id}, \\ObservationPayload, \\BodyFramePointCovariance, \\ClearanceInset, \\text{mask}</em>{\\text{health}}, \\text{crc32} \\rangle$$ {#eq-percep-observation-contract-tuple} In this tuple, $\\CaptureTimestamp$ records the exposure-midpoint timestamp registered on monotonic clock $\\text{clock\\_id}$, $\\ClockConversionBound$ bounds the maximum synchronization error when converting to the clock of the safety MCU that hosts the permission path, and $\\text{frame\\<em>id}$ identifies the calibrated sensor transform. The payload $\\ObservationPayload$ contains the raw sensor array or derived geometric bounding envelope, $\\BodyFramePointCovariance$ characterizes its spatial covariance, and $\\ClearanceInset$ provides the qualified clearance inset. The health mask $\\text{mask}</em>{\\text{health}}$ conveys hardware and perceptual health status bits, and the checksum $\\text{crc32}$ seals both header and payload against transport corruption. Spatial memory consumes this record to update its world model, while trajectory planning uses $\\ClearanceInset$ to inflate collision boundaries. Both modules evaluate evidence age, verify kinematic coordinate frames, and bound spatial uncertainty before allowing an observation to influence a control decision.</p>\n<p>The tuple is the same object as the error-qualified claim $(\\BodyFramePoint, \\BodyFramePointCovariance, \\ClearanceInset, \\DispatchObservationAge)$ that @sec-perception-error-clearance assembled, now wrapped for transport. The claim's point travels in $\\ObservationPayload$, its covariance in $\\BodyFramePointCovariance$, and its inset in $\\ClearanceInset$. Its age is deliberately not stored, because each consumer computes the current evidence age from $\\CaptureTimestamp$ and $\\ClockConversionBound$ on its own clock.</p>\n<p>This contract does not replace binary serialization formats such as Protocol Buffers, FlatBuffers, or ROS 2 IDL; any of these serialization systems can transmit the underlying struct over shared memory or bus fabrics. While transport schemas standardize memory layout and data types, the observation contract establishes verifiable physical invariants: monotonic temporal progression, bounded clock skew, active calibration validity windows, and rigorous spatial uncertainty limits. @Tbl-08-observation-record-schema serializes the tuple as named fields, details each one alongside its validation rule, and populates the schema for the mobile manipulator's navigation camera. It splits $\\CaptureTimestamp$ and $\\text{clock\\_id}$ into <code>capture_timestamp_ns</code> and <code>capture_clock_id</code>, carries the calibration behind $\\text{frame\\_id}$ in <code>calibration_metadata</code>, and adds a <code>sequence_index</code> and a <code>provenance_digest</code> that the tuple leaves out.</p>", "quizzes": []};
